        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>"Carrrar.. pnnuthment," the boy said when the Diboolmistress seated him with the girls. Li-rrr..K.Vioron "Mamma, my hands are dirty; ;dudl I wash them, or put my gloves on?' ItAIN•151 %TUNG may be all right; but what most, nun need is some asheme that will raise the wind. "!Ps llt-no's heart is like a volume in a circu- Istitig lib.cary." "How is that?" Not to be kept loner than two weeks."</p>
        <p>"I wouldn't marry a man for his money." Blanche : "Nor L Still, I'd hate to dbsappoint one who was willing." " WHAT kind of a memory have you?" "Bad— 'Very bad." "In what particular "It remem- bers things that were better forgotten." SIDE (dre,mily) : "Just think of it—we are to be married a mouth from to-day." He "Well, let's be happy while we can.' "I WOULDN'T be a fool if I were you," said Jones to a friend." "If you were me you wouldn't be a fool," was the reply. JONES : "Don't trouble to see me to the door, Mra. Smith." Mrs. Smith: "No trouble; quite a pleasure, I assure you." THERN is a whistling Buoy anchored off the Isle of Wight. Oh that all the whistling boys could be there.</p>
        <p>Excirmiorr.—Amy : "That's a good sign." Mabel: "What is?' Amy: " Papa's name at the foot of a nice large check." "Now," said the physician, "You will have to eat plain food and not stay out late at night." 'Oh, that's what I tkought the minute I saw your bill."</p>
        <p>TUMMY "Did, you ever see a real circus?" Jonpv : "No, but I've heard 'ma and 'pa come home late lots of times." Tint boy is not only father to the man, but he aspires to be boss of the woman. When the Woman is his mother he generally succeeds. " On," sighed the tramp. " What I oat the dogs wouldn't touch," and with a tear in his eye he gobbled up the last hunch of grapes. SOMEONE said to-day that the new minister preached over the heads of the congregation. Do you think so?" "He didn't preach over my head. I had on my theatre hat."</p>
        <p>SMITH: Look hero, Brown, we'll aeon decide the matter ; let's ask the waiter. Waiter, are tomatoes a fruit or a vegetable?" Waiter: "Neither, sir. Tomatoes is a extra."</p>
        <p>POULTRY DEALER: "How do you like the goose's liver you had yesterday?" 'Young house- keeper : "It was very nice, iadeed. I want another one, but from the Borne goose, remember." VERY ACCOMPLISHED. - Bessie Nort is : "L wonder why Kitty Winslow is such a social favourite. She doesn't sing, or play, or even recite." Tom De Witt: "Probably that's the reason."</p>
        <p>FRIEND : " You took your son into your estab. lishment some months ago, to teach him the busi. ness, I understand. How did it turn out?" Busi- ness man (wearily) "Great success. He's teaching 'me now."</p>
        <p>"EXCITED LADY (on the beach) "Why isn't something done for that ;hip in distress, sir?" Coastguard: "We have sent the crew a line to come ashore, mum." "Good gracious! Were they waiting for a formal invitation ?" Tire Widow Jones has sued us for damages for lost time. She charges that while she was waiting on us to make money enough to buy a marriage licence, she lost five husbands. REVERSING TIIE ORDER. - Gasket: "I have decided to marry and settle up." Dolley : "Most people marry and settle down." Gasket : " But lam going to marry the rich Miss Roxy and pay my debts.' TOM: "It seems that she doesn't lack for suitors." Jack : "Who doesn't?" Tom: "That girl graduate who wrote an essay on "How to live comfortably on a pound a weak.' _</p>
        <p>PROOF OF SUCClESS.—Toothpick Charley : Well, Spider, how goes it?" Spider: " Flue ; why when I first went on the road I hadn't a rag on my back.' Now look at me, I m all rage A WaßNlNG.—Beggar : "Sir, I am starving." Crceitis : "Here take this cent and tell me how you became so miserably poor." Beggar: "Au, sir, I was like you. I was too fond of giving away large sums of money to the poor." WHY HE Wl:Pr.—Jones : "What makes you ook blue?" Smith: "My only brother is going to marry Nfiss Wnite." Jones : "I don't wonder you feel bid about your brother marrying that hea.rtlesa flirt." Smith : "Oh, it isn't that ; I want to marry her myself." FF.ATIIIRSTONR : "What are you doing for a living now, uncle?" Uncle Ebony: "l's gone into de dry goods business, eah. Featherstone : " VIJ are you, a floor-walker ? " Uncle Eh:my (with great dignity): "No, sal). I's a floor- cleaner."</p>
        <p>AIRS. HAYFORK to Rural Postmaster: "Have you got a post-card for me from Aunt Sally Spriggs. , I was expecting her to write what day she's coming." Rural Postmaster to wife: "Is there one, wife !" Wife : " Yes ; she's coming on Thursday. Parson—" You go to Sunday school, little girl?' " Yeth, thir." "1 hope the little girls there, when you meet, never do or say anything naughty ?" "Well, thir, Mary Brown did thay that Lizzie Thims wath a fool; but then she ith, you know, thir."</p>
        <p>A SWErT, SYMPATHETIC CHILD,—OId gentle- man (at his daughter's wedding) : "My dear, I don't see how I am going to get along without you." Bride: "Never mind, 'pa. Since the cere- mony was performed my husband has confessed that he hasn't enough saved to go to housekeeping, so you may not lose me after all." "DID you see old Skinflint ?" " Yea ; I told him I had come to ask of him the greatest blessing man could seek—his daughter's hand." "An what did he say ?" "He seemed very much pleased.. Said he was afraid at first I wanted to borrow five pounds." Hut APPEARANCE. —Bingo : "Things have gone so with me lately that I have to compel nay wife to make her own dresses." Kingley : "Too bad. How does she look ?" Bingo : "Daggers." PATER: "Do you think that I would allow a 1 man of your uncertain prospects to marry my daughter ?" Suitor : "Yes ; because if I married her, you see, my prospects wouldn't be un- certain."</p>
        <p>" D tDN'T bring the ring to-night," he murmured, in nervous tones. " Why, Henry ?" she asked, with a look in whih reproach and severity were equally blended. "Why, not?" " Well—er—the fact is—er—the other young lady who had it haiin't sent it back yet." A LA.RCIE CHANCE FOR 'TUE EARLY BIRD.— Earth worms six. feet long are found in Gippsland, 'Victoria. T..ey live in burrows on the sloping sides of creeks, and are the largest variety found in the world. It must be a burly bird which picks up the worm in Gippaland. A Nt7747lgß of country neighbours asked the wife of a village butcher what sort of a person the squire's rewly-married wife was. To these inquir lea they received the oracular but convincing reply: "A pulfect lady; she don't know one joint of meat from another.' _</p>
        <p>A MINTER.V EyzeLAlNED.—Excited old gentle- man: " Didn't you say, when I bought these socks, that you'd guarantee them faat colour?' Affable clerk : "We guerantee everything." Exalted old gentleman : " We!, sir, I wore them one day, and they made my feet so Week it is impossible to get it off" Affable clerk (Lriumphantly) : " Well, I don't see what you want of any faster colour than that."</p>
        <p>GETTING TO TIIF, POINT.—" Sir," said a fierce lawyer, "do you, on your oath, swear that this is not your hand writing?" "I think not," was the cool reply. " Does it resemble your writing?" "I can't say it does." "Da you swear that it does not resemble your writin ? " I do." "Do you take your oath that this writing does not resemble yours?" "Y -e-s, sir." "Now, how do you know? " " 'Cause I can't write." Collapse of the fierce lawyer.</p>
        <p>GOT IT DOWN TO A FECF. POlNT.—Jones (to new office boy): Bob, take this letter around to Mr. Smith. If he isn't in, leave it in his office, where it will attraot his attention 53 soon as he cJmes back. It is very important. Jones (15 minutes later) : Bob, did you deliver that letter to Mr. Smith?" : Naw ; he wasn't in an' nobody else was in der offi,;e. Jones : Well, what did you do with it? Bob: You tol' me to put it where he'd notice it first thing he comes in, an' I stuck a pin erough it an' laid it on his chair, so's to attract his attention.</p>
