        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>Mr DEAREST NELLIE, I should like to impress upon you that a woman should put a certain amount of thought and care into her toilette, so that the world may see that there is some individuality in her choice. LaPge hats are more than anything else Porn by children. Coloured straws and rustic straws are most in favour, and they are trimmed with ribbons and wild flowers,. with Cotton, muslin, and other light materials and colours. Muslin hats, drawn on transparent frames and edged round with lace frills, are worn by small children at times instead of the Puritan cap, and are more suitable for sunny weather, being lighter, and more shady for the eyes. The little anor bat, is s ill worn by young girls from 12 years of age and upwards. They are also seen more than other shapes on young brides' heads. Their brims are just a trifle wider than laat year, They are made also in col 'tired straw more than in white straw, and look all the more quiet in conse- quence. Some are in dark brown straw mixed with lighter colours. Some are in shot and shaded straw. They are trimmed with ribbons to match, with large pine or quills fixed in them. They are no longer perched at the top of the head, but they fit the head comfortably, leaving the hair to hang nal_urally in waves, curls, or plaits, ea the case may be.</p>
        <p>A very pretty rut!' fOr the Mick Lai a rosettes of tab}, ribbon alternating with satin bows in a con- trasting colour. It ia fastened with -a knot of loops and ends. A collarette in black net, edged with white lace, describing waved folds, and fastened with two drooping loops and streamers in shaded ribbon also looks very nice. Some novel costumes, in washing materials, have bodices contrasting with the skirt; for example, the latter is composed cf a mauve and white striped cotton crepe. It is cut in a full shape, and is ornamented with three narrow flounces of the same, each one gathered and edged with whits Valenciennes lace. These are placed at intervals from each other, so that the trimming reacher almost as high as the knees. A blouse bodice is composed of a mauve print, studded with small white spots; it mast match the skirt in shade to a nicety, and is made with full fronts, drawn into s waistband, and has large full sleeves. The blouse is quite untrimmed, and is worn under the skirt. so that it meets the latter at the waist.</p>
        <p>A fakhionable costume is in palcst blue foulard, with a pattern in black. The vest and epaulettes are of accordion kilted pale blue crepe de Chine, the sash and revers of black velvet with jet</p>
        <p>A pretty way of trimming the bottom of a skirt is to edge it NNith broad satin ribbon tied here and there in a double bow. Skirts worn out at the bottom should be attended to immediately upon their showing signs of wear. Rip off the facing or under side of the hem, add a fresh lining and velvet, then finish the outside with a wide military braid blind-stitched en the upper edge of the skirt. Grcradine is, 1,1 possible, still more fashionable than in the early part of the season, and looks charming in accordion•pleats or goffors, and with a trimming of black insertion over primrose-yellow or old rose, or.it is very popular with cream Of Ecru lace. fhe basque seems to have utterly dis- appeared, excepting for the belted type of coal, and nearly all plain bodices terminate at the waist- line. Corselets are cut high and quite round at the waist; but as frills and bretelles are brought down to meet the corselet, or it is entwined with ribbon crossing from back to front, few people grasp the idea that a separate corselet is worn.</p>
        <p>A costume of rough cloth of a roseate magenta has attracted my attention. The skirt is quite plain, the reams folded over in- an original fashion each side of a small insertion of a yellow-tinted lace; the cot has a singularly attractive collar, Ana displays a Fiat front of the yellow lace.</p>
        <p>A pretty dress intended more especially for a tr.iiog gown may be worn with or without the short hodice, as the undershirt is made of fancy cambric or flannel, with sleeves and cuffs entire. Tile model is of dsr k chestnut-brown serge, edged with wide blaok military braid. The loug full skirt has a hand of braid run around the foot hem, awl the simulated lumina or short upper skirt is ornamented in the same way. The ecist has slghtly full revere, also edged with the Inaid. and shirts of d &amp;rent colours may be worn. A dull metallic green is very ( trective with this particu;Nl. shade of brown. A black straw sai:or hat, Landed with red brown ribbon, is uncommon and useful, or the tibbon may be altered to.mateb the blouse,</p>
        <p>You atTre,iiooately,</p>
        <p>JENNIE.</p>
        <p>Darn thin places in blankets as von would stockings. Use whiting and benzine, mixed together, to c:ean marble. Shut a boiler of boiling water in a room from which :lie paper is to be removed. Have all ftnnnels, or broadcloth goods, sponged before In .king up, to prevent spottinz. It is stated that a pail of water ron'aining a handful of Iv if placed in a rorm where there has been smoking, will absorb all the odour of the tobacco. A very simple method of inducing sleep in cases of [insistent insomnia, and one that has sic .eaded wlisre many drugs have failed, is- simply tnarimi dater a moder- ate amount of warm liqiiid food before the patientgoes</p>
