      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>EXAMINER. SATURDA Y, AITGUST 19, 1893.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SATURDA Y, AITGUST 19, 1893.</p>
        <p>, ,</p>
        <p>to bed. This diverts the blood from the brain to the at,d, Mnal organs, and takes away the ceiebral excite- ment that precludes sleep:.</p>
        <p>• The editor of " Baby " earnestly cautions mothers a !ainc ahowinc• their children to paddle and bathe at their own sweet wills when paying the annual visit to the seaside. It is an error into which most mothers fall.; They turn their little ones loose on the sands. allow them to dabble about for hours under a hot sun, and with chilled feet, and then fondly imagine that all is well; and that the children are storing up a grand stock *Rh.</p>
        <p>Ham Toast. Scrape or pound some cold Pam, mix it with a beaten egg, season with pepper, lay- it on buttered toast, and placo it in a hot oven for three or four minutes. Dried salmon, smoked tongue, potted meats; or any other re - fishing via n is, answer equally well upon toasts.</p>
        <p>Small Sausages. rut a teaspoonful of dripping in a frying pan. Let it melt, but not get hot. Prick the sausages with a darning neediv. Put them in and fry very slowly about fifteen nutes, turning them often. Take them out, and if. you like stir a desvrt spoonful of flour into the pan, and a cup of water ; stir and boil. It will make sauce for them.</p>
        <p>Scalloped Fish. rick any cold, cooked fish carefully from the bones, and moisten with an egg beaten up in snilk; add one large blade of pounded mace,Nme tablespoonful of flour, one teaspoonful of anchovy sauce, a lump of butter, some bread crumbs, and pepper and salt to taste. Place, in a deep dish or in scal/op shells ; cover with bread crumbs, butter the top, and brown quickly in the oven Serve very hoz..</p>
        <p>A Summer Bath. 'Put to a cup of sea salt, one half-oturce of camphor and one half-ounce cf ammonia in a quart bottle ; fill the bottle with hot water and let it stand 24 hours ; then, when prepared to bathe with a sponge, put a tea- spoonful of this mixture, well shaken, into your basin. A surprising quantity or dirt will come from the cleanest skin. The ammonia cleanses and the camplinr and the sea salt impart a beneficial effect which cannot be t xag- gerated.</p>
        <p>Silk Underwear.</p>
        <p>Silk stockings and silk underwear generally should not Le worn two days in succession. When taken off they should be shaken free of dust, -turned inside out, and hung in a current of air. This will reduce the number of wsishingernecessary to them. They should bo washed in wsorn, hut not hot suds, made with white Castile soap, and rinsed in warm water. , They should be wrung out gently, stretched and dried, but they should not be ironed.</p>
        <p>TIIE'SUCIALIST LEADERS AT ZU RICH,</p>
        <p>Dr. Aveling and Eleanor Mark Aveling eontributh the following sketches at the Zarich Congress to rhe'" West- minster Gazette." • .</p>
        <p>' Out of the sea of faces of all types, figures of all shapes voices of all imaginable timbres, in this most extraordin- ary and significant gathering, a few of the most ty pica! rise. Charles Hobson, president of the English section. A strange compound ; a strange example of the modify- ing, t, he vivifying effect of inteinational gatherings. A little, leaven of the old trade unionism left. But it leavens a whole lump advanced ideas that—whisper it not in the Sheffield Town Council, tell it not on the magisterial bench—are very nearly socialistic. This town councillor and J.P. has an air of strange respectability in this out- landish Congress an air suggestive of Wesleyan local preaching. He Is scrupulausly fair and impartial chair- man._</p>
        <p>France sends, owing to the elections on August 20, a singularly meagre and ineffective representation. Poor Charles Bernard, the solitary member of the once power ful Foal Ouvricnostands solitary member of the powerful Frenchmen willing to admit Anarchists to a Socialist. Congress. But he stands firm. Being indeed a man of robustious physique, tronzed and bearded, square- shouldered, and not unknown upon the Isis and in the football fields of Oxford. He is, like many thousand Frenchmen, the sworn devotee of Jules Guesde, who, Bonnier says, is certain to be returned on the 20th for Roubaix.</p>
        <p>Over the Pyrenees the Cnngress passes in Spain. Ig- glesias, one of the most picturesque personalities in the Congress ; which unhappily has thus far abounded in personalities in another sense, anything but picturesque Powerfully built, with a face of singular charm—not handsome, but impresive and attractive. He speaks no language but Spanish, save for "a poor penny worth of French." Yet when lie speaks, by the ring and volume of his voice, the kaleidoscopic change of feature, the gestures that speak, the intense conviction and absolute reliability that burn out from the man, we all seem to know what he says. Northwards now to Belgium. Jean Volders, for ten years a fighter—another man of huge physique. But the ten years have told upon him ; his nerves are some- what shattered, his head is not so clear as it was at Brussels two years ago, The Belgians, par consequence,' have been a little mixed once or tivioe. The voice hoarse from just a shade too touch speaking at the Congress. Biit the heart and the big soul, that has led the Belgians through such a wilderness Owards the promi:sed land, as sound and true aS'ever'.</p>
        <p>Across the frontier to Germany, August Bebe!. There are those who say that ke and Liebknecht 4oglinate the German party and the Congresi. But your dominant natures must dominate. They do not dictate. They simply tininess their own powerful individuality on us lesser. folk. When Bebel rose no whlle 'house literally rose at him. Not only the delegates, but every man, woman, and child in the places reserved for the public rose to their feet to look at hitn. Quiet, compact of build, compact of thought, of oratory incisive, con- densed, not elaborate or ornate' a mind's eye piercing straight to the heart 'of men and things, and at once tak- ing in and understanding their bearing upon other men and other things, an absolutely convincing and uncon- querable man. . . .</p>
        <p>_ The sister country, Austria, sends among others Dr. Victor Adler, editor, physician, socialist, prisoner again and again. Also not of the gigantic type, but with any amount of power tomilressed into his spare frame, worn with work for the cause.</p>
        <p>Of the Swiss, with their kindly heartiness and their admirable arrangements, we should like tn give a hun- dred sketches. Two thumbnail ones only. Hermann Orenlich vice president of the Committee of Organisa- tion. Like Rebel, built on medium lines. A grey, hearty man, with a sense of humour at once 'keen and broad. Very faithful, ode would say, looking at the strong, true face, and listening to the strong, true Voice, without a false note in. Business-like withal, and no- table amidst the hurtle of the battle when some of us bad to turn the Anarchists out.</p>
        <p>Karl Burkly, the hale and hearty old man of 70, who on Sunday welcomed the delegates in three languages— German, French, English. Reis a Zurich man born and bred, and a disciple of Fourier and Socialism since the year 1845. A fine old fellow' with his.. white hair and jovial face. An author ta boot, although be was a tan- ner by trade, with a list of books, all bearing on Social- ism directly or indirectly, at great length. The Italian table, whether at the Congress or at the midday cUjetiner or at dinner, is a delightful sight. All fireand verve. Southern vivacity and dash, solidified, as it were, by the informing principle of a creed. The creed, Socialism. At the Congress they are all ears and earnestness, and, occasionally, let it be admitted, all tongues. But the tongues speak not only one language philologically considered, but one language of economics and politics. At the table, in its special sense, they' are the life and soul of any cafe they may brighten by their presence. Why cannot the British delegates put a little more life and friendliness to the world in general into their eating and drinking at least ? The spectacle of the Italians, led in enjoyment as in work by the strong, , clear-headed Turatti and his wife, is an object-lesson to I US of northern and more placid olood. The Poles are much ef a muchness with the Italians. They vote solid, they have the liveliness to which we are strangers, always, as it appears to us, toned down, as their music is, by a greater consciousness of wrong dode, and they are led by a man and a woman, Stanislas and Marie Mendelson. Mendelson, with his jolly figure and face brimming over with humour and kindliness, his eternal cigarette, his jests in three languages la- the last man in the world you would take for a Social- ist first, and under Polish condition, a conspirator after. Wards.</p>
        <p>It is an easy and natural transition from Po!and to Russia. Plekhanova : A singular figure. Personally, one of the many delightful human beings only, as we think, to be met with at these congresses. Something on the Mendelson model, corooreally, mentally, spiritu- ally. Has been through this and that. But to U 3 at the Congress, despite all his bonhomie, his splendid scientific attainments, his energy, his devotion, the embldiment at this Congress of 1893 of the Russian revolutionary Socialist movement. The first delegate that has ever come with an actual mandate from the secret Socialist societies of St. Petersburg. That, at least, is an event in history. . .</p>
        <p>- Away to the north—to Norway, the land of the fjord , and the field, and, at last, of Somalism. Thence comes Kroger Johansen, of the Norwegian Workers' Party and editor of the "Social Democrat" of Christiania. Young, clear-eyed, fair, as becomes a son of the Vikings—who, by the way, would have put down Socialism with a very strong hand—and as clear-headed as clear-eyed. Speak- ing English also wonderfully well. And now, rough further away. Over not seas but oceans. The Atlantic first. ' Across this have come Abraham Cakan, the • beloved of the Jews on both sides of the " herring pond," who made the most powerful , speech from the point of view of strong as against reasoned attack on the Anarchists ; Lucien Sanials, with his sailor air about him, who speaks English with a charming half-French, half-American accent, and French with a perfect memory of his early days ; and Daniel De Leon, ahilorn professor of international law at Columbia University, New York. Still further at sea. This time to Australia, whence,in the true spirit of International Socialisin, an Italian, Francis Sceusa. A dapppr, neatly built man, voting very straight on all advanced questions. and insisting, rightly enough, very strongly on the historic fact that he is the first delegate to an mternationsl Socialist Con- gress from Australia. Altogether a wonderful Collection of faces, typical of the mass of wonderful faces that make theft-tor of the Tonhalle and the galleries a sea with which De Quincey might well deal. Looking upon which one pauses and reflects, " And of such is the society' of the future."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>Creamed Codfish.</head>
        <p>Creamed Codfish.</p>
        <p>Pick carefully into flakes cold, boiled codfish,rejecting all skin and bones. Melt apiece of butter in a sauce- pan, and add to it a large pinch of flour, pepper, alt and grated nutmeg to taste, with one gill of cream, also a little cayehbe. Stir until smooth ; put in the fish, and gently shake it in this sauce until quite warm. If not enough sauce, add a little more milk or cream. After taking it off the fire stir in quickly the yolks of two eggs beaten up with a little milk, and serve imme- diately.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>How To Breathe.</head>
        <p>How To Breathe.</p>
        <p>Comparatively few people paY much if any attention to their breathing. they generally regard this as one of the automatic movements of the body, like the beating of the heart and the circulation of the blood, which in a healthy body regulate themselves without our taking thought about them. If men and women were each day employed at work in the open air that will exercise the muscles of arms, shoulders, and the upper portions of their bodies, this carelessness about breathing may do little harm. The work will compel the person doing ,It to take full breaths of air, and this of itself is surfielent to give the lungs the natural expansion that they need: But there are millions who do not work in the open air, and a large la, rtion of these scarcely exercise the upper parts of their bodies. For all such, attention to correct breathing is the very first condition of health ; for upon deep,full breathing must depend the the healthful beat- ing of the heart that is required to keep up active circu- lation of the blood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>WOUNDING CASES AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>WOUNDING CASES AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>On Monday, at the Widnes Police Court, before Messrs. 11. Wade Deacon and F. A. Stapleton- Bretherten, a young woman named Mary Ann Tobin, 4, Elizabeth-A., was charged, in custody, with unlawfully wounding Annie Goulding, a married woman, on Saturday night. From the evidence adduced it appears that the prisoner lived with the prosecutrix, and on Saturday during a quarrel about money matters,prisoner struck prosecutrix a blow on the head with the hatchet. She was otherwise abused and when examined by Dr. Olieeffe later in the even- ing he was found te be suffering from two contused lacerated wounds on the forehead, both of which pene- trated to the bone.—As the doctor said the woman's condition was very dangerous, prisoner was remanded in custody for a week. , At the same Court a young man named Patrick But- ler, 28, Wellington-street, was charged with unlawfully wounding his wife Mary Ann Butler, on Saturday night. Superintendent Keighley said neither prosecu- trix nor any witnesses were present : but Dr. Donnelly and Police Constable Martin would prove that Mrs. Butler had sustained severe injuries.—Dr. Donnelly said he examined Mrs. Butler on Sunday morning, and found her suffering from a gash in her upper lip, and there were indications that she had been abused. Sergeant Strickland said prisoner when arrested admitted to him of having hit his wife twice in the face, and giving her a " dab "in the mouth. The chairs in the house were troken,and there had apparently been a terrible struggle. Ptittoner was remanded in custody,and summonses were granted to compel the prosecutrix and the witnesses to a, tend. The man Butler was again brought before Mr. Deacon at the Petty Sessions on Thursday. Mr. Knowles who defended, said the wife declined to give evidence, and he asked for the prisoner's discharge. The couple had lived comfortably together for three years,and he was surethat a God-fearing old gentleman like Superintendent Keighley,would not wish to put asunder those whom God had joined together.—Superintendent Keighley said the case must stand on its merits, and Mr. Deacon said tie must hear evidence before discharging the prisoner. —The -rife was sworn but declined to give evidence. Elizabeth Dowd, a neighbour, spoke to hearing a disturbance in the house and, entering, she found Mrs. Butler bleeding from a wounded lip and a black es e. P.S. Strickland clexribed how he found the house in The greatest dis- order, and the woman's clothes saturated with blood.— Mr. Deacon said there was not sufficient evidence to con- vict, but he had no doubt the prisoner had brutally assaulted his wife, and he had had a lucky escape.</p>
        <p>The Poyal Laundresses say Critchley's "STARCH GLOSS is unequalled for Imparting a beautifni finish to starched linen, dc., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr.—, Corn- wall, says :—" My wife will not finish our linen without your '• Starch Gloss.'" From Mrs. —, Bradford "Dear sit lam pleased to inform you that I have tried many so-callo.i Starch Glazes and Polishes, but find none equal to yours." It should be used in every house. Sold at Id., 3d., and 64.. b_y most starch genets. If not In stook will procure It. T. eniTentrr.Blsokburn. P.norl*tor.-74‘ht.</p>
