      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>DARK WOOD.</head>
        <p>DARK WOOD.</p>
        <p>A TALE OF LONEMOOK. BY Mita. HARMET Lowts, Author of "Her Double Life," "Old Life's Shadows," "Lilly Kildare," etc.</p>
        <p>CHArrnit HOW PIETRO's PLANS WomcND. (wen's absence from her rooms was net dis- covered until about nointon the following day. The entire household rose late, having retired late. The Lady Georgina slept until nearly noon, and then rang ,her bell for her maid. The latter came in, looking as if she had but recently risen. "My breakfast," said the heiress, peremptorily. " And tell,Miss Myner that I wfsh to see her." "1 haven't been called to help Miss Myner dress yet, Lady Georgina," said the maid. "I think she , is not awake."</p>
        <p>-" Go and see," commanded the young mistress. The maid departed on her errand, returning with the information that Miss Myner was not in her . rooms, which were in order, and that she had probably gone for a walk. " Very well," said Lady Georgina ; "when she comes in I want to:we her. Itis very singular that she should go out alone, and just:when Iwañt her." , The young lady ate her breakfast and made her toilet leisurely. - Then she sent out again for her companion. - The maid returned with the statement that Miss Myner was still out. • "She is out a long time, I think," said the Lady , Georgina, in a tone of annoyance. "I wonder where she can be?" She went herself to Gwen's rooms and discovered that the girl's hat and jacket were missing. As she avas about to return to her own apartments, she espied the ink-stained envelope upon the open desk, time overturned inkstand, and the stain upon the carpet. . . " A careless piece of business, I should say," sail the piing lady to herself. "She has made a I pretty mess of the carpet. I wonder who she has been writing to—those relations of hers in Lon- demi?" . • She approached the desk and took up the unaided envelope. There was but little sense of honour in Lord Dark wood's daughter. She believed the letter pri- vate—not meant for her eyes—and a desire to read it seized upon her. "The address is all blotted Out," she commented, "and the envelope is not sealed." She opened it- aeliberately.• The address, "My darling," startled her, aroused her,euriosity, and so excited her that no sense of propriety, or honour,. or self-respect could have rest-rained her from perusing the epistle' to the end. "So she has a lover," thought Lord Dark wood's (laughter. "She's a Sly, piece I What's the " obstacle " she speaks of? - Why can't she marry. him ? She met him last evening. Then, it was some one in the castle. It,:-couldn't be a servant. Who was it ?—my father ?" She was inclined to believe that the marquis vv`as. the lover referred to in- the, letter,, ancl Ery incensed according'•7. It Was some time before she arrived at the fall weaning of the letter, ttnd then her conviction was that G Wen had fled' from the castle. She made a hasty search of the rooms, and dis- covered that Gwen's handbag had disappeared. This discovery was brought about by her maid, who had been accustomed to assist Gwen in her- toilet, and wh.m had followed her young mistress now into Gwen's apartment. _ __ .</p>
        <p>em; Miss Myner's travelling bag in her dress- ing-room yesterday," said the maid, "and now it's gone; and she's gone too. What does it all mean, Lady Georgina ?'</p>
        <p>"-Ring tile bell." The maid obeyed. A servant appeared. The Lady Georgina sent a messenger to ,Mrs. Dover, requesting hr presence in Miss Myner's rooms. The housekeeper made or appearance. Cony) in," said Lord Darkwood's daughter..</p>
        <p>'Mrs. Dover, have you seen Miss Myner to aay?" Ws. Dover replied in. the negative. _</p>
        <p>"She has ,g. ii," said Lady Georgina. "Here is a letter she left behind her. The address is blotted out, hut I think she wrote my father's name. The deceitful, sly. minx ! She didn't leave Dunholm Castle any too soon, I can tell her. Read the letter 1" She gave it into the housekeeper's hands. 'Mrs. Dover read it hastily, with a shocked expression on her kindly face. "There is some mystery in this," said the lady. "The letter is unsigned. No doubt Miss Myner forgot to sign it in her agitation. Lord Darkwood ,must know of this immediately, Lady Georgina. It is quite clear that Miss Myner has gona to her friends. But why should she leave so secretly? Perhaps Lord Darkwood will understand the mys- tery." "I will take it to him," cried Georgina, eager to prove her suspicions true.. She hurried down to the library, but it was un- tenanted. She questioned the hall porter, and he informed her that Lord Darkwood had gone down to Dunholin village with his bailifE The girl slowly returned towards the rooms. On the stairs she encountered Pietro, calm, sleek, and smooth as usual. He eyed her sharply. She was about to pass on, when she changed her purpose and came to a halt. " Pietro !" she exclaimed, "when my father returns, send a servant to tell me." "Yes, my lady." "Pietro," she continued, "have you seen any- thing of Miss Myner to-day ? 'r "Nothing, my lady." And Pietro looked surprised. "She is gone, and nobody seems to have seen her. She must have left the house at a very early hour. She could not have walked to Shrewsbury. do wish that my 'father would come !" She returned to Gwen's rooms, where Mrs. Dover was engaged in questioning a housemaid,, who averred that she had not put Miss Myner's room in order that day." "Then Miss Myner either sat up all night,"said the housekeeper, "or she left the castle last night." "She was in her room at nine o'clock," declared the housemaid, for I mended her fire at that time and she said good-night to me." Mrs. Dover, by• dint of cross-questioning the housemaid and one or two other servants, dia. Hortered that Gwen had been seen at the eastle sa late as eleven o'clock on the preceding night. Some one had seen her crossing the quadrangle at . or about that -hour, coming from the direction of the ruins, where she was supposed to have gone to leek at the dancers. • She had not occupied her bed. The door of the small porch opening upon the quadrangle had been by the butler at &amp;bent one o'clock, and had been found unfastened in the morning. he might have gone out at that door at any time between elle o'clock and seven. That she had not gone to, bed argued an earlier departure: But as she was only a tender; delicate girl, she would not have been likely to go out alone at an unseemly hoar, but would have been inclined to wait for the dawn.</p>
        <p>Thus Mrs. Dover argued. Inquiries at the stable proved that she hod not had any conveyance to take her away.. The house- keeper, believing that the girl had gone on foot to Shrewsbury, sent a mounted groom thither with a written message to be given into, Gwen's own hands. By the time all these steps had been taken, and investigations made, it was four o?clock, A mes- senger came up with the announcement that Lord Dad: wood had returned, and was in the library. The Lady Georgina ran 'down to him.</p>
        <p>10 _ .y Georgina ran dol, He was sitting at a writing-table when she appeared, and he looked up with an expression of annoyance. "What do you want?" he demanded. "Fattier," cried Georgina, "Miss Myner is gone !"</p>
