        <p>At the announcement of the names of Miss ! servant at eight o'clock. She was supposed tO" be Norreys and Lord Chilton, Lord Darkwood came sleeping, and it was not nntil noon that the maid forward, all smiles and welcome, to receive his who waited upon her, and who had overslept her- ' visitors. In the delight of greeting the lady of his 'self, went. into the room, and found that the bed love, the marquis forgot the open letter on his liadliot been slept in last night, and that she was writing table, and the annoyance and perplexity gone !" which had been caused him by Gwen's most unex- "The bed had not been slept in ?" kiected disappearance. "No, my lord. Her night was evidently sleep- Miss Norreys, exquisitely attired in an artistic less.' visiting dress' of chocolate-coloured silk' relieved ' Miss-Norreys was regarding the marquis with a with dashes of cream Colour; entered in advance of keen and piercing gaze, as if she meant to probe her escort, her train sweeping the floor, her pale his soul to its utmost depths. Did she suspect him olive face and dusky ripples of hair crowned by a of having harmed Gwen? If she did so suspect at Rubens hat of chocolate colour, decorated with first, she was too skilled a student of mankind to cream-coloured ostrich plumes. - entertain the idea long. She saw that he had been . Lord Darkwood bent low over her daintily-gloved surprised at Gwen's disappearance—that his sur- band, and conducted her to. a chair. He greeted prise and bewilderment were genuine. Lord Chilton with courteous warmth, and inquired I Undoubtedly the girl, stung by the recollection after the guests at Beechmont. After the usual of her origin, with, some exalted notion of self- interchange of civilities, including comments upon sacrifice, and deeming herself unworthy to become -I! the festivities of the previous evening, and ,a little: the wife of Lord Chilton, had gone -away to her pause .10.4 occurred in the conversation, Miss , friends with thelit