        <p>letter. • " Would ycm be good enough, my lord, to excuse the abruptness of Miss Myner's departure ?" in- quirel young the viscount. "And will you kindly seal her to join us ?" " One moment," said the marquis, twirling the fatal letter between his fingers. "How long have you known Miss Myner, Lord Chilton ?" The young viscount looked surprised. A haughty • Bush arose to his fair face and died out. He an- swered courteoosly— " For many Months—since last September, my lord. We were betrothed before she came to Dun- holm Castle." "After she came here she withdrew her address from you, and you did not know her whereabouts until last evening 2" Lord Chilton BOwed assent. I conclude, then, that Miss Myner had reasons, which doubtless seemed good to her, for concealing herself from you," said the marquis.. "Pardon me, Lord Chilton, for my seeming rudeness, but is it not so." Again the young viscount bowed. "Miss Myner conceived that there was an obstacle to our marriage," he replied. "But I set her doubts all at rest during our interview of last evening. She has consented to become my wife. I am quite at loss, Lord Darkwood— " The marquis interposed with a deprecating ges- ture. "Again, pardon me," he said. "If I. could I would further your suit with Miss 3fyners Lord Chilton. It would delight me to be present at your marriage with her and to give away the bride. Her marvellous beauty, her grace, her manners, her intellectual attainments, would do honour to any position, and lend new lustre to the coronet you would place upon her brow. But the obstacles she believed to exist bet yawn you must have been more seriolus then you fancied, my lord. You may have seemed in your interview with her last evening to clear them away, but, when her reason reasserted its sway, they must have seemed to her as formid- able as ever." "I fail to comprehend your meaning, my lord," said the viseomit, puzzled at this very singular address. "Lord Darkwood has more to say," declared Miss Norteys, who had been regarding her host very keenly, and who was struck by his-manner. The marquis hawed assent to this suggestion, "Has Miss Myner made any communication to you in regard to me, Lord Darkwoodl" demanded the viscount, abrupily. "She has not," answered the marquis promptly. "I never heard bee mention your name. Until you told toe yourself, I did not even know that it was you who were the lover. My. lord,. I do. not kuowthow to break the tidings to; you—but. Miss Myner has left my house. She is, gone—" Miss Noereys grew pale as death., "Gone !" echoed Lord Chilton,. stupefied: "Gone 1" repeated the marquis, emphatically. "Her departure surprises me. as nuich as, it can surprise you." "When did she go ?" asked the viscount, looking . .</p>
        <p>"Sign My name," suggested the marquis promptly. "They would expect to hear from me, and, if you did not telegraph to them, I 1% ould - Lord Chilton passed over the paper and pen, and Lord Darkwood appended his own name to the document. ' Resuming his pen, the viscount wrote another message—this one to be sent to Mrs. Quillett. Lord Darkwood, with a strange expression on his face, sidled nearer to his guest, and threw a long sidelong glance upon the paper. These words traced there he read distinctly, as follows— To Mrs. Quillet, Lonenioor, Penistous, York- shire.—Miss Whiter has left her situation. Let me know if she communicates with you. Am verY anxious." To this Lord Chiltern appended his own name with a great misgiving. The Quillets had refused to answer his former inquiries after Gwen, and they would probably not be more communicative now than heretofore. Yet he could not rest without informing them of Gwen's movements. Even if they did not answer it, they might forward it to Gwen, who would understand from those few words his great unspoken solicitude. . He enclosed tile scrap of paper upon which the last.mentioned message had been written in an envelope and stood up. The marquis was standing erect, rigid, strange, and ashen-faced. There was something positively ghastly in his round fat visage, and his eyes were full of amazement and horror. Miss Norreys was staring at him with great Vivid eyes and a pallor nearly equal to his own.. "Are you ill, my lord 1" questioned the viscount,. astonished at his demeanour. "No—that is, yes," answered the marquis start- ing, and drawing a long breath. 4.1 b.g your pardon, my lord. lAM not quite- myself,. The fatigue of the past two nights begin to. tell upon me. lam new to society dissipation.. And this affair coming when I find myself so nearly worn. WA He broke off abruptly in his easy harangue, as ii suddenly conscious that he was sp, eking. He rang Isis hell violently, and dispatched a mounted groom. to Shrewsbury with the two messages.. Miss Norreys now arose, exclaiming— " We will drive on to Shrewsbury, Lord Chilton.. As Miss Myner walked, we may find her still them. We can at least learn if she has been, seen at tho• Railway station." She made her adieuxwith easy graoei, LordDlink- wood escorting her to the waiting carriage. He' returned to his library; and walked to and fro with. impetuous tread, muttering— " The secret is out! That girl I. have known•an. Miss Marian Myner is really Gwendoline Winter!. She has been in my house for six months, and, 1: never suspected her identity. I must find her! It is fortunate that Pietro never suspected the truths more than myself. No wonder that she reBemblee4 the Charteris family. Fool—fool that lam ! Why did I never suspect who she really is? And she has; got away in safety. I'll join in the search for lion' I. must outwit Lord Chilton • I must not excite, Pietro's suspicions—l must b crafty as a ser</p>
        <p>as if he was utterly; bewildered. "That we do not know. I went over to. Dun- hohn village this afternoons" said. the marquis, "and, upon my return, an. hour.a.go, I w.as met by the tidings that Miss Myner had. disappeared. No one saw her go. She did not say goodi-lye to Georgina. She went out secretly— " " That was not like her," interposed Lora Chilton warmly. "Did she leave no letter—no explana- tion ?" "Only this," said the marciiii.s, taking up the letter which he had been</p>
