        <p>In connection with the Kaiser's recent visit to .',.ngland and his tri-quent absence from home, there 117,_,ft good story told of his little son Prince Eitel ut; who has lust reached the mature age of ten. upa, once remarked the little fellow," is always from home. It is a good thing mamma ::nlains with us, else there would be nobody here when the babies are born."</p>
        <p>The Admiralty have informed Lord Charles Beres- ieird:that they cannot allow him to count his year's !!rvice on the staff of Lord Wolseley in the Soudan ZoWards the six years' full-pay service requisite for ~cl!Ptaln to put, in, in order to qualify himselt for 7ttlve flag-rank. This, of course, means that Lord l'vuntles will have to serve this twelve months all lioer again, and consequently defer his return to the use of Commons for a year longer than he had 111. tic!Pated. His lordships feelings may be bett:er leaagined than described.</p>
        <p>tning paper gives currency to a statement BC 444 Tryon has notified to the Admiralty that 8 declines the pension t e which she is entitled as " Admiral's widow, the amount of which is given kg ,e6OO a year. This statement is palpably incor- ftc!: The Admiralty regulations distinctly state odet pensions will not be granted to the widows of las vi hose private incomes exceed the canfiden- tuat ,caie fixed by the Board as the limit for each :Icor officer. As Lady Tryon is possess,ql of ample rnsion, „_lVa.te incans it is evident she could not obtain a even if she wished for one, as though the sideale" i 8 COtliPential, it is known not to err on the e of liberality. Moreover, the amount of the pet q ' Wm would ziot be anything like £6OO a year— Ore than .000.</p>
        <p>toif it is true that, the Prince of Wales, during his toraitg visit to Homburg will occupy a suite of ,21118 at Ritter's Hotel, the fact will point to a zittatual understanding between His Royal llighness I,lle Beniface in question. Some few years ago 1" .lince engaged rooms at Ritter's, and then re- ested that a private entrance should be arranged ur his own use. --- jtBtit Ritter refused the favour, urging that he 11,1 d not inconvenience guests who stayed at his wirl for some months for the sake of the Prince, tb °.Be visit only ext ended to three weeks. On this -crince gave up his rooms, and since then has bay Patronised the hotel in question. People who pri vat e read the present report are wondering if that , entrance has been made.</p>
