        <p>--- e The visit of Prince Bismarck to Kissingen has di‘v been an annual fixture with the pondeious ex- -134,ce110r for the past 21 years. It is scarcely sur- th.Bl,4, therefore, that he is generally regarded in town the almost as their tutelary deity, and that the good folk of Kissingen have erected a statue to baulilethroned statesman. When he drives to the .), Which is a daily duty at twelve o'clock, there .4117 ter, aYe, even now when he is no longer in power, N' s'Wdon the road eager to cheer 'the veteran -"lttiBter.</p>
        <p>j.t. is a sine qua non with every visitor to Kiss- t° be weighed, and to this day they show one Weighing chair in which the great man, in more of the word than one, has been registered 1,17 Year since 1874, and the date of his first visit. bidetn,, the Chancellor took. some 2071b5. to make the Ver. ,"ce even, hut this a few years later reached the ; Pri.,l respectable figure of 2071b5. ; lust year, the c: sr aa . had, thanks to diet and care of his medical the , Dr. Sch weniger, regained his 2061 b. What tr 'l-Chancellor scales this year has not yet been 448Pired, bei°L4lVobin, the chatelaine of which, the young wikesis. of Sutherland, has just presented the Duke of be ,a ittle daughter, claims the proud distinction Isie,lrirZ the oldest inhabited building in the British fern,i Lord Ronald Gower, who, as a member of the O is well acquainted with the many legends ot",,;_a Place, is the authority for this statement. , hie "le original castle, which has stood countless oliftes in the dark ages of Scottish history, is now coMpletely concealed by the modern build- . oef!,,, a maze of graceful turrets and tapering pepper- ' Ate;cir roofs, which contrast admirably with the B°lidily of the grim Scottish donjon keep.</p>
        <p>10018iits lights shine out over the North Sea, over- ideaing the Moray Firth, Dunrobin is indeed the en° castle of the poet's dream, and is peopled with an VI spooks, the heroes and heroines of most 444,13,4 tales, to occupy the attention of Mr. Stead 1t,3 'llB follovvers for the next twelve months. Lord clevllll,ld Gower in his delightful "Reminiscences ' tob."es an entire chapter to the legends of I un- to :ill]. Lord Ronald, it may be mentioned, is uncle e Present Duke' oi Sutherland, the laird of •nrabin Castle. it4e,nragraph has recently been the rounds, relat- or ,r,ti—ln celebration in Canada of the SW) birthday • stitt:ister of Carlyle. The author of " Sartor Re- ailedshad quite a number of relatives in the vra„: States. At the very time that Thomas Carlyle liB_e-tnoulding, as it were, the very train of Eng- kit„`"nught, his brother John was growing pump- Grand Rapids, Michigan, and his chief tiBe,e, Was that he could milk more cows before sun- - vinan any other man in the country round. John ste4l e'B children occupy to this day the old home- terit and one of his sons Charles is a Supervisor of 4rBt, County and and, characteristically enough, is the ",einber of the family who has ever expressed g 114 1.13.3a11e5t pride in having the sage of Chelsea as riarteizan.</p>
        <p>Joh triet e, his father, is well remembered in the dis- -I,iv 1,11 :which he and his brother William Carlyle itite}(ll•°oen in fact,was the tirst white man to settle eueighbourhood. He was as rugged and homely tiwtir:t4l3tlielniz his eermight be expected to be, the Bible con- tt entire library. He is known to have 'en to his brother in England, but the two were ttev'ell dissimilar tastes, that communications were title" killnained. William, the brother, died so long 44otehtil4t he is completely forgotten. There was yet tait- er brother, James Carlyle, who struck out in Atwe,a,clifferent direction, and betook himself to ''4411, uof him no tidings ever reached borne.</p>
        <p>, igte ,- announcement that Ms 3 Ansye. Reade, the ilew`Aarles Reade's daughter, is occupied upon a 1010 novel, reminds one of the singular but little- thrown adventures which that lady has gone' ito.ugh,in her efforts to expose the cruelties and tetlßnities of circus life. When Miss Meade first ki„ceived the idea of writing " Ruby," she deter- gee'ed to investigate the sub,.ect at first hand, and tire°rdingly hung about the tents of the travelling shet'°-ea to learn all she could. In one case, here ere,,nsPected that a child was being ill-treatedv , s tior ander the curtain of the tent when the v.ir, lace Wai left alone and found its back brut to rated. In all this espionage she was subje,c- a itilde"rY liind of insult from the proprieo,S, s InanY occasions ran serious risks age,</p>
        <p>her' " danger did not e.."--c lyt.th the publication * ler book F day sne was a marked Illeacii. • The .orw °nm —pes of the travelling circuses n her • and,when she lectured 114 hier;o.6ll,,44V7—in the country, she did 80 to rooms tde't _he" l'Y aPponents,who hooted her and cried her etua.a-,Vra first to last. But she continued her ---"a a second story," Slaves of the Sawdust," 41,4 ea, *eel' " navr, it is said, look with satisfaction upon, a 1 beneficial result for her efforts. 4458 -- arßti -weeds h* a striking appearance, which 1114 4eB, great strength of will and courage. She „474 ti firm mouth, pole,far-away looking eyes, Nit i'cae hair peeping in curls from under her hat. altogether a woman to attract attention, 4*(l,Wheti she talks, her energy carries her hearers You feel her sincerity at once. c,W. r. Lockhart, who died at Bailater on was the Sptirgeon of Liverpool, and the Istr4tl-41 Tabernacle, the principal scene of his ms 01'1 the 7 WOIVIPV!, mm- IS one of e most spacious place f thloP,,`„ll the provinee.,4 seating comfortably over ladversons. lie had not the pungent tatym; clt: rhetorical gifts of his metropolitan pro- `-the had a tree and unconventional style Ittgl "ntease earnestness that was very effective e tl"Qoti people hears him gladly. Thong! lkstti.„4,lla3Y Preached twice a gunday, and had tnh eme ar —„,ee of swnething like seven thousad ' snecu.tending the Tabernacle and its three at 4 Missions, i.e remained all hi a life a layman ' tki:4B first a broker in the Spanish trade, and managAr of the Lirerpc.ol Investment 1441'11.1R Raciety. fie was a staunch total abstainer a characteristic instance of his strict prin- ''ess 4%,at when he succe(Wed to his father's busi- tind teraker he deliik:ratety abandoned the large tatiolpitivs connection of the firm in the impor- -BPAnish wines, as he did not think he</p>
        <p>Could consistently have imrthing to do with the liquor trade in any iorin. The sacrifice was the greater because he as then on the eve of marriaae, fis wife heiniA the youngest daughter of Mr. Joslina Freeman. one of the largest tenants on the Sandringham estate of the Prince of Wales.</p>
        <p>Endowtd with tine physique, Mr. Lockhart delighted in manly sport, and in his younger days was a notable cricketer, captaining the Birkenhead Park Eleven. for many years, and retaining till the last a warm interest in the game and in the fortunes of the Eleven of his adopted county. That he has died at the comparatively early age of fifty-eight, must be ascribed to the tremendous strain upon his Constitution by his double labours, but measured by the work he has accomplished, his life has been long indeed.</p>
