      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>WARRINGTON TRIPEDRESSER IN</head>
        <p>A WARRINGTON TRIPEDRESSER IN</p>
        <p>TROUBLE.</p>
        <p>lIIS FIRST COW.7-A SHADY TRANSACTION.</p>
        <p>- - - John Massey,tripeboi ler, 5, Wild erspool-caus ewes, was summoned before the Warrington magistrates, on Monday,on the charge of having in his possession for sale,on July 27,the carcase of a cow,alleged to be unfit for human food.—Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk) prosecuted, and Mr. Lees, of Wigan,defended. Mr. Percy Davies watched the proceedings on be- half of Mr. Ashley, in whose slaughter-house the cow was being dressed. The Town Clerk said that these proceeelings were taken under the Public Health Act, 1885. Information was conveyed to one of the Corporation inspectors that a cow was lying in afield off Slvitcher's-lane, Arpley, on the 26th ult. A man was engaged to watch it during the night. He remained until two o'clock in the morn- ing and no one came, but on returning next morning at about ten o'clock, he saw James Green and some other nien come into the field and go to the cow. They had a conversation and presently one of the men stuck the cow. At about twelve o'clock, the defendant Massey, James Green, and some other men came to the field with a horse and cart, into which the cow was placed, and then covered. Massey went before the horse and cart towards the signal box,and looked about. He then gave the signal, and the cart proceeded along VVilion Patten- st. and took the body of the cow to the slaughter-house of Mr. Ashley, of Wash-lane. The man who was watch- ing, after he saw the cow placed into the slaughter- house, rushed to tho Town Hall, and informed Inspector Powell of the circumstances, and that officer imme- diately proceeded to Latchford and had the cow seized and examined by Dr. Peacocke, whp pronounced it to be unlit for human food, and an order for its destruction was obtained. The minimum penalty for offences of this character was £2O, or three months without hard labour. The questions the magistrates had to determine were "Was the carcase diseased ?" "Was it being pre- pared for sale ?" and "Was it intended for the food of man ?" The defendant would have to prove a clear negative to this.</p>
        <p>John Molyneux, who had watched the removal of the cow, corroborated the Town Clerk's statement in this portion of the case. Inspector Powell said that he accompanied the last witness to the field in Arpley, on the 26th ult. They found the cow lying by the side of a dry pit. Witness could see that she was diseased, and she breathed so heavily that he could hear her across the field. She was also unable to stand up. When the last witness told him that the cow had been taken to Wash-lane, ha went there, and found the body hanging with the hide almost pulled off, and the butcher had almost finished dressing it. On examina- tion, witness said that he should seize the carcase, as it was suffering from tuberculosis. Massey then came to him and said that the cow belonged to him, as he bad paid £2 for it to James Green, as a sound ceiv. The car- cace was afterwards medically examined and destroyed. —ln cross-examination witness said that he thought Massey had been led into the trick. He had told him (witness) that he had intended to have had the carcase examined before selling it. He also gave all assistance necessary. The carcase weighed 5991b5., including offal. .</p>
        <p>John Ashley, licensee of a slaughter house in Wash- lane, gave evidence, after which Dr. Peacocke gave the result of his examination of the beast. He said the flesh was very flabby and soft,and he could stick his finger through any portionof the buttocks On examining the lungs lie found that they were covered with tuberculosis matter. The meat was decidedly un- wholosome.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lees contended that his client had been sold in his bargain, and to show the honesty of his intentions, had intended to have called in the Inspector to examine it..</p>
        <p>Alderman Harrison in giving tlie decision of the magis: trates, said that they thought there had been great in- difference or negligence in the matter. Mr. Massey should have been more careful in buying the beast. They would fine him £5 including costs._ _</p>
        <p>Arising out of the above case, James Green, Lovely- lane,was charged with aiding and abetting in the sale of food unfit for human consumption, and also with cruelly illtreating a cow on July 27.—Mr. Arthur Browne repre- sented defendant. Similar evidence was given as in the last case as to the sale of the cow to Massey.—James Ellis said that he saw the cow lying in a pit for several days, and on Thursday, July 27, it appeared to have been dragged out by someone. There was no food or water about.—lnspector Dowty, R.S P.C.A., said that he saw the cow en July 25th lying in the pit. It was breathing heavily and unable to get up. Witness ascer- tained that it belonged to Green, who said that he had never given it anything to drink, and that there was nothing the matter with it but a broken leg. Be killed the cow eventually,and witness advised him to take it to Toole's or Robinson's,or bury it,as it was unfit for human food,—Arthur Lunt and John Molyneux, having given evidence for the prosecution, Mr Browne said that his client sold the cow for rendering down for oil, and not for human consumption, and also denied any cruelty. Defendant was fined Ll and costs for cruelty, but the charge of aiding and abetting in the sale of food was dismissed.</p>
        <p>---.y_- CHARGE AGAINST A FISH DEALER.</p>
        <p>ANOTIIER SEIZURE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court on Thursday, before Captain Ridgway (in the chair), and Coun - cillor James Fairelough,James Marr and Mary Marr, wholesale fish dealers, St. Helens and Warrington, were charged with exposing for sale on the 4:h August,3ls!bs. of fish which was unfit for human food. Mr. J. Lyon • Whittle (town clerk), prosecuted, and Mr. Riley, solici- tor, St. Helens, defended. Mr. Whittle said Inspector Powell went into the War- rington market on the day in question and found the 'female defendant in possession of nine or ten boxes of fish, three of the boxes containing hake. One of the boxes of hake was opened and the other two boxes under- Death. Mrs. Marr was selling fish from one of the other boxes of fish. Inspector Powell seized the three boxes of hake and had them conveyed to the rown Hall. Dr. Peacocke examined the fish and cermfied that it was un- fit for human food. Inspector Powell corroborated the Town Clerk's state- ment, and said when he asled the female defendant why her husband had not eyamined the flit), she said he had examined it and said it WaS good enough. Toe labels om the boxes were aderessed to Mr. Marr, fishmonger, St. Helens, and the DOXPB bad come from Milford Haven. Dr. peaoncie said that on the 4th August he examined the fish atld o'clock at the Town Hall, and found them in an aavanced state of decomposition. They were cored with a whole swarm of blue bottle ilia, and s,..elled very badly. They were unfit for food. Mr. Riley, for the defence said, the fish arrived at the St. Helens station the morning the fish were seized. He would prove that the fish sent to Warrington were trans- ferred from the fish truck at St. Helens station, tp a cart which bronght them to Warrington the same morning, and that the fish had just been lifted from the cart at the market when the Inspector seized them. The de- fendant sold fish on commission aud had always given , satisfaction to his customers. The total value of the fish seized would be only CI, so that it was incredible that he would hazard his reputation for the sake of the coin• mission which would be one shilling. Mr. Riley also pointed out that none of the fish had been sold and that Mrs.Marr had not had time to examine it properly. Thomas , Reardon and Thomas Clare deposed to meeting the fish train at St. Bek n on the day in question, and loading the cart.for Warrington. Evidence was also given by Wm. Whittaker, Patrick Reardon, and Thomas Wilcock, fish dealers, Warrington. The Magistrates considered the Inspector had done his work well, as bad fish would be the means of spreading disease in the town. They were, however, of opinion that Mr. Marr had not the slightest intention to offer bad fish for sale, if he could help it, but warped him to be r more careful in future. They would dismiss the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>LAMBETII RATES: 12,000 SUM MONSES ISSUED.</head>
        <p>LAMBETII RATES: 12,000 SUM MONSES ISSUED.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Martin and Mr. Langdown, members of the executive of the Lambeth Ratepayers' Association, waited on the church wardens and overseers at the ILamleeth Vestry Ball, on Tn sday night, as a deputation Ifrom the association, to ask that persons who had been summoned for non-payment of the current rate should be allowed 14 days,instead of eiteat, in which to pay up. Last quarter the summonses reached the usual number of 7,000, but now over 12,000 summonses had been served upon defaulting ratepayers. The overseers are unable to account for that sort of things, but they ac- cepted the suggestion of the deputation that many rate- payers had not got over the strain of the last rate. As Uhe result of the discussion, the overseers said they would give as much time for payment as possible, but they ideclined to allow 14 days all round, as each case must ibe considered ou its merits, and (Lev wet* adverce to making a precedent. The parish of -Lambeth is a least f..60,Cen0 in debt.</p>
        <p>" TOBACCONIST!? Commencing " Illustrated guide (post free) TOBACCONISTS' OVTP:TTING Co.-166. Busti.sn road, Loudon, 1666.-46114.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>IBE EXA.N INER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 19, 1893.</head>
