      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>Tr[E DURHAM MIRACLE.</head>
        <p>Tr[E DURHAM MIRACLE.</p>
        <p>INDEPENDENT INVESTIGATION BY A</p>
        <p>JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Belief in the marvellous was never my strong point, I and it was with feelings something akin to scepticism , that I this week set out in a professional capacity to investigate the following case. I was commissioned to call on Mrs. Twiddy,the wife of a Durham miner, living at 1, Richardson's Row, Sunniside, Durham. When I nearing the village, which is two miles from a railway I station, I chanced upon a collier leaving his work and , bending his steps homeward. Not knowing the exact Whereabouts of my destination, I questioned him, and ' received the cheery informatioa that Sunniside was close at hand. Hearing that I was about to visit and ascertain the history of Mrs. Twiddy, he confirmed the report which had already reached me. He said," If ever a miracle was performed it was performed on her. " She was, " he went on, " watched for dying more than once, and there was not a soul in the place ever expected she would get better." He went on to say that MTS. Twiddy's case was the talk of the place. I began by this time to feel deeply interested, and realised that my errand to Sunniside was hardly the wild goose chase I had anticipated, and it was with sensations of wonderment that I directed my steps to the door, when a woman, apparently about 33 years of age, tripped lightly towards the door, and cut short my apologies by saying, "lam Mrs. Twiddy. I don't look very much like an invalid do IP" Indeed she looked anything but an invalid. Fresh from the wash-tub, with her sleeves rolled up to the elbows, she seemed a picture of healthy sturdy womanhood. She commenced, in reply to inquir- ies, to tell me of her wonderful deliverance from death's door. "I owe my life to Dr. William's Pink Pills for Pale People, and I thank God they were ever brought to England." She said : "Thirteen years ago I came to Sunniside from my home in Norfelk,healthy and etrong. I didn't at that time know what sickness was, having rarely, if ever, ailed anything. I was married at that time. Shortly after my marriage I was watching over the deathbed of a relative, and the end came as I watched. Iliad not seen anyone die before, and my system received a dreadful shock. At the time I felt an alarming pain at my heart, and from that time to the time when I commenced taking Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, just over three months ago, I was troubled with an affection of the heart. At times my condition was dreadful. My heart seemed to stop beating, and I have lain for weeks at a time helplessly weak and ex- hausted. During all those years my complaint got no better. Sometimes I could follow my household duties, but the slightest excitement would bring on palpitation of the heart and other alarming symptoms. I took every kind of patent medicine, but they at most only brought temporary relief. Often enough they left me worse. The climax came in October of last year. I could go no longer, and was obliged to take to bed. A first-class doctor and his assistant attended me, and notwithstand- ing their kindness and close attention, I gradually be- came worse. I lost almost all my flesh, and was hardly anything but skin and bone. I had an all-gone sensa- tion at the pit of the stomach that nothing would satisfy. But this was not the worst. Every now and then I suffered terribly at my heart. I used to think that it stopped beating. It felt more like cold flagstone than anything else. When I had these bouts I used to sweat terribly, but still those about me had to do everything they knew to keep life in me. I had hot bricks, hot poultices, and hot flannels all over my body, and even then, after hours of such application, my blood didn't seem to circulate at all. My nervous system got ex- ceedingly weak, and wind on my stomach would swell me out dreadfully, and cause me no end of anguish. These attacks—and I have had them often—lett me frightfully exhausted, anal with every fresh visitation they developed in severity. When things were at the worst my youngest child, a boy sixteen mouths old, sickened and died. • The shock aggravated my illness, and the attacks I have told you of were more frequent and more severe. Often enough I was watched over for dying, and I never expected that I would get better. my husband was kind and good, and the neighbours often looked in to render what help they could, but des- pite all their efforts and the daily attendance of medical gentlemen I got worse. I could not eat or drink any- thing, I lost my voice, and I almost felt bereft of my senses. My husband got me an electric belt,. he thought it might do me good, but it never benefited me in the slightest degree. The weeks passed wearily by, and I thought I had not long to live. In one of my easier moments I chanced to open a Christian Conmonwealth—we take it ia regularly --at a page describing the case of a lady who had been cured of a disease that was apparently like my own, by taking Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. My eyes were weak, and my head went dizzy when I tried to read but with a great effort I managed to read the whole of the statement. An almost irrepressible desire to try Dr. Pink Pills came over me. I didn't say any- thing to my husband, but I sent a postal order to the Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46. Dolbora Viaduct, London, E.C., and asked thorn to send me a box. They came on Thursday, the 16;11 March laet. I com- menced taking them immediately, following the instructions on the box. On the evening of Thursday I had another terrible attack, but still continued to take the pills. With Friday came worse experiences. The pain at my. heart was shock- ingly bad, and I was completely exhausted. A liberal application of hot poultices brought me round somewhat but it was not until I had lain more dead than alive for several hours. I continued with the pills, but on the Saturday I bad in attack as bad as ever. It was the last I From that day to this I have not had any attack. I I took the pills day by day as prescribed, and every day I gathered strength. I was dreadfully weakened as you may imagine by these attacks, and besides I had hardly had a wink of sleep for months. I began to get a little sleep, my appetite and voice returned, and with a con- tinuance of the medicine, I have grown from that extremely weak condition to what you see me to-day. I feel better now than I have done for years,and since March when the cure was effected, I have increased more than two stone in weight. I weigh nearly eleven stone now and do the household work for a family of eight. I have had a hard struggle for life, and I owe it entirely to Dr. Williams Pink Pills.'' When she had finished this con- cise and intelligent statement of her case, Mrs. Twiddy, Iputting her hand into her pocket, and drawing out a small wooden case, asked me, " Slave you ever seen the I pills I confessed I had not, and she immediately uncovered the case and showed it nearly full of small pink pills. She always carried them about with her she said, and felt sort of affection for the medicine that had done so much for her. She was devoutly thankful at her recovery, and felt it her duty to tell of her wonderful deliverance. People had been to see her, she said, from far and near, and already others in the locality had, on 'her recommenda- tion, commenced to take Pink Pills with satisfactory re suits. Before Mrs. Twiddy had finished telling me her story, a neighbour came into her room, and, at the end of the recital,she gave her testimony to the truth of what Iliad been hearing. Bdore I turned my back upon Sunniside, I called upon a local tradesman, and asked him of Mrs. Twiddy's wonderful restoration to health and strength. He corroborated the story, and tI walked away, to say the least astonished at the result of my mission. I came to the conclusion that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, are a marvellous medicine, and that through their agency Mrs, Twiddy had been restored from a condition of suffering and imminent dan- ger, to health and strength. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills far Pale People are recognised as one of lbe greatest modern medicines—a perfect blood builder and nerve restorer,curing such diseases as Rheu- matism, Neuralgia, Partial Paralysis, Locomotor Ataxy, St. Vitus' Dance, Nervous Headache, Nervous Prostra- tion and the tired feeling resulting therefrom, diseases depending upon Humours in the Blood, such as Scro- fula, Chronic Erysipelas, &amp;o. Dr. Williams' Pink pills restore pale and sallow complexions to the glow of health, and are a specific for all the tioubles peculiar to the female sex, while in the case of man they effect a radical cure in all cases arising from mental worry,over work, or excesses of whatever nature.</p>
        <p>These Pills are manufactured by the Dr. Willis ne Medicine Company, of 4e, Holborn Viaduct, London, ELliand (and of Brockville, Ontario, Canada, and</p>
        <p>Schenectady, New York), and are sold only in boxes bearing the firm's trade mark and wiapper at 2,1. 91 a box, or six boxes for 13s. 93, A Descriptive Pamphlet sent free by post on application. Bear in mind that Dr. Williams' Fink Pills for Pale People are never sold in bulk, or by the dozen or hundred, for any dealer who offers substitutes in this form is trying to defraud you, and should be avoided. Ask your defiler for Dr. Wil- liams' Pink Pills for Pala People,and refuse all imitations and substitutes. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills may be had of all chemists, or direct by post from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company from the above address. The price at which these pills are sold makes a course of treatment comparatively inexpensive as compared with other remedies or medi- cal treatment.—Durham Chronicle, July ZB.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>COAL TRADE CRISIS.</head>
        <p>THE COAL TRADE CRISIS.</p>
        <p>OUTRAGE BY GARDEN ROBBERS. - A shocking outrage was reported on Wednesday night at Ashton-in-Makerfield. A small farmer, seeing his produce being plundered, fired a gun into the air. when he was set upon by a gang of men, one of whom is alleged to have taken the weapon and brutally attacked him with the butt end, infl'cting serious injuries. Gar- den robberies are becoming frequent, several belng stripped nightly. House to house begging is very pre- valent.</p>
        <p>THE WBLSFI SILTATION. The situation on Thursday in the South Wales and Monmouthshire coalfields showed nsi prospect of a settlement being arrived at. In the Rhondda Valley a mass meeting, attended, it is estimated, by 15,030 strikers, was held around the Druidical Rocking Stone, where the Welsh bards recently met to declare peace throughout the kingdom. The meeting was opened with a prayer for " peace and goodwill," and then speeches and resolutions followed in quick succession. The strikers called upon all colliers who had been offered 20 percent. advance to decline to resume operations until the concession had become general • declare the "legally appointed Committee "the only body authorised to call meetings ; urged their followers to refrain from disturb- ing the peace or manifesting party feelings in their de- monstrations ; and invited the co-operation of "engine- men, stokers, and outside firemen "in demanding an ad- vance of 20 per eent. At Ebbw Vale a serious collision took place between the strikers and the colliers and other classes of workmen who are opposed to a stop- page. A large force of military and police was in the district, but did not intervene. Clubs were freely used, and one of the strikers fired a revolver, happily without fatal effect. About a dozen of the strikers were seri- ously injured, and he had to be attended by an ambu- lance staff which was in readiness. DESTITUTION AMONGST LANCASHIRE COLLIERS.</p>
        <p>It is reported that the miners and their families in Leigh district are beginning to suffer from hunger as the result of the coat stoppage. A large number of women and men are going from door to door asking for assist- ance. A soup kitchen has been started at the Drill Dail, some hundreds of persons obtaining a supply. Some have taken carts into the country districts, and return laden with vegetables. Property owners are unable to obtain their rents, as the strike pay is insufficient to provide the necessaries of life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>DISTURBANCE IN SOUTH WALES.</head>
        <p>THE DISTURBANCE IN SOUTH WALES.</p>
        <p>A Nntypridd correspondent telegraphs that several hundred strikers, armed with cudgels, marched on Wed- nesday morning to Cileli Collery, and by threats of vio- lence induced 200 colliers to leave work, and then pro- ceeded to Clydach Vale Colliery on a similar mission. Only the Aberdare Merthyr Company's pits are working in the Aberdare Valley. The manager of Ferndale Col- liery, employing 7,000 men, announces that unless the men return to-day, the horses will he withdrawn from the pits and a lockout declared. Thousands of strikers, headed by brass bands, have poured into Blaenavon. The whole of the Blaenavon Company's men are now idle, and have joined in the de- mand for a rise of 20 per cent. The attitude of the Blaenavon men was loudly applauded at a great mass meeting, while the action of the Ebbwvale workers in asking for military protection was warmly con- demned. The meeting passed a resolution pledg- ing the men to abstain from acts of violence during the struggle. THE POSITION AT ST. HELENS.—MORE WORKS</p>
        <p>STOPPING. AN ADDITIONAL 1,500 HANDS IDLE.</p>
        <p>The position at St. Helens is becoming more acute, and the meeting of the Miners' Federation in London on Monday is being looked forward to with much anxiety. The whole of the caustic plants at the St. Helens Works of the United Alkali Company, Limited, have been stopped, on account of the shortness of fuel,and on Wed- nesday nearly 1,500 hands were paid off. If the strike continues it is almost certain that the other portions of the chemical works will have to stop, and this would affect other 2,000 persons. WIDNES AND THE COAL STRIKE.</p>
        <p>On inquiry at the local works of the United Alkali Company on Wednesday a representative ascertained that the coal strike had thrown 2,000 men out of their usual employment, but only five to six hundred men were en- tirely idle. To the company's advantage and to the benefit of the men the remainder are being utilised in the repair of plant, working in shifts of a thy each ; prefer- ence is being given to married men and old servants. It is not anticipated that any change will take place up to the end of the present month. The supplies of cr)al and salt will amply suffice for that perimd, but should the colliers and their masters not have made up their differences then matters will assume a very serious as- pect in the chemical trade of Widnes. In Runcorn two hundred men have been thrown out, but the majority have obtained work on the Ship Canal. NO STRIKE IN NORTHUMBERLAND.</p>
        <p>The final result of the ballot among the .Northumber- land miners which was declared on Wednesday shows that there will be no strike in the county, the men hav- ing declared by a large majority in favour o a peaceful settlement of the present dispute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>APPEAL FOR SUPPORT.</head>
        <p>APPEAL FOR SUPPORT.</p>
        <p>The St. Helens district of the Lancashire and Cheshire Miners' Federation on Saturday issued the folluwing appeal for support : The St. Helens Miners' Lock-out. To the Unionists of the United Kingdom.</p>
        <p>An Urgent Appeal for Moral and Pecultiari Assistance. Fellow Workers,--It is with much regret that we make this urgent appeal to the various trades of the United Kingdom for your sympathy and support in our present struggle, which has been brought about by our employers wanting to reduce the already too low wages we receive by 25 per cent. This demand we consider unjust and unreasonable. There are 3,000 miners out of work in the St. Helens district. which means that a large amount of money will be required every week the men are out. Our funds are not very large, as our employers have caused us to pay over £1,500 during the last eigh- teen months in resisting private reductions. We believe that the employers are also trying to break up our great federation, so thit the battle does not only aff..ct 11 the misers, but it will affect all trades, and we appeal to all trades unionists to give us all the support they can, for if the miners are beaten it will be a great blow to the other trades. The miners have always been ready and willing to help any other trade having a dispute with their employers. We trust that you will do all in your power to help us to win this ' the greatest battle that has ever been fought between Capital and Labour in this country. (Signed) Thomas Glover Miners' Agent, St. Helens. ' The whole of the unionist miners on strike have received their first strike pay. The St. Helens Trades Council have endorsed the strike of the miners, and have recommended all trades in St. Helens to support them in their struggle.</p>
        <p>STOPPAGE OF BOTTLE WORKS AT ST. HELENS. On Saturday two or three battle houses belonging to Messrs. Nottsll and Co., St. Helens, were stopped on account of shortness of fuel. This affects about 300 men. The bottle hands, about fifty in number, will receive out of work pay during the stoppage. At Mersrs. Can- nington and Shaw's bottle works, the men are working on daily contracts, but no portion of the works has yet been stopped. MASS MEETING OF MINERS AT ST. HELENS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>MR. S. WOJDS M.P., ON THE STRUGGLE,</head>
        <p>MR. S. WOJDS M.P., ON THE STRUGGLE,</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Woods, M.P., was the principal speaker at a mass meeting of mined employed at the Sutton Heath and Lea Green Collieries, St,Helens, on Tuesday. About 2,500 persons were present.—Mr. E. Walkden, district secretary, presided, and said he believed the general opinion among miners was that they would not accept this 25 per cent, or any reduction whatever. (Cheers.) The demands of the employers were the most insulting that had ever been made, and he hoped they wouldre- main firm to the Federation, and to their families.—Mr. W. Cook proposed a resolution pledging the miners not to submit to the reduction nor listen to the masters offer of arbitration. Ho said that in 1888 he was working in the Sutton Heath Collieries, and received Ss. 21. per yard for straight work. The men had since had 40 per cent, advance, and they were now receiving 53. lid. per yard. Where was the 40 per cent. ? (A Voice " Up the spout" and laugh:, r.l—Mr. J. Britton seconded.— Mr.T. Greenall, miners' agent, supported. He said that settling this dispute by arbitration simply meant that the miners would have to submit to a sliding scale.— Mr. T. Glover, miners' agent, St. Helens,also suprorted. He said that the present was one of the greatest labour strugeles that had taken place in the country, and the , ruiners were bound to try and justify their claims. (Cheers.) The men were determined to have a living wage, and that wage was not to go any lower than at present. (Cheers.) They would sink or swim together. He hoped they would maintain their position for two months, and they would be nothing the worse for it. (Cheers.)— Mr. S. Woods, M.P., said that was the eleventh meeting he had addressed during the last eight days. Referring to the fight in the Gmse of Commons, Mr. Woods aid that the highest cultured assembly in the world required policemen to keep them in order,but 300,000 miners who had been deprived of their rights</p>
        <p>knew how to behave themselves under the most distres- sing circumstance's, and they did not need any police- men. The masters meant to get as much as they could out of the miners,and that by starving women,children, and working-men. 'they would adopt the principle of starvation to force a reduction. It was a misrepresen- tation, a delusion, and a farce to try and make the pub- lic believe that the vial trade was not paying. The main object of the employers was to strike a blow at the men's union and smash it up. He believed the men would have to.have a special fund created throughout the Midland counties, not to be touched except during a national strike. (Cheers.) If they had had such a fund ! now they would have been able to snap their fingers at the employers. (Cheers.) He believed there were hun- dreds of employers who would give way at the present and withdraw their notice if they could find an honour- able way out of the struggle. They wanted " Peace with honour," and if the masters wanted a ladder to climb down they (the Federation) would make them one at their meeting next week. (Laughter and cheers.) Durham and Northumberland, by not coming out, were hindering the cause and preventing them from gaining a victory. He urged them in future to prevent stacks of coal being made ; to prevent employers from making a strike clause in their contracts, as it was a national injury; and to prevent employers from underselling. The time would come when the land mines, and rail- ways of this cluntry would be nationalised as they were in France, Belgium, and other places.—The resolu- tion was carried unanimously, and votes of thanks con- cluded the meeting. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>VOLUNTEER PRIZE MEETING AT WIDNES RANGE.</head>
        <p>THE VOLUNTEER PRIZE MEETING AT WIDNES RANGE.</p>
        <p>The 2nd and 3rd competitions in the annual prize meeting of the let .Volunteer Battalion (P.W.V.) South Lancashire Regiment took place at the Wid- nes Range, on Saturday, Captain Buckton and Captain Appleton were present at the Range. Teams from each of the seven companies in the Battalion com- peted in the second competition, but it is to be regretted that several companies were unable to muster full teams. The donors of prizes in the second competition were Lord Lilford and Mrs. Beamont. In the volley firing the teams were to consist of 8 men from the various companies, to be commanded by a sergeant of their res- pective companies. Number of rounds, 14—seven at 530 yards, seven at 400 yards. Time, 70 seconds at each range. Position, single rank, kneeling, and also the same teams in the independent firing. Namber of rounds, seven. Distance, 300 yards. Position, two ranks,kneel- ing. The prizes were,los. to each man of the best squad in the two practices ; of be. to each man of the next best squad in the two practices ; and 2s. 6d. to each of the third best squad in the two. To the commander who displays the best fire control and maintains the best fire [discipline (not necessarily to the commander of the squad making greatest number of hits), a prize of 10s. will Ibe given ; to the next, 6s. ; to the next, 45.1 The first prize was carried off by "G " (Newton) Corn- ' pany with 125 points which is considered to be remark- ably good. They succeeded in getting every shot on the target at 300 yards and got the highest possible number of points (56) on this range. "C" Company succeeded in gaining the second with 95 points, which by the way, cannot be considered a very good second. "B" Com- pany came third with 93 points. The following are the scores of the seven competing teams with the names of the commanders. 1, "G" Company k.B men) 125 points, commander, Colour-sergeant McCoy. 2, "C " Company (8 men) 95 points, commander, Lance-sergeant Dennett. 3, "B" Company (8 men) 93 points, commander, Colour-sergeant Spencer. 4, "A" Company (8 men) 86 points, commander, Colour-sergeant Jolley. 5, "F " Company (5 men) 50 points, commander, Sergeant Betties. 6, "E" Company (6 men) 49 points, com- mander, Sergeant Wilcock. 7, "D" Company (4 men) 47 points, commander, Colour-sergeant Wood. In the commanders prize competition the judge was Sergeant-Major Sullivan and the following are his awards :-1, Colour-Sergeant McCoy, " U "company ; 2, Colour-Sergeant Jolley, " A " company ; 3, Colour- Sergeant Spencer, "B " company. The third competi- tion was for rapid individual firing,and the donors of the prizes were Captain T. E. Withington and Mr. W. Mortimer ; Ist prize £l, 2nd prize 155., 3rd prize 10s. The shooting in this competition was very good and Lance-Sergeant Tunstall " U " company, is to be con- gratulated upon his excellent shooting, he having obtained 33 points out of a possible 35. The competi- tion consisted of seven shots in 70 seconds at 300 yards, the target being a Bisley third-class and the " bull " twelve inches in diameter. Lance-Sergeant Tunstall and Corporal Bailey " G " company," tied " in the first seven rounds each, scoring 28, which necessitated their shooting seven rounds aeain when Lance-Serg- eant Tunstall scored his 33. It is thonght that his good scoring had an affect on Corporal Bailey as he was only able to score ten against his com- peer's 33. It should be mentioned that" G " company have far better facilities for practising than the War- rington men, they having the use of the Parkside range, The Warrington men, have,of course, to brave a journey to, and also a walk throuah "salubrious" Widnes before reaching their range: The following are the scores of the first five competitions :-1, Lance-Sergeant Tunstall, ' "G" Co., 33 ; 2, Corporal Bailey,"G" Co., 28 ; 3, Sergt. Russell, "A" Co., 23 ; 4, Corporai Powell, "G" Co., 22 ; 5, Colour-Sergeant Jolley, "A" Co., 19. The final com- petition for Mr. o.Alfred Woods' challenge cup was next taken. The cup is competed for for three years, when the winners of each year compete for the final posses- sion of the cup. The distances for shooting are 300 and 500 yards, and seven shots at each distance, target .111s- ley. The scores were as follower—Lance-Sergeant Bram- hall, "C" Co., 48 points ; Corporal W.Plinston, "A" Clo., 47 points ; and Colour-Sergeant Spencer, " B " Co., 24 points. Mr. Woods gave his cup to encourage shooting for Bisley,but as the officers have not seen their way to send any men to Bisley the last year or two, it could not be devoted to that object, so Mr. Woods consented to his cup being given every three years to the volunteer who made the most points in the long competition and the drill and shooting competitions combined. A consola- tion mire of silver spoons is given by Mr. Robt. Davies. Mr. James Fletcher gives his prizes for recruits. Lord Lilford and Mrs. Beainont are now the donors of the Winmarleigh volley firing competition prizes. These competitions are yet to be shot off.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>SHOCKING RAILWAY DISASTER IN WALES.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING RAILWAY DISASTER IN WALES.</p>
        <p>TWELVE PERSONS KILLED AND SIXTY INJURED. A distressing accident occurred on Saturday evening on the Taff Vale Railway, about three miles from Ponty- pridd, resulting in the loss of twelve lives and more or less serious injury to about 60 persons. The half-past four train from Cardiff was proceeding along a sharp curve near Llantrissant Junction when several carriages, by some means, went off the line, and were overturned down a steep embankment near Treforest Station. The train was full,as many were returning from their week's or fortnight's work. Besides these, there were three carriages belonging to the Cambrian Railway full of holi- day travellers. It was noticed that the train was going at a very high speed, with a view, it is b9lieved,to mak- ing up for some delay at Pontypridd. At one side of the line the hill rises to a considerable height, while at the other side the embankment slopes down for some fifty yards. Some of those in the train had just realised that some- thing unusual was about to happen. The six carriages left the line, and rolled down the embankment, turning over and over in the fall. The locomotive, however, re- mained on the railway line. The coupling must have either broken or become detached. Some other car- riages also left the line, but did not go down the em- bankinent. The following is a list of the killed :—Thomas W. Shapland, aged 27,married and three children, Llandaff, potato merchant ; John Davies, aged 26, single, Fern- dale ; Wm. Davies, 55, married, no children, wll; George Brown,3B,married,several children,Scott-at., Cardiff ; Mary 47, wife of Captain Williams, of Borth ; the child of Mary Ann Williams, of Coburn- st., Cardiff ; William Jones, 28, married, two children, Cross Inn, near Llantrissant ; Davicl Davies, 59, mar- ried, four children, butter merchant, Yystrad ; William Williams, 25, single, Ferndale ; Wm. Atwell, 25, single, hawker; Wm. Lewis, 45, quarryman, Ynyshir ; Henry Simonds, ganger, married, no children. There is a conflictof opinion as to the cause of the acci- dent. The generally received theory is that the carriages left the metals on account of the high rate of speed at which the train was travelling ; hut both the driver and the fireman state that the train was not going faster than from 35 to 38 miles an hour, and considering the weight of the train, and the fact that it had only tra- velled a mile and a quarter when the disaster occurred, this statement appears to be well supported. It should be borne in mind, too, that the line bAween Treforest and Llantrissant Junction has a gradient of one in 600, and is, therefore, almost level. In the opinion of ex- perts the accident was due to the breaking of a spring of one of the through carriages. It is alleged that this caused the body of the carriage to drop on one side fully six inches. The weight thrown on the rear wheel would, it is believed, cause the front wheel to tilt and run off I the metals. The permanent way was torn up for a con- siderable distance,in ROITIO places the rails being twisted like so much wire. Both the driver and fireman were experienced men. . . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>NARRATIVE BY A PASSENGER.</head>
        <p>NARRATIVE BY A PASSENGER.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Brace, the representative in South Wales of the Miners' Federation of Great Britain, was a pas- senger in the illfated train, and bad what may literally be pronounced a hairbreadth escape. When seen after the occurrence by a reporter, Mr. Brace, who was then bearing the outward and visible signs of the extreme peril through which ho had passed, said he was thankful that beyond a slight scratch on the back of the hand and a severe shaking he had escaped unhurt. Asked to give his own account of the terrible occurrence. Mr. Brace said : I looked into the Cambrian Railway carri- ages when the train was at the Pontypridd Station and saw that they were full up, PO I had to go on till I came to a vacant place in one of the middle carriages. Had the first carriages been only partially filled, I should undoubtedly have got into one of them. After we left Treforest, and perhaps half a mile this side of the station, and the train going at tremendous speed—we were travelling quite as fast as if it were in an express</p>
        <p>train—one of the party in the same compartment as my elf said in an alarmed manner, "I believe this trai. is g.ing (.ff the line." In a moment, we felt that the train was ,AT the metals, and I then realized that we were being carried along towards the edge of the embankment. I made frantic dforts to get out of the carriage on the opposite side, but found that the door on that side was locked. We came to a standstill, and when I sprang out I saw that the first three or four ' carriages had gone down the embankment, and were lying in a confused mass of wreckage at the bottom, the wheels of some being up in the air, and the poor unfor- tunate passengers buried beneath the ruins. There were clouds of dust in the air, and the whole scene was one of the most terrible and indescribable confusion. I never in my life witnessed a scene so appalling, and I trust I never may again. The air was rent with the shrieks of despair of women and children. I saw at a glance by the awful manner in which the woodwork of the carri- ages had been smashed up that the loss of life must be deplorable. It seemed to me that in some of the over- turned carriages the people were so wedged in that even those around them could render no assistance until the necessary appliances had been brought to the spot, and gangs of the company's workmen put on. Isaw four of the dead while I was on the spot, but I do not know how many more there may be. Some terrible torturing scenes were witnessed. There were thousands of people on the scene in an incredibly short space of time, and they, of course, lent every assistance in their power,but the carriages had literally to be smashed in with hatchets in order that the poor people might be liberated. It was plain to my mind that the carriages must have turned over three or four times on the embankment. The cause of the accident appears to me to be the break- ing of the couplings of the first carriage and the speed at which we were travelling. The uninjured passengers were brought on later by special train from Walnut Tree Bridge Station. I never before had so narrow an escape and it is only new, when I have a little time to collect my thoughts, that I fully realize what a remarkable deliverance from death I have had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday, Mr. ASQUITH said the petition signed by Irish women against the Home Rule Bill would be placed before the Queen as soon as the physical difficulty of transporting it had been surmounted.</p>
        <p>After questions had been asked with regard to the report Evicted Tenants Commission and the steps taken for the appointment of the Indian Opium Commission, Mr. GLADSTONE, replying to Mr. Everett on the currency question, said he understood that the bill introduced into the United States Senate dealing with the free coinage of silver in no way represented the policy of the United States Government, and he doubted whether any com- munication from the British Government with a view to united action on the subject would at the present moment be discreet. . . . .</p>
        <p>A number of inquiries were made referring to Welsh intermediate education, and the House then resumed consideration/of the Home Rule Bill as amended. Discussionfwas continued on Mr. Carson's new clause, which set forth that the Land Commissioners would be appointed by Her Majesty. Mr. Chamberlain strongly supported the clause, saying that under the Government proposal the Commissioners would be appointed by the representatives of the tenants, and that would be a gross injustice to the landlords. Mr. MORLEY stated, however, that he had never contemplated a state of things under which the Irish Legislature would not be able to make laws with regard to the land Mr. T. HEALy spoke for the tenants, and Mr. D. R. Plunket for the landlords. Mr. GLADSTONE frankly admitted that he did not see h•iw it would be possible ' for Parliament to pass over the three years without some further legislation respecting Irish land ; but lie held that there were abundant safeguards against revo- lutionary notions about property in land, even if the Irish Legislature indulged in them. The landlord class would do well to reckon with the fact that there would be awkward tenants among the loyal minority, for the Ulster tenantry were by no means satisfied with the land laws. It would, he considered, be to the interest of the landlords if the appointments were made by the Irish Legislature. After Mr. Balfour had spoken, the clause was rejected by a Government majority of 35. Other clauses were discussed, attd the House adjourned at ten minutes past twelve o'clock.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY. In the House of Commons on Tuesday Mr. GLAnsToxx replying to questions, said it would not be to the public interest to put an export duty on coal, that there was nothing to justify the statement that the Welsh Suspen- sory l3ill had been abmdoned, and that the substance of the private telegram he had received as to the result of the Behring Sea inquiry was that the award was en- tirely satisfactory to the British case. The House then further considered the Home Rule Bill. Mr. Kimaxa proposed a new clause to the effect that if the Irish Legislature passed an Act that went be- yond their powers the Imperial Parliament might sus- pend the Act. Mr. GLADSTONE pointed out that the Home Rule Bill provided for judical procedure of a most impartial character, and he said it would be a mistake to take away any Act from the judical authority and hand it over to the two political parties in the British Parliament. The hon. member evidently decided to turn the law inside out.—Mr. BALFOUR, Mr. Chamberlain,and Mr. Sexton spoke on the clause. The latter said Mr. Kimber was seeking to revive POYNINGS' Law, but in a more objectionable form than that given to it in Ireland four centuries ago. The clause was negatived without a division. A new clause moved by Mr. G. BALFOUR, that no writ should issue from any Irish court for ser- vice in England, was rejected by a Government majority of 51. Sir A. BOLLIT, the Conservative member for South Islington, declined to vote for the amendment, saying that it would put Ireland in a worse position with regard to the assertion of her rights than any foreign country. This concluded the consideration of the proposed new clauses. Mr. flEfin.son then moved that the preamble of the bill should be omitted. Mr. GLADSTONE said the right lion, gentleman had made a third-reading speech on his motion to exclude the pre- amble. That was a very unusual proceeding ; certainly no speech had been delivered in similar circumstances since 1832. In the opinion of the Government the bill carried out the conditions they had laid down. On a division the preamble was carried out by a Government majority of 45. Mr. BALZIEL'S amendment against the creation of a Second Chamber was rejected by a majority of 83. The House adjourned at a quarter past twelve o'cleck. WEDNESDAY. In the House of Commons on Wednesday,consideration of the Home Rule Bill as amended was resumed. Mr. AMBROSE moved that the words" for the peace, order, and good government of Ireland " should be expunged from the second clause, and that power should be given to the Irish Legislature simply in all matters that related to local government. The amendment was negatived without a division. Mr. John MORLEY moved the inser- tion of words in the third clause prohibiting the Irish Legislature from making any law authorising the carry- ing or using of arms for military purposes or the forma- tion of associations for drill or practice in the use of arms for military purposes. The new sub-section was agreed to. Mr. BARTON moved that the Irish Legislature should should not have power to make any law with regard to criminal procedure. Mr. ASQUITH characterised the pro- posal as a grotesque one,and it was negatived without a decision. A long discussion took place on an amendment moved by Sir John GORST that the question of the hours and conditions of labour in factories, workshops, and mines should be dealt with by the Imperial Parliament. Mr. John MORLEY said English workpeople had never found the Irish members hostile to their industrial in- terests, and he could not accept the amendment. Mr. Ileentiny, Sir. S. F. Powell, and Sir Henry James were anxious for the Lancashire operative's sake that the amendment should be adopted, believing that the action of the Irish Legislature, presuming they lengthened the hours of labour, would be inimical to English trade. On the other hand, several hon. members, including Mr. Wolff and Mr. Oldroyd. did not apprehend that the Irish Legislature would attempt to increase the hours of labour, or that the shipbuilding, woollen, and cotton trades would suffer as some anticipated. Mr. BALFOuR nevertheless thought the sympathies of the Irish mem- bers would be with the Irish industries. The amendment was rejected by a majority of 45. Mr. PnovAen's Shop Hours Hours Act Amendment Bill was read a third time, and the House adjourned at twenty-five minutes to six o'clock. THURSDAY. , In the House of Commons on Thursday many ques- tions were asked. Replying to one with regard to the recent manceuvres at Aldershot, Mr. CAMPBELL-BANNER- MAN said Sir Evelyn Wood stated in his report that he took all precautions to save the volunteers from fatigue. The extreme distance marched by any of the prior to the operations was five and a half miles. The day was very hot. Eight men were admitted to the hospital. and a volunteer, wh. was nearly 60 years old died. The SECRETARY for WAR could hardly see his way to issue general instructions that troops should not be exercised on these days of great heat. Mr. DARLING asked, amid great laughter, if it was not the practice of the Duke of Cambriege on such days to protect himself with a parasol. Mr. fthenurtv asked several questions with regard to the attitude of the War Office towards private inventors and whether at the present moment cordite was being manufactured secretly, though the patent had been as- signed for use to a foreign manufacturer. MT.CAMPIIELL- BANNERMAN, in the course of his explanation, drnied that the manufacture of cordite had been kept a secret. Mr. BANBURY said he proposed to revert to the subject on the Estimates. Sir HENRY ROSCOE asked a question with the oLject of urging tnat every effort should be made to suppress cholera at Indian fairs and at the Mecca pilgrimage. Mr. ASQUITH said there was no intention to release Dr. Gallagher, who was in thorough health. Mr. R. COOTIE took the oath and his seat for Hereford. The Reuse resumed consideratio of the Home Rule Bill.</p>
