        <p>strength. A copy - will be se:it , FREEgratis and post paid, by Guy s, . Tonle Company, (0 Department), 6, Sloane Square, London, S.W.</p>
        <p>WATCHING A DOG IN BATTLE. A writer in the Contributors' Club, in the August " Atlantic," describes vividly a dog in battle :—The deepening danger grew tragically intense, for there was nothing to be done except to stay and confront it. At last to the manifest reliet of the long, long line, the orders were to advance ; and as we stepped forward somewhat blithely, we noticed, moving directly in front of us, a little more rapidly than ourselves, a small body of glittering cavalry escorting a group of gorgeously appointed staff officers. We also noticed that they had a most enthusiastic companion. A large dog, with shaggy, jet-black, curly hair, and most musical voice, followed the calvacade closely, barking sonorously, as though he left his duty to be akin to that of the bugler, His superb, waving tail moved high in air in rhythmic cadence with the marching men who were behind him. rather than in time to the clattering hoofs and chaos of sound which preceded him. The awful peril of our situa- tion did not prevent us from wondering how the noble beast had managed to get there, and what would be his fate if he remained. We could not learn, even, whether he belonged to any of those who were actors in the fear- ful drama before us ; but presumably this was the case, for there was that in his movements betokening a dog who felt himself to be at home, rather than the vagrant canine who bad run away whither he had no business to go. Still on and on, as the brigade advanced, went the delighted dog, occasionally raising his head yet higher, as if for the purpose of throwing his voice farther, while his ever swaying tail responded to the cadenced step of the dear dog's fellow countrymen. flow he loved the r eer i nature d fairlya c scene ! I have often been charmed with the delight going showno bn y; their whenm o t emotional eolri otherng in frolic move- ment of any kind. The least sociable dog is always ready for a run or a romp with the least congenial com- panion, and there is no being to whom the infection of i gaiety s 'so generously contagious. All through the desolate two hours occupied by this engagement, the first of the war, our friend still pur- sued his delighted tactics—an example of glowing fearlessness. " Why," exclaimed a soldier, " he's bet- ter than a brass band to keep our spirits up ! " We were all young then, the group surveying this ecstatic dog, and much nearer to college and classic phraseology than most of us have ever been since : and so, when a recent graduate cried, " Well done, Gaudia Certanainis ! " tin) refrain was taken up by all who could translate it, an d by a great many eho could not, and Gaudia Certarninf s the deg remained throughout his martial career. Later on, as one by one the various regiments were drawn off from a field now deemed impracticable, we could see our handsome friend holding his gi o d t ming the very last, until, in the deepening twilight, he was observed majestically striding after his cavalry escc.rt as we now called it, on their way to the rear. Ali, then that sway- ing fad, which an hour before had moved in joyous unison with the soldiery, had fallen to a discontented level, as the poor dog bewilderedly retraced his footsteps following whatever master among the glittering staff might lay claim to his prowess and his fidelity. All the succeeding night we were kept awake by the fierce whistle of trains bearing what we only too well knew were reinforcements from Richmond. When at last, at daybreak, we stood on that vast plateau where we were to see by far the greatest battle yet fought on this con, tinent, we instinctively looked for our fourfooted corn- ' rade, G udia ; for somehow we were well assured that he must be with us, taking his share of peril, as beseemed a noble knight. At dark of that day of mon- strous calamity the warring hosts had separated, and, for some reason which will never be known, the legions of the North were in full retreat, having been victorious all through the day ! Terrified teamsters, homesick militiamen, panic-stricken mules, and stragglers of every sort had started the stampede at the rear long b 'fore the fighting in our front had ceased. The German reserves stationed at Centreville, five miles behind us, were in full retreat an hour previous to the last charge of the 79th Highlanders. As our discouraged, despairing soldiery staggered in retreat towards the stone bridges. walking as though wading through seas of clotted blood one of the last scenes that fell upon our disturbed vision on passing through Centreville, was that of cur dear Haudia, returning from the field,-with a small red rill of blood trickling from his neck. He probably did not live to go much farther ; and associated as he was in our minds with the army when advancing, it seemed to us well if he did not survive to witness that retreat which brought mourning into nearly every house in the land.</p>
        <p>CONVICT LABOUR IN AMERICA, THE LAND OF THE FREE ! The San Francisco " Argonaut" has some remarks upon the subject of convict labour in the United States suggested by recent outbreaks of leased convicts in the South. The practice of leasing convicts is, it says, not peculiar to the South. It is used in Colorada and one or two Northern States. The principle of the thing is this —rural taxpayers object to bear the expense of peni- tentaries, and insist that they shall be self-supporting. But when an attempt is made to employ convicts in the manufacture Of an article which can be sold, the labour unions ri ;e in wrath and declare that criminals are tak- ing the !.read cut of the mouths of honest men. To pacify these ignorant demagogues, prison authorities lease a block of convicts to a contractor who is exploit- ing a quarry or a mine. He pays for the use of them just what it costs the State to feed, clothe, house, and guard them. But the plan involves two drawbacks. It does not suit the labour unions,which still complain that the convicts lake work that would otherwise fall to honest men ; and it is certain to involve cruelty in the management of the convict labourers. No man of de- cently humane instinct will take a position as an over- seer of a convict gang; the job is certain to fall to a maim, mutilate, and kill his Lweogrrke,,er,s .wkhnoo will several examples of the working of the system. It in- stincts with iing nwti tihlyiap.t hTen ecaSno uitand luilagse itse r operieosue ns ahen gangtlieo fßci ocnh ni tn ba udreDras nwv rere eagoi !-. ri)oi transpiresoaydedw o a sn ttbhbeua tgrading; the contractor worked or beat several of them to death, and, of course, was never called to account. Twenty-five years ago, the convicts of the State of Georgia, make and female, were leased to a firm of contractors who utilised them in the cotton fields. This firm subsequently sublet a portion of the prison supply to other consumers of labour, among others to ex-Senator Brown, who is said to have made out of the enterprise the 50,000 dollars he gave to the Baptist Seminary. Complaints of abuses having arisen, a Legislative committee was appointed to investigate. The Chairman, Colonel Alston, reported that men and women were huddled together like brutes, that the fe- males were at the mercy of the males, as the birth of twenty-five nameless children in the prisons probed; that all were whipped and otherwise treated with savage cruelty. For signing this report, Colonel ;Alston was shot dead in the State capitol at Milledgeville. The story reads like an episode of the old slavery days. Then the Leeislature passed a law dividing the sexes, and leased the women to the owner of the Maddox farm, on the Savannah River. Here they raise cotton. When a female convict is lazy she is stripped to the waist, bound to a post,and lashed until she promises to work faithfully hereafter. As in the old days, Maddox keeps a pack of bloodhounds to hunt runaways. It is not surprising that. fire of the women lately escaped, and when they were tracked foug,ht desperately with the men and dogs, and work, and common . ) credit n the other sixty female convicts threatened to rise in a body if they were punished. This can hardly be said to be a sueces however, dnonnvpliurcoststtpl ite sicoi pukisei npa State et ao ta r ors t-c t r gc d mi tno Pri- soners,soenlig enlightened sense suggests that they should work at some gainful labourf the tent iar yu oyns tge IT a tt oc tna eptasy ce or sm. p eTt eh withom i branch of labour, so as to minimise the cost of the peni- pi ol na ienstt man in efithnrseotufigebileidetaoolficsaveuntssiepl elaroeyr enadlreuercinttei oxi,sne r;r:foattwaeanngtist 01 iennde at nti yn eat,: atcercntmi tevi uon i lcat branch ; end, second, because labouring men have no- , right to doom convicts to idleneall or to impose the cost of their maintenance on the body of taxpayers. holl, 8 vague apprehension that their own monopoly or labour may be disturbed. Everybody has a natural right to labour, even a convict. The " Argonaut suggests road making as a suitable employment for convicts, against which it thinks that not even- the most rabid " labour union could complain. The objection to the plan is that a certain proportion of the convicts wot2d be apt to escape. So they would, says the writer, who adds— "lf t be warders employed guards who were in the habit of shooting straight the proportion of the runaways who made good their escape would be small. And that would be another misfortune against which society would bear up. When a man once gets into the peni- tentiary not the worst use to make of him is to put him cut of the way."</p>
