        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>MATURDAY. AUGUST 19, 1893.</p>
        <p>TIME are, as yet, no signs of The strike. a settlement of the coal strike. Reports'from various directions in- dicate that there is DO abatement of the determina- tion of the miners not to submit to the large re- duction that is demanded of them; and,on the other hand,the coal-owners—the largo coal-owners at all events—do not show any disposition to withdraw from the position they have taken up. The strike has extended rapidly in .South Wales, but in the Nerth the miners of Durham and Northumber- land have now definitely declined to follow the directions of the Federation and "come out " for higher wages. This action, though by no means unexpectO, will naturally he a great disappoint- ment to the leaders of the Federation and it will probably lead to the expulsion of the northern miners from the great organization to which they so lately asked for admission. At the same time, , even the poll which has reEelted adversely to the policy of the Federation shows -chat rapid strides Federation principles have made la the North of late. Not many months ago the miners association of Northumberland stood in proud isolatitq with apparently no intention of joining the Federation, yet a rapid conversion followed amongst the rank and file and, to a great extent, in spite of the orOesition of the officials of the association. At the present time there are several circumstances which render a strike particularly unpalatable in that district, yet the poll that has been taken this week gives, it is saii, only a small majority against obeying the behests of the Federation. It is now not at all unlikely that this latter, body will attempt the for- mation of a rival organizations in the two northern counties, as it seams to be assured of a large num- ber of enthasiastic adherents there. In that eventt!i 3 strike may lead to a consoli- dation and strengthening of the Federation that has not been calculated upon by its oppo- nents. It is probable, too, that the strike will, in other respects, be fruitful of important issues. It is almost certain, for instance, to quicken the efforts of those who advocate the settlement of industrial disputes by some other moans than the disastrous afbitrament of a strike. If anything useful be effected in that direction, it may well be said that a permanent good has resulted from a temporary evil. And, then, again, we notice that much is being said at meetings of the workraem about the aeoumulation of stocks at the pits. It is openly asserted that one of the objects of the master's in bringing about the strike is to get rid of these lar,i stocks of coal, at "famine " prices. If the coal-owners have been influenced to any considerable extent by such a motive, their conduct is, to put it very mildly, not deserving of commendation. Apart altogether from its effect upon those most immediately concerned, an ex- tensive strike in the coal trade disarranges all the industries of the nation to such an extent that it should not be at all dependent upon a scheme for " rigging the market," no matter how smart this scheme may seem from a commercial point of view. If the accumulation of a stock on the pit bank is lit :celptit..ioc.. ;c .2 to</p>
        <p>means or another, a cessation of labour, it to be hoped that the miners when they rettlrn-r work will be in a position strong enough t°' vent these accumulations.</p>
