        <p>Sr. JOHN'S RAND OF HOPE Ruxylem On Tuesday afternoon, the members and friends of this society held their anneal excursion to Merrey View, Overton Hills. Al! thoroughly enjoyed the outing. RUNCORN NATIONAL SCHOOL. The annual Sunday School treat took place on Wed- nesday, when the children were conveyed to Mersey View, Overton, and had a most enjoyable time. CO-OPERATAVE TRIP FROM RUNCORN The annual excursion in connection with Runcorn Co-operative Society took place on Monday, when about 800 persons travelled in two special trains to Llandudno The weather was charming and the excursionists had a pleasant day. SUDDEN DEATH AT WIDNES. About half-past four o'clock on Thursday morning, David Hughes, aged 40 years, bricklayer, of John-st., Widnes, died very suddenly. lie had been a strong healthy man all his life, never requiring the attendance of a doctor, and on Wednesday night he went to bed in his usual health. At four o'clock his wife was ins pain and awoke him, and then appeared all right. Half-an- hour later she heard him moaning, and he died before she could summon help. Dr. Hutchinson, who was called in, gave the opinion that death was due to heart disease. ELLESMERE-STREET SCHOOL, RUNCORN. On Wednesday the teachers and elder acholars of the above school had a trip in waggonettes, supplied by Messrs. Yarwood Brothers, to Chester and Eaton Hall. After having spent a pleasant day, the party made their homeward journey, arriving at Runcorn shortly after 10 o'clock. They then adjourned to the schnolrOmm, where an interesting presentation tlok place, in the form of a beautiful marble clock, to the Nev. James Martin, on his leaving the town for Market Drayton, to resume his ministerial duties at that place. Am, HOUSEHOLD LINEN, hemme I and market, free of charge. —J. and W. Durro C. Sankey et.-.-Advf. •</p>
        <p>THE LUCKY NUMBER. On Tuesday evening an appropriation meeting in connection with the 3ed Warrington Starr-Bowkett Building Society. was held at the office, 14, Cairo- street, under the presidency. of Mr. B. Aukland. Messrs. Stout and Perrins were appointed scru- tineers, and a by nam2d Gleave :drew the ballot. No. 22 wag drawn, which entitle-d Mr. D. Fanshaw to an appropriation of £4OO on the usual terms. A vote of thanks to the Chairman and scrutineers closed the proceedings. NO REASONABLE OFFER REFUSED, if made at once at MAIM a ihirniture Warehouse, Bridge Foot, Warrington. See fuller Adv., page 4dvl. SUCCESSES Ca' A.K EARLESTOWN SCHOLARSHIP</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>STUDENT.</head>
        <p>STUDENT.</p>
        <p>In the statement of results of the Sessional exam- ination held in connection with the Manchester Technical School, we find that 111..1 11. Harrison who gained. the Camay Council scholarship stands sixth-in order of merit out of 37, first year students in mechanical and electrical engineering. Out of on aggregate of 1,600 marks he has gained 1,105, distributed in the following subjects :—Applied Mechanics 92, steam 92, physics 70, German 60, ecclid 36, practical physics 53, physics ii, 76, tris gonometry 28, theoretical chemistry 79, geometry 83, machine drawing 89, moulding 65, algebra 40, wood-working 77, practical chemistry 70, practical engineering 95. THE THORNHILL COLLIEBY DISASTER. A concert was given in the National Schools, Church-street, on Tuesday night,hy the Bank-street Mission Amateur Minstrels, in aid of the relief fund of the survivore, and the widows and orphans of the colliers who were killed in the recent colliery dis- aster at Thornhill, Dewsbury. There was a very fair attendance The " darkies '' gave a very credit- able display of their vocal and humorous talents, in a miscellaneous programme. The following were artistes :—J. W. Taylor, W. Cartwright, E. Butter- worth, P. 11. Simkin, T. Hayes, G. Caldwell, J. Wrench, W. Gray, A. Pemberton, Bancroft, Daintith A. Simkin, A. Jowett, Ogden, A. Fosster. The pro- gramme concluded with a humourous sketch entitled " Blinks and Jinks " in which the follow- ing characters took part :—Snow, W. Bancroft ! Cloe (Scow's wife), te. Simkin Blinks, W. (iray ; Jinks, P. H. Simkin. PRECOCIOUS MENDICANCY. The schoolboy vocabulary "scrumping" is indica- tive of high jinks and many apples,and other things* sometimes including a whipping. To the owner of an orchard it means quite another thing. This, Mr. Johnson, the landlord of the Fiddlers Ferry Inn, will have -discovered. He possesses an orchard which has been particularly wealthy in its crop of fruit this season. On Saturday night the fruit was collected—but not by the owner; not even by his instructions in fact, Some unknown persons en- tered the garden in the dead of night and stripped the trees bare and made good their escape. They left no trace save that of a greasy apron, and there is no doubt that if they come back to claim this— which is not an impossible event in these days of precocious mendicancy—they will arouse consider- able suspicion. which will not be good for them. The rsquired persons are alleged to be youthful tramps. UMBRELLAS RE-C)VERED.—J. and W. DUTTON, Sankey-St., Warriugton.—Adrt. MESSRS. MCCORQUODALB AND CO., AND reams WORKMEN. It will be remembered that about a fortnight ago Mr.Satnuel Woods M.P.raised a question in the House of CoMmons in relation to Messrs. McCorquodale and Co, who do Government printing, refusing to employ Union men, or to allow their employes at their works at Newton to become members of a Trades Union. The matter was fully discussed,and as a result of this, on Saturday at noon Mr. A. C. McCorquoiale called the compositors together and informed them that he had been instructed by his co-directors to notify them that in future no objec- tion would be raised to their joining the Union if they so desired, and that no distinction would be made between Union aril non-Union men. The An- nouncement was received with evident satisfaction. The firm at present pay Union prices, but the differences arose on the question of payment for overtime, which will no doubt be settled on a satis- factory basis. ALL HOUSEHOLD LINEN, hemmed and marked, free of charge.—J. and W. DUTToN, Sankey-Bt.—Advt. SMALLPDX RETURNS. Dr. Tatham, the medical officer of health for Man- chester, in his quarterly return, just issued, says : " The 215 reported attacks by smallpox last quarter were equal to an annual rate of 1.67 per 1,000 of the city population, a rate which is higher than bas been observed in any second quarter of recent previous years. In the Manchester township the incidence of smallpox was by far the heaviest, the notified cases corresponding to a rate of 296 per 1,000 annually. In North Manchester the rate was 1.87, whilst in the South it did not 'exceed 0.75 per 1,000 of the population. The type of disease throughout the quarter continued to be very severe, many of the cases having been of the confluent or semi-confluent and some of them of the hsemorrha- gic variety ; eighteen of the attacks proved fatal. In the ten selected notification towns of Lancashire and Yorkshire, which together contain a population estimated at 1,797,908, the reported smallpox attacks last quarter numbered 493, and were equal to an annual rate of 1.10 per 1,000 of the aggregate population. Of the individual towns, Warrington was again most severely affected, the attacks there being equal to 5.16 per 1,000. In Preston the rate did not exceed 004. In London 1,459 cases were notified. correeponding to an annual rate of 140 pe 1,000 persons living." ALL DOUSEHOLD LlNgit, hemmed and marked, free of charge —J. and W. DUTTON, Sankey-st.—Adet. DISPUTE BETWEEN A FARAIBIt AND HAY- DEALER. At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes, William Hankey, Cross Farm; Appleton, sued William Par- , kinson, hay dealer, Bank•street, Warringtoon, for ' .£7 ss. for a quantity of hay purchased by defend- ant, a portion of which had been delivered.—Mr. Jenkins who appeared for plaintiff said that on June 30th, Parkinson made a bargain with plaintiff for a quantity of hay at .£5 per ton, which was to be de- livered in a fortnight. A load was taken to the de- fendant's place of bu-iness,and on it being unloaded he complained of it being wet, and said there would have to be a reduction for that reason, and plaintiff denied that the hay was wet, and that only the bands had been damped to make t hem twist. There had been 39 eyrie.. delivered. He did not send any more. William Hankey was then called and said that the hay was musty, and was sold for musty hay. He howevOr denied that any water had been put upon the hay. A large number of witnesses were called to corroborate this statement.—Mr. Browne for the defence said that his case was that the hay was wet, and not as it was bargained for. Ile had also a counter claim for .E 2 Ils, for loss ow- to hove to buy other hay in the place of the hay which was not delivered.—Parkinson said that he had suffered this loss, as the price rose after the bargain had been struck with liankey,ancl lie had to pay extra for a fresh lot, The hay which was de- livered WR3 wet in the inside of the bundles.— After a large number of witnesses had been called to prove that the hay was wet when delivered, the judge in siimmingnp said that the case had taken an unreasonable time. Ile would give judgment for the plaintiff for $6 5e., and the defendant for £2 Ur.</p>
      </div>
