      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>E EX A MINER-- SATURDAY, AUGUST 19 1893... HAIRDRESSERS AND SUNDIY LABOUR. I WARRINGTON TECH N ICAL INsniute.,</head>
        <p>E EX A MINER-- SATURDAY, AUGUST 19 1893... HAIRDRESSERS AND SUNDIY LABOUR. I WARRINGTON TECH N ICAL INsniute.,</p>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT RIINCOUN:</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL COILILESPNVENT.</p>
        <p>That the forthcoming election of Ruttorti Com- missioners is a frequent topic of conversation and the list of candidates continues to bring them.</p>
        <p>That Mr.lturaour is responsible,for the following: Messrs. E. Itnison, W. Mellor, T. Dean, T. Jones,•A. Hough, S. Yar wood, and G. Torrie.</p>
        <p>That there are also the retiring members :_vii:: S. Taylor,. W. Fisher, 8.. W. Humphreys andß. Abet</p>
        <p>That it is seldom sanitary defects are given as the direct cause of death, but a Bunnorn jury. on Mon- day, acting on the evidence of Dr. Robinson, re- turned u verdict to thafeffect.</p>
        <p>That one of the Jury in corrversation described the house where the death occurred, as a miserable hovel, without furniture, and that the stench which filled the Once was vile in the extreme.</p>
        <p>Thnc the terros upon which the Comnaissioners have arranged to borrow the sum necessary for the purchase of the Water works are extremely favour- able, and congratulations are extended to the Com- mittee who have negotiated the loan.</p>
        <p>That it is sincerely hoped that the- prediction will prove true, and that no increase will be made on the rates.</p>
        <p>That the epidemic of typhoid fever at Widnes so far shows no sign of abatement.</p>
        <p>That Widnes Trades Ctmncil have determined to rn n a candidate at the November nunicipal elec- tions, and have unanimously selected their secre- tary, Mr. A. G. Lowry, to contest tiimms' Cross Ward.</p>
        <p>TEST CASES. A considerable amount of interest was shown at Warrington Borough Court yesterday, in two teat cases, which were brought Wore the bench by the Warrington Hairdressers' Association. The court was crowded with spectators atitnngst whom were many representatives of " the profession." The prosecutions were conducted on behalf of the Asso- ciation by Mr. Arthur Browne. George Davies, barber, of Tanners-lane, was the first disfendant called. The magistrates' clerk said : " You are charged with having on ske•l3th day of August, within, the Borough of Warrington on the Lords day, commonlfy called Sun- day, being a hairdresser over the age of fourteen years, thatyou did nolawfully do and exercise certain worldly .labour business, and work in your ordinary calling of 'hairdresser aforesaid, upon such Lords day,. tire same 'not being work of necessity or charity." Despite the involved character of the charge,the defendant promptly pleaded guilty. Mr. A. Browne, them stated that he appeased on behalf•of the plaintifr,. May..nrnith, the secretary to the Warrington Hairdiessers) Ast,ociation. Tfie defendant had been summoned forchaving opened his shop on Sun- day last and carried on, hie calling as barber and hair dresser. He explained,. thitat aathe Balch woe no clotabt , awareran association of heirdressers had been formed I in the tow/I,4nd had' been. joined by all in the business with the exception of four men. The association had certain-objects to carry Out and one of them was to 'Secure Sunday closing. Amongst th 9 members were a. number of young men in busieesa who said : "if our neighbouring barbers open on a; Sunday we are bound toopen, otherwise our trade will gobo those who are opening." They banded themselves together for the purpose etclosing not only oiro Sunday but on a Thurs- day afternoon. Out of the tot* membership there were no fewer than twelve who hid' formerly opened on Sun- days but who closed now. It was necessary if they were to keep their business and spend Sunday as a day of rest, that they should compel odwrs to close their I establishments on Sundays: Accordingly they had ' summoned the defendant under a section of a very old , Act passed in the time cf Chmicell_ Mr. Davies had been warned that he would be prosecuted if liedid not close. Aceordingly lie closed hi f root di or and received the customers through. the side duos, a fact which he bad summoned a witness to, testify to. Last Sunday from about seven o'clock until ten there were no fewer than Id persons sfhived Itudefiortatit's shop, all of whom might t aily have been shaved:oo I lie Saturday. llt would be perhaps as well to allow it to become pub- licly known that titles persons going.i,rito the shop on a Sunday and being she ved, were liable to, becliarged with aiding an I abetting, but altlio.igh they had at hand the names of most of the customer, theqkssociation did net intend on this occaSion to take proceedings against them If, in future, barb-rs persisted in breaki.ng the law, the Aosocia.tion would, brhig both thermand Hoek customers up. Alderman Harrison : Whom do,yon say you represent, Mr. Browne Mr. Browne-:. Mr. Smith, the retwetary. Alderman Harrison : Has he a locses P—Mr. Browne : I heard you ask that yest Tday. He has got a locus staturi; sod toe summons has been signed by two magistrates. Alderman Harrison : Oh ! The defendant upon being called stated that. if he did not open his shop.on Sundays, he would have to close it al- together. He was only able to get business on Saturday afternoons and Suhdays.—Mr. Browne Nineteen pen- nies are net much to be keephig, a shop open on.— Alderman Harrison : It is curiousyou do no work dur- ing the week, and have to depend upon Sunday labour. It cannot keep. you.— The defendant : It does a lot to- wards it. Mr. Browne: The defendant upon getting his sum- mons exposed it in his shop window, and thus created considerable bad feeling amongst Trade Unionists, and in the case of the other defendant who is to come be- fore you there was some trouble caused owing to the summons being exposed. The Chief Constable You told me you wouldn't open again, Davies, on Sundays.—,Yes, I didn't- open. My assistant volunteered and he did the shaving and got the money.—Aldertuan Harrison Well then, if you give the =no' to an assistant, leow is it going ro keep you. —The defendant: Oh, this is only a test case.—Alder- man Wright : I should have been better pleased if you had fallen in with the plans of your fellow tradesmen.— Alderman Harrison : So should I. It seems to me to be a monstrous thing almost to be desecrating the Sabbath by working, when it can be avoided. It is indecorous at any time, to say the least of it. Can you promise not to open again, if the prosecution is withdrawn 2—De- fendant: Yes, Sir, I promise.—Mr. Browne agreed Of the withdrawal of the summons on the conditions pro- mised. Gregory. Murphy, barber,- Scotland-road, was sum- moned for a similar offence,—The defendant did not appear in court —Mr. Browne explained that Murphy was the person who had caused a disturbance by expos- ing his summons in the shop window. The reason for the charge avainst this defendant was similar to the other one. There were other barbers' shops in the locality and owing to defendant keeping opett on Sundays the ether tradesmen were in danger of losing their business unless they opened on the Sunday also. Last Sunday 14 persons were shaved by the defendant and amongst them were a medical assistant and other uer,ons,includ- ing.a clerk who finished work at two on Saturday alter. noon. They might all be shaved on the Saturday.— William Porlinore, barber, Golborne-street, was called and testified to seeing the number of men named corning front Murphy's shop as though they had been shaved.— The defendant was again called but upon not appearing the bench ordered a policeman to go and bring hlm, adding that lie was liable to be apprehended on a warrant.—The defendant was brought into court in a short time afterwards, and when asked why he did not appear sooner, he said that "lie was not so well." With regard to opening his place of business on Sundays. he said that he had received authority from the Chief Con- stable to open.—Mr. Hunt: "I don't remember anything , about that '—Defendant said that he had sent Inspector Spinks to the shop t woyeamago to tell him that he could keep of ea on Sunday morning until 9.30. Defendant wished to know what about. winter, as people did not get up so early then, but Mr. Hunt made no reply to that. —Mr. Hunt again said he never remembered doing st).—Mr. Browne said that the only object of the Assoc- iation was to get these men to close.—Alderman Harri- son addressing defendant said that the, magistrates were determincd as far as they could to carry out the law. In the last case berme them, the person summoned had aereed to close on Sunday. Mr. ,Browne had withdrawn the summons. They had sent for him in the hope that he (defendant) would listen to a similar reasonable pro- posal.—Defendant upon this said that be would close an Sunday to which Alderman Harrison replied sincerely hope you will."— Mr. Browne hereupon withdrew the case, and said that he would like it to be known that all persons who were shaved on Sunday .were liable to prosecuticn for aiding and abetting. • RTJSIIBEARING AT LYMM.</p>
        <p>1t19301/F9 OF THE EXAMINATIONS... The final remits of the examinations having, tow been received ware able to present a complete list of passes io all stihjectr, together with the priAw and scholarships gained by students of the School, ' of Art. The general efict is that notwithstanding the smaller wamber of studentsr in comparison with the previous year, the work date,. as shown by the exatniliations,was ow the whole as largeein quantity and better in quality. The School of Art was veryseriously affected by the out- breath of smallpox, which .kept many students away, but the results altogether compare very favourably with forme? years. Witte respect to the Science Classes, a great eleange was made-last year in the regulations of the Science. and Art Department, by whicls second class elementary passes ceased to be recognised. As the majority of the passes ware-of this grade last year, it was anticipated that the result would be a great reduc- .tion in the-number, but it 110 V, appears that, in propor- tion to thcrnumber of studirits, the school has secured , more passes than before. Segal, a; we have informa- tion the remits gained in Werniongton are above the , average.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>RITNCOW4T WARD OF COMAIIiSIONERS.</head>
        <p>RITNCOW4T WARD OF COMAIIiSIONERS.</p>
        <p>-- LOAN FOR WATERWORKS PURCHASE. A special meeting of Runcorn Improvement Board 1 of Commissioners was held at the Town 11411 On Tues- day morning, to cnnsider a recommendation of the Finance Comenittee,in reference to a tender of a loan for .869,000,f0r thepurchase of the Wilier works Co.'s undertaking. Mr. F. Wiswall piesided. There were present, Messrs. IV. Fisher, J. IV. Lightburn, R. Abel, W. Handley, W. Leach, B. Bisbrown, W. Ilan- kinson, T. Lea, J. Meadowcroft, F. A. Lake (Law Clerk). • • The Commissioners recommended " thatthe tender of the Manchester Trust, Limited, to lend a sum of £69,000 for a erind of 30 years at interest of 3i per cent, per annum, for the purchase of the undertaking and plant of the Runcorn, Weston, and Halton. Waterworks Co., and the payment of expenses incidental. thereto, at a commission all per cent., upon the Amount of the roan and eehject to the payment of a stint not exceed- ing £lOO, in respect of law casta.exclosive of out of poc- ket expenses, be accepted." Mr.Fislier moved the adoption of the recommendation. The Committee had been in correspondence with several fmancial houses in London, Manchester and Liverpool, and they had also had interviews with the representa- tives of .some of the houses. The recommendation was the outcome of careful inquiry and consideration. The Manchester Trust, Limited, had dealt with them in a very straightforward manner, and the money would be ready when it was wanted at the end of September.— Mr. Light burn seconded the motion. This was one of the largest transactions the Commissioners had had to deal with, and the Committee had gone through the whole scope of money borrowing and stock if 'ulna be- fore coming to a decision. If the Committee had adopted the common practice of repayment by instalments there would very likely be a charge of fiyepence in the £ on the rates for some few years, and posterity would have had the benefit of it. But he thought they had done a good deal for posterity, and in 60 years by the present mode, they would make a present of Runcorn Waterworks to ' posterity. The arrangement decided upon by the Committee was a ' capital dne. Besides paying the interest for the next thirty years, they would oho pay a cerain sum per annum into a sinking fund, which would be allowed to accumulate, and at the end of that period the mortgage would have to be re-paid. The sinking fund and interest would then be available, and the amount then t) be borrowed would be less than the total of the fund. At that time the Commissioners would be in a position in re-borrowing to take advantage of any reduction in the money market. He congratulated the members and the ratepayers generally, upon the very excellent arrangement. He thought it redounded to the credit of all concerned, and he spoke of the admirable manner in which Mr. Fisher had yerformed his duties. Mr. Bisbrown also offered his congratulations. At one time it was thought the purchase would pet a penny or threetialf pence on the rates, and it was now thought that would not be neceesary.—Mr. Fisher : If it comes up to our expectations, the rates will be quite clear. Mr. Handler thought the committee had tdone wisely in borrowing the money at the present rate, and he was sure they deserved the thanks of the Commissioners and the town. At the same time,he should like a little more Information given to the ratepayers than that given. They must remember that this was the biggest transac- tion ever entered into by the Beard, it was borrowing a , sum of money that far exceeded the whole rateable value of the town, and it was a very important and , momentous step, especi illy as it might lead to further expenditure that would add to the debt. Personally, he preferred the re-payment principle. He should have preferred, if they could have borrowed the money for 60 years,to be re-paid in equal instalments of principal and interest, and the debt would have lapsed at the end of that time. He could not follow Mr. Lightburn in Ids calculation that the adoption of this principle might have caused fivepence additional burden upon the rates, because he made it out if they could take the figures given at the public meeting as correct, that the net re- venue of the waterworks was about £2,600 ; and bor- rowing money for a period of 60 years at 3± per cent., the annual payment of principal and interest would haw amounted to £2,700. Mr. Fisher said they could not get the money at 31 per cent. on the instalment principle. It would have been 31 or 31 per cent. Mr. Handley said as there was no hint by the mover and seconder that additional interest would be clzarged he was justified in assuming that the Committee' had annual interest calculated the figures upon the same per centage of in- terest i percent. fwounnudldtihnavtnie taking Le 9 .00 Oc h 4. forr charge 6 or years at m alone of £2,243 103. It was important that the rate- payers should know exactly where they stood, and lie therefore asked, how mech per year could they lay by out of surplus revenue when that large amount had been paid for interest, if it was taken for granted that there would be no charge on the rates. In other words how much surplus revenue would be available for sinking fund. He understood they would be all the better for what they could put into the sinking fund, because it would bear interest at from 2f to 2i per cent.—Mr. Meadowcroft said he favoured the principle adopted by the Committee because if they borrowed the money for 60 years at or 31 they could not take any advantage of any fall in the money market. The Board were al- ready suffering from that system. They were paying tlf for money which if they hal only the power to pay it off they could borrow it at 3 or 3:k. He thought all features considered the recommendation of the Finance Committee was decidedly the best. Mr. Fisher said on the annuity system the interest at 3* would have been £2,806 per annum: It was intended to put £430 per annum into the sinking fund. Ile added that the figures had been care-fully pone into and on the instalment principle sd. in the £ would have been laid on the rates.—Mr. Handley said not according to the figures supplied to him—Mr. Fisher said the figures must be wrong —Mr. Handley said he quite saw that the Committee's arrangement was thebest. He pointed out however that that the amount being borrowed would only cover the purchase; there was no provision for further extension of the Water works. r. Mead° wcro f t said that was so. Any further provision of water would have to be met by further expenditure. 1 The recommendation was unanimously passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>LONDON AND NORTH-WESTERN RAILWAY. THE SHIP CANAL AGREEMENT.</head>
        <p>LONDON AND NORTH-WESTERN RAILWAY. THE SHIP CANAL AGREEMENT.</p>
        <p>- --- The half-yearly meeting of the proprietors was held in the Boardroom at Euston, on Tuesday. Lord Stalbridge presided, and in moving the adoption ef the report and accounts, said the widening between Saltney and Birkenhead would be proceeded with as rapidly as possible. He referred feelingly to the great loss the company had sustained in the death of Sir George Find- lay. He believed that the shareholders would approve the action of the directors In voting £lO,OOO to his widow and famili. In the new general manager, Mr. Frederick Harrison, they would have a good guide for the future. and the re-organisation of the superior staff augured well for the continued prosperity of the com- pany. The revision of rates had been a succession of meddle and muddle, and the difficulties they had to con- tend wit:, were endless. With regard to the Manchester Ship Canal they had waived their rights. A sum of money equal to the whole of thdr claim had been depo- sited in the names of himself and the chairman of the Ship Canal Company, and the Canal Company had got possession of the old lines on the 10th of July, whereas under the agreement they would not have got possession till the 27t1 of November. He thought they had done all they could to oblige the Ship Canal Company. The award. helioped, would be out this week, and he trusted that ua rutuze qualation would a:ise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>DYING CUSTOM.</head>
        <p>A DYING CUSTOM.</p>
        <p>Like many another ancient custom or fete, the Lymm Rush bearing festival is year by year showing less vitality. Monday last gave an instance of this. The interest displayed in the annual performance was scarcely noticeable. Most of the principal shops were closed, but almost all the inhabitants were away with the excursion to Scarborough. The old cus- tom is commemorated in this manner at re esent :—Be- tween twenty and thirty villagers rise in the small hours or Saturday morning, and having donned fancy cos- tumes, they divide into three parties and proceed into the village, each from a different direction. A kind of morris dance is executed by each party, and assistance is expected at each house the na ncers stop at. At nine o'elock the proceedings come to a close, and the morris dancers divide the proceeds ap.d depart to enjoy them- selves for the remainder of the day. Such was the cere- mony which took place in the villa,so on Saturday, and a stranger would certainly have found it difficult to see the connection between it and the old custom.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>PLOT TO TO ABDUCT JABEZ BALFOUR.</head>
        <p>PLOT TO TO ABDUCT JABEZ BALFOUR.</p>
        <p>£6,000 GUARANTEED FOR THE EXPEDITION,</p>
        <p>lam informed writes a et;r;si;(7ncrent of the " Pall Mall Gazette," on authority on which I can place the most implicit reliance, that it was determined yesterday to complete a plan whereby the runaway Jahn Balfour would be brought within the influence of the British authorities. The most minute precautions will be taken to avoid failure, and these I have been able to procure. It appears that Balfour has had some sort of notion that a plot against him has been in course of formatios, and had made formal application to the Government at Buenos Ayres for its protection in case of attack, and I learn that promise of protection was distinctly made to him. It remains to be seen, however, whether the Argentine police will be wary enough to guard their pre- cious protege firm the resolute men who have deter- mined to bring him home to be tried for his crimes. Four men who have been victimised by the Liberator frauds will proceed at once to South America, and by means of which I have not been able to learn the de- tails, they will strive to offect a capture of Balfour. It is proposed to get him across the Argentine frontier im- mediately into a territory with which an extradition treaty exists. A large amount of money—£6,ooo is the sum mentioned—has been found for the expedition, a well-known sportsman being said to have provideitthe greater part. This gentleman even offered to provide a steam yacht and fit her up coinPletely, on which the abducting party might bring their victim home, but it was thought that the authorities at Buenos Ayres might deal with a vessel in is way which would render the desired transport a difficult matter. The four man con- stituting the abducting party are well educated fellows, none of them above forty sears of age, and if their ap- pearance and their determination, as expressed in most forcible language go for anything, Ballour is as good as once more among us and with an excellent chance of spending a little time under the same reof as his whilom companions, Messrs. Hobbs and Wright.</p>
      </div>
