      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>VirAitiliNGTON S LAID:3IIITE a HO USES. SHOVLE. IrgßY BE ABOLISMO ? _ 3r •ICLso.7)</head>
        <p>VirAitiliNGTON S LAID:3IIITE a HO USES. SHOVLE. IrgßY BE ABOLISMO ? _ 3r •ICLso.7)</p>
        <p>Of all departments concerned in the Publirßealth &amp;bat dealing With food in flesh, fish, and Pitit is 2iott recogn i set as being of paramount importance. iiith a regularity that lc painful, cases of deemed meat and bad ffdit have to be dealt with by the Vocal magtstrates;in the present issue of the "Examine there 'era two suck' cases, one of meat and the other of Ob. • These cases,.as•siraitar previous cases have- done,-itave evoked considerable public indignaticv and thn diseased meat case irr cpestion will be util- ised am-another occasion by which to concentrate and egAlite public opinion a* to he necessity of closing :he private Mouth ter bottlle9 and erecting a publicalesttoir. Health, demands• it, morality re- quires it, and humanity pleads tor it: in this p?e- sent tropical 'weather, with the alter-shadow of the Smallpox :Tend not yet wholly withdrawn irom us, with the diarrhoea an an/twitted and unwe/tome visitor amongst us,and with the fore-ebadow of the cholera demon almost on ear shoresr each a step is all the more-necessary. Ono Corporation have for years recognised it,the officials who go in and out of these places anti have the Sanitary and Health de- partments under their special charge are zealous to have the much needed alteration bruni.ilat about,but without the public rousing themselvesso cast these institutions fronrtheir tuidst ;.t he hands of the Cor- poration and their 'officials are ;emetically tied. I would have wished that the duty of draming pub- lic attention to this important subject had been taken up by some abler pen, yet as the present op- portunity is one not to be missed L nsake no apology for submitting the resultof my investigations begun months ago. I use the personal pronoun with no egotistical motives. As an individual I feel the Importance of the subject and ao an individual I desire to speak with the directness which one in- dividual employs with another-orra public concern of mutual interest. Neither by hook nor by crook, neither by fair means nor foul means, can this be dragged into the category of .party queetions; it. is a question hr the whole community. As a sort. of preface I may say that, it is the intention in a series of short papers to treat the subject in a general and descriptive way, and also from,a health, moral, and humane point, of view to give-particti/ora.as to how public abattoirs are conducted in towns whetth they at present exist and then to sum top with sugges- tions as to where and how (mediocre-Is (-Oral be car- ried oh ill Werrington. On the hot mentioned phase several places have been poitstiel out to me in course of my investigations as suitable for the pur- pose indicated and I have pretty well considered which is the most suitable. Others of-course will differ from me, and to them Limas' soy that any sug- gestions addressed to Cleo,""Ezaasiner " Office, will receive the consideration they deserve.</p>
        <p>• THE FitESENT STATUS OF Ti 3C.AtiGIiTEIL HOUSES.</p>
        <p>Warrington may fairly well he taken as typcal of many towns in which private slaughter houses abound, it. is none worse than its neighbours, it is none better than it might be. There are 25 regis- tered and licensed slaugh.ter houses in Warrington, situated up and .down the town,but in the majority of cases in very central and: densly populated dis- tricts.Th lain itself is an argument which loudlycalls for their abolition. Take Upper and Lower Bank-st., which runs from Buttermarket-st.,toDiersey-st.Here in this unsavoury locality are situated four of the oldest slaughter houses in the town, with a qumul- tive.accommodation for 41 oxen,6scalves,&amp; 95sheep Could a more unsatisfactory situation f r this enorm- ous traffic be fixed up sn?,To make matters worse for this neighbourhood, there exists a smaller slaughter house in the adjoining, and equally tnaladorous Town Hill. To review the location of these establish- ments is essential; the importance of it calls for further reference. In Market-street,where of all places a slaughter house could be best spared, one has existed since 1,815 as iegistered premises. Bridge-street, Prieeley-st reet,Church-street,But ter- marliet-street,Orford-strcer, Antrobuseit wet. Morris Yard, Church-streetwlcrhn-srreet, Williamson-square Church-street, Golden, Grose-street. Green-street, Foundry-street, and Liverpool-road, all have their contingents. The districts are thickly populated with working classes,but even the Cheshire aide, which is of a more residents' character has its atmosphere contaminated by the stench which at times emanates from the slaughter houees placed in there, Twenty-five slaughter houses (although I or 2 of them are at present not in use)for a popul- don of 55,000 is absurd on the face of it, and scat- tered as they are throughont the town, it is impos- sible that they can be under proper supervision, no matter how stringentjthe clauses ofthePublicHealth Act. 1875. or Town',, Improvement Claus-es Act of 1847 may be. This i§ a fact so plain that it would require no elucidation were it not for the abject toleration with which such a state of things has been allowed to exist. It should te tolerated no longer. Yet before discussing a future arrange merit let the present and past system be thoroughly comprehended. There are three classes of slaughter houses in Wartington, as elsewhere, viz: (11 Those which existed before 1848 when the Local Government Acts came into operation in the district. These only required to be registered once. They include : seven of the largest in Warrington. (21 Those which have been licensed since the Local Govern- ment Acts came into operation. These licenses were for an unlimited period so that once licensed they were always licensed. But since 1878 the Corpora- tion of Warrington have made an important proviso when granting such unlimited licenses. Recognis- ing that it would be necessary at some future date to establidi a public abattoir they only granted licenses on the condition that when a public abattoir was meted such licenses would lapse and aformal agreementwas signsuivt he license, s acquies cing in that agreement. (3) Since 1890, slaughter house Ilcenses have been granted by the Health, Committee only for twelve months at a time; application for their renewal has to be made annu- ally, These are the three cla,ses of the private slaughter houses of to-day ail `.l fc)r toe put; pose of ffttllc, Sze. inapection, Coming dilltfth operation ‘;`. t/.e ri.ititC Health Act, 1875, and the Towns Improvement Clauses Act, 1847. iVith the third class no difficu:ty whatever would be experi- enced, neither in the case of Warrington would there be any difficulty with any of the licensees granted since 1878,altos her aboutBinnurnber.With the remainder as the law at present stands consid- erable difficuly might be raised by the present owners and occupiers. Section 109 of the Public Health Act says, Any urban authority may, if they think fir, provide slaughter houses and they shall make byelaws with respect to the manage- ment and charges for the use of stir slaughter houses so provided." The intention of this section is undoubtedly that the authorities should provide slaughter houses of their own but let an Authority do so and erect an abattoir of the finest des- cription and the owners and occupiers of any tumb'edown old slaughter house snap snap their fingers and say " You have got power to provide this abattoir but you have not the power to close ours. We will boycott ysurs or compete for cus- tomers with you and will remain where and as we - are as long as we've law on our side or until you pay us reasonable compensation. The shortest way out of the difficulty would of course he to amend the law and give authorities power, if they see tit to close all private establishments of this kind. There would be no injustice done, it would only he right and proper that this important indus- try in which the health of the entire community is bound up should be under the personal supervision of a resident responsible official of a Corporation. I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>WA RRINGTON AND DISTRICT HOMING</head>
        <p>WA RRINGTON AND DISTRICT HOMING</p>
        <p>SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The last race of the season was held on Wednes- day, for old and young birds from Bournemouth, 188 miles. Eight members despatched 65 birds, which were liberated by Mr. Collinge, the Manches- ter Flying Club convoyeur, aZ, 8-20 a.m., in a light south soot h-east wind. The first to wire in were Messrs. Buckley and Burrows, each at 229 p.m., W- I Big quickly followed by Mr. 11. J. Longton at 222 p.m. Owing to an irregularity on the part of Mr. Busikley he was disgust Bled trem first position, which he would undoubtedly have secured owing his greater time allowance. All the birds timed in la ere young ones, and this is the first time that young birds of the year Lave raced from so great a distance as Bournemouth into Lancashire. Velocity Result. 'Name. yds. per minute. I—E. Burrows 2—F.. Borrows 3—lt. J. Lonctoo 917 4-11. J. Longton 5—F,. burrows 6—E. Burrows 7-11. F. Greenough 824 8-11. F. Greenough BO4 9-11. F. Greenough BO4 IQ—J,In Houghton</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND' SEEN' AT WARRINGTb:N.I.</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND' SEEN' AT WARRINGTb:N.I.</p>
        <p>11.</p>
        <p>The folluvvingt tattle front" Truth " wili 'lf in- terest to Warrington readers. "A resident Of War- rington tells me that he has observed win•a- tortishaient a staff:eergeantr, a col4nr-sergeanfr and other N.C.O.'s apparently tcrtd off • for the spental duty of preventing chiffiren playing about a drain' which runs beside the barracks. I share hie• astonishment, and it tray be that my feelings are • stronger than his, for as a taxpayer I object to , soldiers being put to work', which, I should have thought, ought to be perfeetned by the Warrington .poFice. Probably, if the soldiers thus employed were consulted, it would be Viand that their objec- aorie are even stronger.'' P must confess that I cninot see the force of this "'taxpayer's "objection, ottlese, of coarse, he thins- the non-coms' pay is increased for the onerous cluttes livesys they are engv.,-el in.</p>
        <p>Tte•following appeal appeared*in tbre Ti mea " adretllement columns, a few ?kr ago. for the SAKR of GOD nun LIIS' Iforchmen, will you allow a school' to be ruthlessly torn hum. the Church? In the poor village off—, near c'ehlent" schools, which hano,been °duetting the children of the poor for fifty years. will he lost nuke,* 2,500 can be missti, kf carry out the ciemards'of tfielltineation Departinect. Fullest particulars on a,•pliontion References kindle peernitted to his Grace the .A.rchbiailfm of Canterbury. his Lordrhip. the Bishop ot Ripon, the Right Hen. W. diekson, fal.r. Subscriptions may be paid into the 13ank.----or direct to the Vicar, who vslßlckf coratedge all , amounts in Oki* column. - - - -•</p>
        <p>Is it possible that the wealthy Episcopalians are becoming tired of subscribing to- elementary schools? ',his seems the only conclesion. one can arrive at, when it becomes necessary- to ,advertise in the leadisg newspapers in the cormtry for the paltry sum of £5OO.</p>
        <p>As everyone knows, the eminent artist, Mr. Henry Woo..is. is is member of a well-known War- rinatan family. and therefore local readers will be interested in. the following extract Iron:lithe Man- chester " fitturdian." "There is now being ex- hibited at Itessrs. Agnew's gallery in St.. Ann-st., the picture chosen as a wedding gift to the Duke and Duchess ot York by the committee Ammerl to collect subscriptions in Manchester for that pur- pose. It is the "Rivals" painted two years ago by Mr. henry Woods, a Lancashire artist by birth, anti neo-Ven.et iTh sm in his choice of subjects: e inci- dent iog itol faclaigqitlitetrwyady,eptichteedph N.rilurn. Woods isinterest- bright, facile, fairly spirited, and oaf itrltiete tigui rdees. Everybody who cares for Mr. Luke 'Ride's neo- Venetian picture in the City Art Gallery will like Mr. Wood's picture too. If an interest it prettily coloure I light comedy painting may hopresumed in the minds of the recipients, togetherwith a not too severe demand for perfect lightingiarfor close- ness, and originally of observation, the present could net hove been more happily choson.." •</p>
        <p>The smell arising from that open sewer—com- monly called Sankey Brook—has during the past week been unbearable, and it would not be surpris- ing to know that in its train it has brought with it some disease. The Warrington people seem more tolerant of nuisances caused by neighbouring towns than they ought to be. It will be remembered that during the small-pox epidemic in Warrington, the St. Helens people showed thernelves more ardent believers in the law of self-preservation than ap- parently the Warrington authorities seem to be. Is it not time that some drastic action was taken to cleanse this brook ?,</p>
        <p>Although it is not expected that there will be any " bartering "-of licenses such as there was last year, it least not be taken for grant-A that there will be no " incidents " at the Brewster Sessions next Thursday. As we mentioned last week there have been onanges on the licensing committee and the beer party are not in such a preponderating force as they have generally been hitherto. The tetoperaare party will again,l utvierstand, be repre- sented by Mr. Char!' on, I)f Manchester, who cham- pioned their interests so ably at tbe last sessions. • MERLIN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>WAIIRINGTON AND THE COAL CrtISIS.</head>
        <p>WAIIRINGTON AND THE COAL CrtISIS.</p>
        <p>TWO TIIOUSAND MEN OUT OF WORK.</p>
        <p>The latest details respecting the effect of the coat strike upon local industries are far from re-assur- ing. Next week the Dalian] ad Bewsey, Forges, will be closed, and 1,500 men will he thrown out ot work. But this will only be a temporary stoppage, for we understand that the company are taking this step simply to allow orders to accumulate, so that in the following week they may hare all the . departments working. When all the departments are at work in the forge, 5,000 tons or more coal is burned each week. In case all the acou.mulated orders are worked off during next week but one, there will he another short cessation of work, unless the strike comes to an end. Certain railway employes in Warrington are also feeling the effect of the strike. We learn that a great number of brakesmen, who. worked on the coal traftl3 and others who have been " thrown out," because of the diminished general traffic, are being employed ou the platforms at Arpley and Bank Quay as porters. Of course this will mean a reduction in their earnings to a considerable extent. The drivers on the engines that have been stopped have been put on what otker tralE3 there is room for them; but the Company is doing all it can to find work for all their servants who are affected by the dispute. Inquiries at Cockhedge Mills show that Messrs. Armitage and Rigby are in a very safe position yet. They have a sufficiently large stock of coal to enable them to keep all bands on Nil time for mare than another month. Monks, Hall, and Co, are very seriously affected. During the present week only one-third of the [Peddlers have been at work, and nest week there will be It fdrther stopperte of. abr.mt 4cbg men. .BhOtild the strike extenl beyond next week, ahaalvae all Pt roOb n bn ii y a furtherltill the whole works will supply of coal is ob- tained. The intense heat is having its effect upon the s u lei st s Whitecross Wire Company,causing great delay with the work. .The strike, however, has not., as yet caused any stoppage of men,though in a few week, it will no doubt have this effect. At Itylands Bros. there is a stock to last them for five or six weeks yet, so that there is no immediate prospect of these works closing.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MILITARY AND TIIE COAL STRIKE.</p>
        <p>Yesterday afternoon, orders were received at Orford Barraks, to have the men of the 3rd Liver- pool and South Lancashire Regiments stationed there, to he ready at a minute's notice to proceed to Ebbw Vale, at which place there have been some disturbances among the colliers. In eohsequence of this order, the men were detained in the barracks -last night, the usual nightly permission to leave the barracks being cancelled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>LY MM.</head>
        <p>LY MM.</p>
        <p>LYMM PETTY SESSIONS..</p>
        <p>FRlDLL—Before Mr. T. P. Norris (in the chair), and Dr.</p>
        <p>Braha z ln.</p>
        <p>DOG OWNERS FlNED.—Alfred Massey was charged with havi?g on the 24th July last, kept a dog without a license at Lymm. sergeant Garner proved the case. The defendant had had a dog in his possession for about three weeks without securing a license. Ile Was filed 7s. 63. including cests.-7Wm. Hale Sutton, Heatley, was charged with keeping two dogs without a lic.mse. The defendant had only one license for the two dogs in his *possession. He was fined 7s. 61. including costs. Veret.E WITHOUT A LAMP.—Jos. Clarke, Was charged vrb it riding a bicycle without having a lighted lamp at Partington, on the 26th July. He was fined 54. includ- ing costs. WILFUL DAMAGE: BY YOUTITS.—Peter Leather, Herbert Dandy and Win. Fray, three youths, were charged with destroYitig some rose and carnation plants, the property of Peter Burrows, Booth's Hill, Lrnim, to the extent of 104. on the sthitist. Defendants pleaded guilty. Ser- geant Garner said from information received, he went to the garden and saw that someone had been to the apple and pear trees. He went to see Leather's father and ascertained that his son had been out all night. He found that Leather's shoes corresponded with the .foot prints in the garden. Leather admitted being in the garden and having taken two apples, and the other de- fendants also admitted taking some fruit. They wiTe dismissed with a caution. THR ALMOND CRUELTY To CHlLDREN.—Elizthoth Hume, Sandy•lane, Lymm, was again placed in the dock charged with having wilfully caused her children earned Thomas (8). Samuel (5), and Henry (3), timiecessary auffering.—lnspector Uphill (N.S.P.C.C.), Warringtor, said since the summons had been taken out he had visited the defendant's house on two occasions, and found a great improvement in the state of the house and the childrer, and he now wi‘hed to withdraw the case. The case W 53 accordingly witlidra-va.</p>
      </div>
