      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>HE Gives 1.11319FLF UP. On Wednesday a, middle-agtd man named Peter William Fey, walked into the Police Station and said he wanted to surrender himself for embezzling £25 from his employers Messrs. William Galloway and Co., wholesale ironmongers, 77, Milton-street, Glasgow. His evidence was Laken down by Inspector Spinks, and the Glasgow police were communicated with. Foy was brought be- fore the Borough magistrates at the Police Court, Irlam- street. on Thursday, when he was remanded for the Glasgow police to fetch him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>SELLING ROI TEN PEARS.</head>
        <p>SELLING ROI TEN PEARS.</p>
        <p>At the Wan-ington Borough Court, on Monday, James Rathbone, 9, Flag-lane, was charged with selling on the 3rd August, pears which were faded and unfit for human food, 301bs. of which were seized by Inspector Norman. Inspector Norman said on the day in question he was in Orford-st., when he met the defendant hawking pears. He was selling them to little children at it per pound. They were quite rotten and some at the bottom of the barrel were in a mash. He told him a great number of persons were suffering from diarrhcea in the town and the consumption of rotten fruit would cause it to spread. Defendant said he gave 3s. ed. for the pears in the mar- ket but thought they were only faded skin deep. He was fined gl including costs. ASSAULT ON A LODGINGZOUSE PROPRIETOR.</p>
        <p>Martin Curley and -,Trii-n-ihield;;;;;;b4--h-Ctia;ged at the Warrington Borough Court on Tuesday, with assault- ing Mr. Bruchemite, landlord of a model lodging-house, oa Friar's-green. Mr. A. Browne appeared on behalf of Mr. Bruchemite. Mr. Browne said some time ago John Skil&amp; was sent to gaol tor assaulting Mr. Bruchemite, and on Sunday Mr. Bruchemite was in his enclosure-when Shields came through the lodging-house and told him that he had got one month for him before, but now he would have three months. Be then struck Mr. Bruche- mite and as his client was getting his coat off Shields again struck him. Mr. ttruchemite pushed him away and he fell in a tub of lime and water. A struggle afterwards ensued in which Mr. Bruchemite was struck by Curley once. Messrs. Bruchemite, J. Jones, and J. Gorton gave corroborative evidence. Curley was lined ss. and costs or seven days' imprisonment and Shields was sent to gaol for one month hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>ALLEGED INFRINGEMENT OF THE CANAL BOATS ACT.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED INFRINGEMENT OF THE CANAL BOATS ACT.</p>
        <p>Thomas He 64, of Runcorn, was charged at the War- rington Borough Court, on Monday with using a canal boat as a dwelting house without being registered on July 31st,and Richard Abel also of Runcorn,was charged with permitting it to be so used. The Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) prosecuted, and Mr. Percy Dat.ties de- fended.—lnspector Norman said that he found the boat at Mersey Mills on the date in question. It was laden with India corn. Witness went into the cabin and asked the captain. Thomas flashy for his certificate which he produced. The bust, however, was not lettered or marked with the number as required. Wit- ness afterwards evamined the locker, and found within some bed elothed ins dirty condition. Helsby said that he had slept on board the night but one before, and that he only slept on board when he was away from Run- corn.—lnspector Powell corroborated.—Mr. D vies denied that his client had slept on the boat and Abel on being sworn said that it was an oversight that tke letters and marks had not been put unotothe boat. The magistrates dismissed the case, but told Mr. Abel that he should have the marks placed upon the boat. oce OBJECTION TO A LICENSE TRANSR FE. At theu transfer day at the Warrington Borough Court on Monday, a number of transfers were granted. Mr. Hunt (chief constable) objected to the transfer of the off license at 153 Jones' Buildings, from Mary Ellen Lea to Charles Augustus Lea. Mr. Browne who appeared for Lea said that the applicant bad worked at Whitecross Works for 13 years, and his father formerly held the license. Last year it was transferred to the daughter. It was however, unfortunate that she was sleeping at the" Red Lion "occasionally where her parents reside, but since she had found out that this was not allowed. she had lived in the house. She now wished to trans- fer the license to her brother. She was acting in ignor- ance of the law in not sleeping on the premises. The Chief Constable said that when the magistrates heard what Inspector Spinks had to say they would have a different opinion. The license in question was trans- ferred to Mr. John Lea on July 13th, 1891, who after- wards wisheA to transfer it to his daughter Mary Ellen Lea, a single woman. He (Mr. Hunt) thought she was rather young, arid when it was transferred to her, he told her the requirements of the law, that the holder must be a resident and live on the premises. On July 21st this year, Inspector Spinks was on the premises and saw the wife of the present applicant serving at the bar. He asked for the licensee of the premises. He was told that she was at tire" Red Lion," and that they (present applicant and his wife) were managing for her. She has! not lived there for twelve months. Inspector Spinks then went to the "Red Lion" when Mary Ellen Lea's mother said that the business was theirs and they paid the income tax. They wanted to keep thd business in their own hands to see how the" Red Lion" went on.—lnspector Spinks was called and corroborated the Chief Comtable statement.—Mary Ellen Lea was called by Mr. Brown and said that she had been ignor- ant of doing anything wrong in sleeping off the premises' aud ever since Inspector Spinks' visit she had slept on the premises.—The transfer was granted, but the magistrates gave warning that they would not grant transfers in similar cases in the future.</p>
        <p>lIINOR CASES.</p>
      </div>
