      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE .COURT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE .COURT</p>
        <p>• • ....----- - SATIIRDAY.—Before _ISI,-. John Burgess (in the chair) and</p>
        <p>Mr. Lewis Vmiey,</p>
        <p>KICKINO THE Por;i-ca—.:--Bd,rd-I.lix-ley, lodging at Mr. J. Al en's, Pelican Vaults,Buttt.rmarket-st., was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-st., at 11.30, the previous night, and also with assaulting P.C.'s De'allay and Bruin at the same time and place. From the evidence of the two police officers it appeared that the prisoner was knecking, at the door of the Pelican Valets and using most obscene language. P.C. Delahay spoke to him about his conduct, and immediately he took a running kick at the officer, knocking a piece of skin off his leg. P.C. Bruin cameup and he kicked that officer also. After evidence by Mr. John Allen, the pri- soner was fined 10s. and costs, or 14 days.</p>
        <p>MONDAY —Before Alderman Harrison (chairman),Messrs F. Monks,_ and J. W. Hayward, Dr. Gornall, and</p>
        <p>Councillor B. Roberts. DELNIC AND DISORDERLY.—Wm. Brown, Friar's-green, Wm. Cotton, Boyle-st., Wm. Bolton, no address, John Bohan, Earlestown, Elizabeth Kearns, 11, Silver-st.,Ann Gore, Wm. Ross, and John Ross, 1, Dakin's-vard, off Church-st., Annie Donlan 42, Silver-st.,Ellen Hardy, 18, Silver-at., Charles Fudge, Ann Fudge, and Bridget Walsh, 10, Vernon's-yard, off Dial-st., Wm. Bailey, Golden Grove-at., George Webb, 1, Priestlev-st., were dealt with for this offence. SPENDING HIS FORTUNE.—AIbert Alcock, was charged with being drunk and disorderly on Saturday night, in Win%ick-road, and with assaulting Mary Ann Alcock. P.S. Ttowbotton and Mary Ann Alcock having given evidence of the assault, the Chief Constable said that toe prisoner had come into a fortune some time ago, and would not be happy until he had spent it all.—Alder- man Harrison said that the magistrates would prevent him from spending it for a little time, and would send him to gaol for a month at Her Majesty's expense. SLEEPING OUT.—John Urey, Earlestown was charged with this offence, but Inspector Dowtv, , ap- plied for his transfer to the county police for an offence committed in the county.—The magistrates granted the transfer.</p>
        <p>Canztzz TO A HORSE.—EIIen Davies, Widnes., was charged with causing a horse to be illtreated on J3ly 254 1892.—P.5. Taylor and P.C. Leathwood, proved the case, and prisoner was fined 53. and costs or seven days. TRANSFERS OT LICENSES. —Transfers of licenses were granted from Jos. H. Clarke, at the Albion Hotel, Bat- tersby-lane, to John Llewellyn; from George Pilling at the Imperial Hotel to Thomas Partridge ; John Starkey to Elizabeth Starkey, at the Brook Inn, Knutsford-road; Henry Longshaw to Mary Grounds, provision dealer, 1, Allen-st. ; Mary Ellen Lea to Charles Lea, 153, Jones's Buildings. Marie Littlewood was granted temporary authority to sell at the Unicorn tun, in the place of her husband deceased. _</p>
        <p>RIDING ON THE Fool.Para.—Albert Scott, 204, Long- ford-st., was dismissed with a caution for riding on the footpath on a bicycle, in Orford Avenue, on the 10th inst. OBSTRUCTION OF THE Roan.—Joseph Ca!doyen, green- grocer, 152, Winwick-road, was dismissed with a cau- tion, for leaving a cart in Market-st., an undue length of time on the sth inst. PITCH AND Toss. —The case of George and Joseph Jackson for pitching and tossing on the 6th inst., was adjourned until Monday next. PLATING" BANKER."—James Jordan, no address, was charged with-having on the 23rd July, played a game of chance. Defendant was caught playing at " Banker " with three others by P.C.'s Barlow and Clough, in Cloth Hall Yard. He was fined 58. including costs. FIGHTING.—George Lemmon, residing at Stockton Heath, a coloured man, was charged with fighting in Buttermarket-st., on the sth inst. He was bound over in his own recognizances of £5 to keep the peace for six ritonth.—Frederick Stockton, 12, Gaskell-st. and Albert Whitham, 154, Orford-lane, were charged with fighting on Piraner's Brow, on the 7th inst. They were hound over to keep the peace for six months.—James Rooney, 10, Union-st., and John Collins, Cloth Hall Yard, were dismissed on a charge of fighting in Bank. at., on the Bth inst. TUESDAY.—Before Mr. R. Garnett (in the chair), Coun- cillor J. H. Fieming and Mr. Lewis Voisey. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Frederick Symonds was charged with this offence in Bridge-street, the previous afternoon. He was fined ss. and costs or seven days. Ellen Rainford was discharged on a similar charge in Sankey-street on promising to leave the town. A DRUNKEN DRIVEIL—William Lomax was charged with being drunk whilst in charge of a horse and lurry belonging to the Midland Railway Co., the previous afternoon, in Hill-street. This was prisoner's third appearance for the same offence and he was fined 10a. and costs or 14 days. • Hs Ibrrusumn HOME.—Michael Cunningham was brought up on a warrant charged with being drunk in Winwick-street, on the 4th of May. Prisoner did not answer the summons at that time and left the town,but on returning on Monday he was arrested. He was fined 5s and costs. _ . .</p>
        <p>HER HALF-DAY OFF.—Ellen Smith was charged with absconding from the Warrington Union Workhouse,with the clothing belonging to the Guardians.—Sergeant Rowbottom found her at 12 30 that mornin,g in Crosfreld- street. Prisoner said she had been allowed half-a-day off from 12 o'clock to 8, but had been to Moore ..nd could not get back in time and was afraid to go ih.—The magistrates sent her back again to the workhouse. A FALSE ATTESTATION.—John Quinn, a soldier, was (-barged with making a wilful false answer on the 3rd May, that he had not previously been discharged from Her Majesty's Army when he had been so discharged.— Prisoner pleaded guilty. He was sent to gaol for 14 days' hard labour. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE CASES.—Nr. G. Riley prosecuted in the following cases in which parents had neglected to send their children regularly to school. Elizabeth Bates, housekeeper, Orford-lane, her brother Peter was sent to the ship" Clio "as the boy was proved to be incorrigible Mr. A. Browne appeared on behalf of the defendant, John Barnes, cab driver, King-street, 2s. ed. or seven days ; Sarah Chorlev, Orford-lane, 2s. ed. ; William Farrington, forgeman,Bewsey-road, 54 ; John Mackey, forgeman, Winwick-road, 2s. ed. ; Elizabeth Moores, School Brow, 2s. 6th; Thomas Morris, newsagent, Thel- wall-lane, Latchford, is. each for two children. WEDNEEDAY.—Before Mr. John Fairclough (chairman), - -</p>
        <p>Councillor J H. Fleming, and Mr. Join Geddes. ASSAULTING His WIFE.—John Allen was charged with assaulting his wife Mary Ellen, on Monday. Mrs. Allen said about 8 30 on Monday nights she got her husband's supper ready, and he commenced grumbling and gave her a smack on the face, causing a black eye. He also kickid her in the breast several times. Prisoner ad- mitted giving his wife a black eye. He said he workcd during the night time, but was unable to sleep during the day, owing to his wife drinking with neighbours, The maeistrates bound him over to keep the peace for six months in two sureties of £lO each, or go to gaol for one month.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISOIIDERLY.—David King was-lined ss. and costs for being drunk and disorderly at Friar's-gate, the previous right, and John Leigh, confectioner, was dismissed on a similar charge, on promissing to sign the Pledge.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY—Before Alderman T. H. Sutton (in the chair), Councillor James Fai:clough, and Captain Ridgway. FALSE ENLISTMENT.—Peter Doyle or Peter Coroboy was charged with enlisting in the 3rd Royal Lancaster Militia whilst at the same time belonging to the South Lan- cashire Militia. D S.Monaghan proved the case. Prisoner was sent to gaol for one month. The usual reward was granted to D.S. Monaghan.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Major Pickmere and Measrg, Ziba Armitage and J:Crostield.</p>
        <p>EJECTMENT ORDER ACIAINST A POLICE CONSTABLE.—Mr. Robert Lowe, auctioneer, applied for an ejectment order against John Grundy, a police constable of Bur- tonwood. The order was granted. DRUNK AND DISORDERM—JaMPS Mather,CrOft ; John Morris, Sankey Bridges ; and Mary Williams, Sankey Bridges ; were fined for this offence. BICYCLE ON FOOTEATIL—Fnr riding on a bicycle on the footpath in Cuerdlev on the 12th inst., John Joseph Caine was fined 2s. ed. ARREARS IN PAYMENT OF RATES.—A distress warrant was issued against Thomas McCarthy, Buirtonwood, for I Bs. due for rates.</p>
        <p>POLLING UP TußNlDS.—James Flynn, Moss flank, Widnes, was fined in costs for polling up a number of turnips in a field of James Wright, Cueriley, and doing damage to the extent of 3d.. .</p>
        <p>CRUZ'. LTY TO A Roam—John 'trey, was charged, that on the 12th April he illtreated a horse in Burtonwood by beating it. Inspector Dowty R.S.P.C.A. said that pris- °Ler was beating the horse with unnecessary violence, when be was driving behind another ca rt in a field and could not pass it. Prisoner was fined 10s. and costs or in default 14 days. TEE CHURCHWARDEN AND THE FARM LABOURER.—Wm Taylor, farmer, Sankey, was charged with having on the 7th inst., assaulted and beaten James Ryan, and Ryan was also charged on a crosssummons with assault- ing and beating Taylor on the same date. Mr. Percy Davies appeared for Taylor.—ltyan stated that he was a farm labourer for Mr. Jeans, in Sankey, and when Mr. Jeans was away, he took charge of the farm. Mr. Tay- lor was ernplo)ed to cut the corn. Mr. Jeans was away on the day of the alleged assault when Taylor sent two men to his field to cut some corn. Witness told them that the corn was not fit to be cut as it was too wet. The men went to Mr. Taylor and told him that they had been stopped from working by witness. Taylor came to see witness about the affair and waited in the Chapel House Inn for him. Witnoss went into the public-ionise to see him, and was carrying a pair of dubblng shears at the time. When Taylorsaw him, •lie came ta him and demanded why be had stopped his men. Witness told him, whereupon Taylor knocked him to the ground and gave him two blows on the face.—Frank Johnson and Michael Mannion wire called be Ryan, but Mannion remanded payment fi r loss of time if he gave his evi- dsTce. Ryan paid him 34. 6d., after which Marinion said that the door of the public-house was shut when the scuffle took place and he did not see it begin.—Mr. Davies said that his client had been churchwarden for</p>
        <p>Sankey for some years, and he had never had any dis- pute with anyone before. He had looked after Mr. Jeans' harvest for years before Ryaa worked there, and Ryan had no right to stop his men. Oa the day of dis- pute, Taylor was annoyed at Ryan's action in stopping them, and when be asked hire why he had done so, Ryan said : " lam boss here. I sleep on the place." During further recrimination, Ryan lifted up the shears and was going to strike him, and in stopping him Tay- lor knocked him on the grouad.—The magistrates dis- missed the charge against Taylor and the crosssummons against Ryan was withdrawn. FRWAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), Alder- man Wright and Mr. S. Wright. ASSAULT ON A CRlPPLE.—Patrick McGrath, Percivals Fold, was sent to gaol for 14 days, for assaulting a cripple named Mannion, in Horsemarket-st., on the pre- vious Right. CRUELTY TO a HORSE.—Arthur Owen, 23. Mill-st., 13 was charged with ill-treating a horse 3, working it while in an unfit state, on August 12th, and Edward Connor, Sankey-st.. was charged with allowing it to be worked.—lnspector Dowty said that on Saturday night ha was in Sankey-st., and saw defendant Owen driving three horses attached to a large 'bus heavily laden with passengers going to Penketh. The centre horse was ex- cessively lame on its near foreleg. It had to lie against the pole as it went along Sankey-st. Witness stopped him and told Owen to take the horse out of the 'bus. He found that it was suffering from enlargement of the bone in the fetlock. When questioned, Owen said that , he had been told by Mr. Connor to take the horse to Penketh for that journey as there was no other horse available. The horse was totally unfit for the journey as its side was raw and a lamer animal he had not known fora long time.—P. S. Cheeny corroborated the Inspeceor's statement.—Mr. C3ooor denied that he knew that the horse was lame, and the other defendant also said the same.—The magistrates fined Connor 10s. and costs and Owen was fined in the costs.</p>
        <p>WASHING DAY Row.—Bessie Grant, 37, Priestley-st., was charged with assaulting Mary Evans, 35, Priestley- St., on August 15th. Mr. Browne appeared for the complainant, and from his statement it appeared that it was a case of the clothes line and nails again. On the date in question, Mrs. Evans had some blankets on her line v-hich was attached to a nail to which both of them had equal right to. A dispute appears to have arisen about the line, and Mrs. Grant took off two of the blankets and threw them over Mrs. Evans, and dirtied them. She subseqently emptied a bucket of water over her. Mrs. Grant complained that she had no comfort owing to Mrs. Evans, and she was aggravated when she poured the water over her. She was bound over to keep the peace for six months, and ordered to pay the costs.</p>
        <p>WIDNES PETTY SESSIONS. THURSDAY —Before Mr. H. Wade-Deacon.</p>
        <p>Datum AND Disonnstay.—For this offence the follow- ing were fined :—Patrick Moran, Nelson-st. ; Elizabeth Hunter. Charlotte-st. ; Bridget Mitchelmore, Caroline- at.; James Hooligan, Margaret-st. ; Thomas Pigeon, Oxford-st..; John Howard, Marsh-st. ; Mary Ann Hughes, Berne-Bt. ;Mary Brown, Mill Brow ; and Henry Bisman, Steward-lane, Ditton. The following were bound over in the usual sureties for committing breaches of the peace I—Maria Cane, Nelson-st. ; Mary Ann Turrill, Victoria-st. ; and Ann Fitzhurst, Victoria-st. BICYCLIST(' DT TPOUBLE.—Thomas Goodwin and Fredk. Tilley, of Bold, were each fined 2s. 6d. and costs for riding their bicycles on the footpath. Before Mr. Deacon and Mr. Robertson Gladstone.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT ON THE POLlCS.—Edward Bond, 71, Pad r-st., was fined 23. 6d. and costs for being drunk and disor- derly, and 103. and costs, for assaulting P.C. Spratt. When apprehended on the former charge, the prisoner violently kicked and struck the officer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY. James Handley and Mr. P. Speakman. DRUNK AND DISOIRDERLY.—For this offence the follow- ing were fined :—Peter Connolley, Mill-street; Henry Cooke, Robert-street ; John Salt, Penketh-lane ; George Wilkinson, Snuffy-row; James Badge, address unknown. ALLEGED APPLE STEALING.- -A boy named Walter Sinnott, of Stone-street, was summoned for stealing 20 apples growing in thegarden of Mr. Samuel Beckett Chadwick, J.P.—The case was dismissed for want of evidence. DAMAGING POTATOES AT RUNCORN.—George Bra zendale, John Henry Hough, and John Hadley, youths of Run- corn, each pleaded guilty to having in the township of Clifton committed damage to the extent of 7s. 6d.,upon potatoes growing inn field belonging to John Morris, by trampling the same down. The complainant said he had suffered great damage through this sort of thing and he was compelled to seta watchman to protect his field. The defendants were searching for mushrooms.—The defendants were each fined 6d. and costa Us. BEGG &amp;G.—Elizabeth Eccles was charged with begging in Church-street, and pleading guilty, was sentenced to a week imprisonment with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Messrs T. J. Gillespie (in the chair), R. Stone, A. MeCerquedale, and T. H. Pearson. . .</p>
        <p>LICENSE TRANSFERS.—The following licenses were transferred :—The Queen Ann Inn, Golborne. from Thomas Hu- st to 'Thomas Cliffe, Upholland ; The Owl's Nest, Hayclock, from John Cunliffe to William Waine ; the Manor Arms, Golborne, from Alice Hart to William Melling, auctioneer; the Hand and Banner, Newton, from James Marsh to George Sturgess, and an off license from Mary Boardman to Thomas Naylor. DRONE ON LICENSED PREISISES.—WiIIiam Tracy pleaded guilty to being drunk on the licensed premises of the Ram's Head Hotel, Earlestown, on July 29th, and was fined ss. and costs. A LITTLE INGRATE.—CharIes Cullen was charged on re- mand with having stolen on the 4th August half a sovereign, the property of Septimus William Rothwell, of 181, Legh-street.—Defendant pleaded guilty.—Prose- cuter, who appeared in the box arrayed in the garments ot a eoldier of the Salvation Army said that on August 2nd the defendant came to his house,and moved witness to compassion by the narration of a pathetic story of his own woes, and the cares and miseries he suffered owing to being an orphan without either "father or mother." Witness took him in and gave him meat and drink, and told the lad he would try to obtain work for him the following morning. On Friday the prosecutor came home after drawing his wages. He put half a sovereign on the mantel piece, and then he went out and presently the lodger in company with the good Samari- tan's " half quid " followed suit, and also went out, but unlike the witness they had gone " never to return." One of them thought so, anyhow. But when the witness perceived their absence he went to the Earlestown Station and discovered young Charles promenading the platform like a " bloated aristocrat," with a ticket for Leigh in his pocket and 93. 80. change. Prisoner showed an emphatic disposition to prove his rights to the money, so he was given into the custody of P.C. Green. It was stated by Inspector Kelly that prisoner had been lurched at IVarrington in 1889 for thett. Prosecutor suggested that the Bench should deal leniently with the lac', so they comppitted him to prison for 10 days, and ordered that he should be sent to a reformatory for four years. WRAPPED IN Rl:MS.—Robert Travis, was summoned for being asleep whilst in charge of a horse and lurry at Haydock. A constable who proved the case stated that when he found the defendant lie was fast asleep wrapped in his rugs.—Fined 2s. 6d. and costs. .</p>
        <p>A SLIGHT DISAGRSEMENT.—James Meadow croft, of Lfaydock, was summoned for assaulting Joseph Seddon, at Haydock, on the sth inst.—Complainant stated that on the day mentioned, he was speaking to the defendant when the latter, suddenly, and without warning, kicked him in the jaw and then ran sway. A constable saw the occurrence, and advised him to take out a summons. —A collier named Edward Young stated that complain- ant started the quarrel.—The Bench ordered defendant to pay the costs, and also advised him not to introduce his clogs in future disputes. DRUNKFNNESS.—The following persons were dealt with far this offence i—John Twist, Lowton ; Albert Hoyle, Newton; Matthew Stacey, Newton ; John Overton, Newton.</p>
        <p>The boating and bathing fatalities reported on Wed- nesday gave a death roll of thirty-three. lty one acci- dent, which occurred on the Shannon late on Monday night, a party or seventeen excursionists were drowned; they were crossing from the Clare side to Kerry, and it is supposed—for no silt. survived to tell the tale—thst the boat capsized when near Carrig Island. No bodies have been recovered.</p>
        <p>" VADCWIT'S COCO has, in a ren.arkabie deal-re. those natural elements of sustenance which give the cyatem endur- ance'.nd hardihood, with a-stelay action tlatt rtstthla it a most acceptable and reliable beverage."—Heaqh.</p>
