      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>HELENS.</head>
        <p>HELENS.</p>
        <p>MR. J. H. WILSON, M.P., AT ST.</p>
        <p>THE RECENT LAW SUITS.</p>
        <p>A public meeting of working men was held on Sunday, at the Chemical and Copper Workers' Club, St. Helens, Mr. P. J. King presided, and was sup- ported by Mr, J. H. Wilson, Ma, general secretary of the Amalgamated Seamen and Firemen's Union, Mr. T. Glover, miners' agent, St. Helens, and Mr Edward 11PHugh, Liverpool. . . .</p>
        <p>The Chairman referred -to the recent actions in which Mr. Wilson had figured. He said he was still determined to fight, and in a very short time they would see him prosecuting and defending cn his own account before the judges of this country. Mr. Wilson, who was received with loud cheering, said lie had been getting well advertised lately. He did not wish to condemn the Press, because generally it was fair and straightforward. He had been the victim of a conspiracy, and had even been condemned by men in his own rank, who had called him foolish for going to law ; but he was going to draw a line somewhere. Why should they be called everything that was bad because they advocated the cause of labour. He did not object to fair criticism, but as an Englishman he claimed the protection of the court whether he was an agitator or not. (Cheers.) Mr. Wilson referred to the jury law, and said he intended next session to introduce into the House of Commons a bill which would ena,ke it lawful for every ratepayer entitled to vote at a Parliamentary elec- tion, to be selected on a jury, and that whilst serving on a jury he should receive 10s. ed. per day. Mr. Wilson then detailed the circumstances which led to his recent actions against London papers, and said he would put the audience in the position of a jury to try him. A chemical worker here rose and said they were not ,finding fault w-ith Mr. Wilson._ .</p>
        <p>The Chairman: Sit clown. Don't let us have any more interruptions. I won't have it. The Chemical Yorker: Won't you? You will have to have it.</p>
        <p>The Chairman again rose, and, amid shouts of "Put him out," went to the man and forcibly ejected him.. . _ . .</p>
        <p>The Chairman subsequently said the; li'ad been slightly interrupted by a man to whom he had been Obliged tp show the door. (Laughter.) Mr. Wilson, in the course of further remarks, said he was inclined to think that he was considered a danger- ous man, and that the House of Commons would be better without him. In one of hiscases the judge would not grant a stay of execution, with the result that the defendant had hurriedly got a taxed hill of costs, and the bailiffs were at his house then. They would, how- ever, have to get in. (Laughter, cheers, and "Don't let them in.") He did not think they would get in, be- cause lie was now travelling. They were like Shylock, and wanted their .pound of flesh. The working men could prevent that. He believed he had been fizhtin g the working men's cause, and if they helped him, he would still go on, in spite of thoopposition and persecu- tion. The total amount of costs was £6OO, of which £lOO had been raised. He could have had money from other sources, but declined it. Mr. Glover moved a resolution sympathising with Mr. Wilson. and promising hiM what practical support was necessary to ensure justice. He advised Mr. Wilson to hold the fort a few weeks longer, until the miners pulled through, and then they would be ready to support him. A chemical worker seconded, and Mr. E. M'Hugh sup- ported. • The resolution was carried unanimously, and a vote of thanks to the chairman closed the proceedings. . . .</p>
        <p>, It is understood that a committee will be. formed to carry out the resolution.</p>
        <p>MR. GLADSTONE AND THE MINERS'</p>
        <p>EIGHT HOURS BILL.</p>
        <p>LETTER TO MR. S. WOODS, M.P.</p>
        <p>Mr. Samuel Woods, M.P., has received the follow:n? reply from the Premier in answer to his letter askina to a day for the consideration of a Miners' Eight Ilours Bill : _</p>
        <p>Downing-street August 11 1893.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Woods,—Eight liours (Miners') Bill.—The present position as regards the Government is this : WA shall propose autumn sittings for business. What busi- ness ? That must be considered comprehensively. Every important claim, and yours among them, will be taken into view. But our duty is to the country, and we have to choose what win best justify the expenditure of time it may require. It would really be a breach of duty on our part to give away the time piecemeal. If you say we ought to give that comprehensive considera- tion as soon as practicable, I agree. In the meantime, I commend to you these questions which I think import- ant for your friends to consider :—(1) You desire "a least a day." Will" a day" be of any use? (2) Or does it mean a day and such other days as may he needed to settle the question at OnCB ? This would, under the cir- curtstances, seem rather a large demand. (3) It appears td me individually that the divided state of opinion among the districts is the greatest among the obstacles in the way. Can you convert the dissentients? If not, can you con4iliate them ? That is perhaps quite as Important it matter as any reply from the Goverhment at this moment.—Faithfully yours,_</p>
        <p>W. E. GLADSTONE.</p>
        <p>THE HEREFORD ELECTION.</p>
        <p>The polling took place on Tuesday at Hereford. When the figures were declared it was found that the Conser‘A. tive candidate, Mr. Radcliffe CoOke, had received 1,504 votes, as against Sir Joseph Pulley's 1,480. At the last election the Liberal majority was 127. " Since the general election the Unionists have gained four seats— Huddersfield, Grimsby. Linlithiowsh iro, and now Hereford ; and the LiberEils three—Walsall, Ponte- fract, and Hexhain. Cirencester has changed hands twice. There have been fifty-four by-elections since the new Parliament came into existence, seven being caused by death, seven by the unseating of members on petition five by elevations fo the peerage, seven by resignations, twenty-seven by acceptance of uffice under the Crown, and one by a double return.</p>
      </div>
