        <p>AmtiotitoN UItIUIEU</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your reference to the evening match against I MQh c;11k)u your notes of last Saturday is inisleatl7nA. A tlic; member wh9 arrangec. ttiese ee, !ling matchts during dlr.: sU.S6ri, I may state they v., re arranged to encourage junior cricket in the neigh- b urliod an,l to give match practice to the players of the town cliih. The eievens which have done duty in these inalches are cot the first eleven at all, but a scratch team, which has been as often below the strength of our seo,nd eleven as above it. Again the junior clubs get innings and bat and 116:1 18 players, which impfiltant le-Zit you 4-aVe y,';itr readers in ignor- ance of in your reference.—Yours truly, JAMES DUNN, Captain 2nd Eleven. 2, Egypt-street, Warrington, 17th August, 1893.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>NE%‘ BATHS.</head>
        <p>THE NE%‘ BATHS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l can fully corroborate " Old Swimmer's " marks with reference to the state of the baths. During the first por, - In the I was of such h• of thIS Week the Witter .„ celOtlr that Mittierous inquiries were 'Tab! Oft IM..;llday ttiite nhethrt the water had been iiakt frcin Saturday. hut to make bad matters *Porte ho 4 Children are ncw allowed in the sixpenny bath on three afternoons in the week at a charge of each. On such occasions with four or five score howl- ing youngsters in the place it is like a Bedlam, and the cleanliness of the children is not remarkable, to say the least about it. It is utterly impossible for those who take their bathe in the afternoon to at all enjoy it on these afternoons, and there are a considerable number of swimmers who are obliged.if they wish to swim at all,to unfortunately he in the water at the same time as these children,—Yeiurs &amp;c., AFTERNOON BATHER.</p>
        <p>Warrington, August 18th, 1893. Sir,--:W,e were much surprised to see in your issue of Saturday last a letter signed " An Old Swimmer" com- plaining of the inferior quality of the water supplied to the new plunge bath, and must certainly, in justice both to the Corporation and the members of the Warrington Swimming Club, contradict the extraordinary state- ments contained in such letter. It is the unanimous opirgoa of the members of our Club (now exceeding 100) that the water is everything that could be wised for ; in fact it is simply perfect for bathing purposes. We cantot but agree with "An Old Swimmer" that be 9OCIZet have been sadly "out of condition."—Yours,kc., T. G. GROUNDS t Joint Ilon.Secs. STANLEY WILSON f W.S.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>LONGFORD MANURE WORKS.</head>
        <p>LONGFORD MANURE WORKS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—On the way between Warrington and Winwick, a short distance from the former, on the left hand side of the thoroughfare, stands the Corporation Manure Manufactory. It is a small and rather ugly loeking 'building with a tall chimney attached, and one finds it rather difficult to believe that it is the tomb of £E5,954 of the ratepayer's money. And yet it eally is so, for according to the Borough Accounts, bends were granted for the amount for its erection and extension. This Manufactory has been a bone of contention in the Town Council for along time ; some Councillors holding it up as a model manufactory of the kind, and a source of profit to the ratepayers; whilst others have denounced it as a crude and wasteful experiment in the treatment of sewerage. In view of this conflict of opinion at head- quarters I submit the folowing statement of the cost and production of the works for the year ended 25th of March, 1893, to your readers as a guide for their Judg- ment in the matter.</p>
        <p>LONGFORD MANURE. DEBTOR.</p>
        <p>£ s. d. - To Manure In Stock, 25th March, 1892 — 910 0 0 „ A year's Interest and Sinking Fund l, 77 5 0 „ A year's talaries and wages „ Cartage lO6 0 7 „ Tools, material,, repairs, &amp;c. „ Rates and Taxes 43 17 10 „ Miscellaneous expenses 2BB 16 8</p>
        <p>5,055 16 2</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>CREDITOR.</head>
        <p>CREDITOR.</p>
        <p>£ s. d. By Cash received for manure „ Credit Sales of manure „ Clinkers supplied to the raving and Sewer- age Dep rtments „ Manute in Stock as at 25th March, 1893 „ Balance, beilig lass 995 0 11</p>
        <p>5,055 16 2</p>
        <p>These figures settle the question of profit and loss, and I commend them to the serious consideration of your readers.—Yours truly,</p>
        <p>A BURGESS.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY CLOSING OF BARBERS' _SHOPS.</p>
        <p>Sir,-01 late a friend of mine, who is a barber, and on Wednesday last, A rev, gentleman who has the welfare of barbers at heart, as well as labouring men as a whole, requested me to do something in order to help these struggling tradesmen to win Sunddy Closing at their shops on the Lord's Day. First, then; let ins say that I am now and have been, a strong believer in Sunday closing for more than 20 years,not only of such business but of every business not essential to human existence. We must eat if we are to live. Notwithstanding this *fact grocers are not allowed to carry on their life-sus- taining business on the Lord's Day, and the nation is none the poßrer nor less healthy, nor happy,because the life-giving business stands still one day in seven. But all such tradespeople who understand the benefit of resting on the Sabbath in relation to their health of body and in- creased comfort and wealth of heart and mind, long for the dawn of the hallowed Day above all the other days of the week. _ _</p>
        <p>_ Such is the case with my friend the barber. Shaving and hair cutting can much better be done vvithoue, on the Lord's Day than clothing and feeding the human body can be done without. Further, if no Sunday shav- ing, &amp;c.,was done,less time for Saturday drinking would follow, and more church and chapel going ; also less gambling and Sunday drinking. What physical, social, moral, spiritual, and even commercial loss the working classes of this country would sustain if the Lord's Day had to be lost sight of. If our Lord Jesus Christ had done nothing more for humanity than cause the Day on which He rose from the tomb to be given for the benefit . of the working community, the toiling classes have re- ceived the unspeakable gift of a God. On that ground alone we ought to reverence and love Him. Should we let His blessed day of rest elude our grasp,. we should earn not a single cent, more than we do now to keep body and soul together ; and from year's end to year's end we, or the coming generations would eventually, , through the g feed of gold in the hearts of employers and ' rulers, should be treated as mere material tools in their hands ani thought as little of. "Of all the Divine In- stitutions," said Lord Beaconsfield, "the most Divine is that which secured a day ef rest for man. I hold it to be the most valuable blessing ever conceded to man." For body and soul's sake, for time and eternity's sake, let us, fellow-workingmen do all we can to keep the Lord's Day holy.—Yours sincerely, R.S.</p>
        <p>THE NEW DRINK SHOPS IN BEWSEY ROAD AND BATTERSBY LANE. Sir,—lt seems to me to be absolutely hopeless to ex- vet any diminution in the tide of drunkenness which overruns and pollutes our streets so long as the licensing bench is occupied by men who are, apparently, more anxious to further the interests:of the liquor traffickers than to promote the good order and well being of the town. It ever the people are to have a controlling power over the traffic which makes such havoc amongst them, that power must be exercised by themselves direct, in some constitutional way, and not through political partizans of the traffickers, whether Whig or Tory. Another serious obstacle in the way of drink reformers is the attitude of the clergy in relation to the great brewers and publicans. How can the common pe-iple be expected to regard the liquor trade as an evil and destructive one when their spiritual guides take every possible opportunity of holding up the Greenalls and Walkers of society as generous benefactors of their fellow-men. All men, whose opinion is worth attention, are agreed tlfat the drink tiaffie, as at present carried on, is the greatest evil in this country; and it must be painfully obvious te every inhabitant of the borough, who will take the trouble to observe and consider the matter, that Warrington is shamefully overdone with public-houses. And yet in the face of these damning facts a very full bench of our local magistrates last year granted two new licenses for drink shops to be erected in two quarters of the town that were comparatively free from the evils of drink. Ah. but did not the philanthropic brewers of Wilderspool generously offer to surrender the licenses of some three or four of the smaller fry of their houses in congested districts in exchange for permits for the " Imperial " and " Albion." Of course they did, and the obliging bench so far overlooked their duty to the public, and the respect due to the magisterial office, as to entertain and endorse the illegal and discreditable offer. Greenall, Whitley and Co. hold their licenses from year to year as other publicans do, and had no more right to propose a bartering transaction to the bench than the humblest beers( Iler in the town. Into the motives which actuated their worships in granting the new licenses I cannot enter, they were known to themselves, and perhaps to others interested in the matter. But it would he sheer folly to imagine that they were obtuse enough to belif ye that the magnates of Wilderspool meant any decrease of business by their I surrender poliey. The two palaces are now in full swing and bearingthe usual fruit of such places. The action of the bench will e keenly watched when the liceneescorne up for renewal</p>
        <p>at the coning Brewster Sessions. I fear' there is lialr ground for hoping tl.;:.t t toy will be ri.f..ised. Th, Chief Constable and Mr. William Owen will certify that the houses Lave been properly built and equipped for a large trade ; and as Greenall, W'hNey and Co. hive expended a large amount of money upon them the old cry of 'confiscation would be raised in the event of the licenses being refused. The fact that these new houes are fresh centres for drunkenness and will inflict serious injury upon property as well as upon men, women, and chi:then will not, I fear, have much weight with the licensing Justices of I.Yrrington. However, we shall see.—Yours truly,</p>
        <p>OBSERVER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>PICNICS FROM W.9.4iiINOTQN.</head>
        <p>PICNICS FROM W.9.4iiINOTQN.</p>
        <p>The meraberS of the girls - F.nuly society at Holy Trinity Church, had their annual picnic on Satur- day. The place chosen was Dunham Massey and they were conveyed there in 'buses supplied by Messrs. Maginn Bros. They were accompanied by the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton (vicar), Rev. 11. R. Wild (curate), and the Rev. J. J. Langhara. A break in the journey was made at Warburton, and an address was delivered in the church by the Rev. .J. J. Lang- ham. After which the journey was resumed to Dunham, where a capital tea was pzovided. The remainder of the day was spent in visitin: places of interest in the neighbourhoo,l until 7'30" when the return journey was. tll'e'need,and War- rington was reached at. 6e3vii 9.30, a pleasant and enjoy9!:•4o ititernvii naNing been spent.</p>
        <p>Mis% Litton's sewing class connected with St. tleorge's Mission, had a pleasant trip on Saturday. The party started from Warrington in waggonettes at half-past twelve, and drove to Budworth, where they were met by the vicar, and subsequently had tea in the schools. The various places ol interest in the district were visited,and all expressed them- selves delighted with the outing.—The members of Mrs. Willis's Bible Class, numbering about 100, drove to Bale and Rainhill on Monday, and took part in a school treat which was held in the district. The Rev. E. C. E. Jackson delivered a suitable address to the cfißdren.</p>
        <p>The boys of St. Paul's choir had their annual ; picnic on Monday, when between 20 and 40 jour- neyed to Blackpool, accompanied by the Rev. T. Young-Hughes (curate). The principal places of attraction in the town were patronized and an , enjoyable day was spent. The men belonging to the same Choir intend to have their picric on Mon- day to Llandudno. The Sunday school teachers and .choir of Orford I Church, through the lindness of Mrs.'Beamont of Orford Hall. had an enjoyable outing on Monday. Die party numbered about 80, and were accom— panied by the Rev. S. Phillips, M.A.,,chaplain. They left Bank Quay station at -7 a.m. and many of the pamy proceeded first to Blacl:pool, and thence to Lytham. The usual attractions were appreciated. Tea was partaken of at the Lytham Institute mtben the sixty who sat down were well satisfied with the catering of Mrs. Fletcher. After tea a vote of thanks was passed to Mrs. Besmont, in proving the•treat. The party arrived at ‘Varrington about ten o'clock. By the seven o'clock train on Monday mornir., a large number of members of Padgate Church deft for ftrauchport to enjoy what our friends in the South would term their annual " beanfeast; or trip. The chief places of amusement and interest were visited during the day. and a substantial re- past was provided, to which ample justice was done. A start was made for home about 9.10 p.m., Padgate being reached a little after ten o'clock. All the•excursionists were fully satisfied with the cluy's out,,which they had thoroughly enjoyed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>100 MILES ROAD RACE.</p>
        <p>On Thursday the great event of the season in connection with the above club took place. The club oCertd to the members who could cover 100 miles under 7 hours gold medal, under 8 hours gold centre medal, and under 9 hours silver medal. The result was that 13 members faced the starter (Mr..l. Critchlev) on Thursday morning. The weather was rather hoc but the roads were in grand order, and fast times were anticipated. The course,which for obvious reasons was kept secret till the last mo- ment, was very secluded. and entirely on Cheshire roads, 'extending over the prettiest parts of the county. Refreshments were provided for the com- petitors, and Messrs. J. Podmore, T. Davies, J. Bel- lian, and E. Pickavance, jun., gave their services as checkers. After some grand riding the competi- tors came in as follows :—Gold medals—J.Wadding- ton,6 hours 16 mins. ; F. Molyneux,6 hours 58 mine.; T. Earns, 6 hours 591 mins. Gold centre meaals— S. Cook. 7 hours 17 mine.; W. Henry Illian, 7 hours 19 mine. ; G. Whitham, 7 hours 42 miri. ; J. Davies, 7 hours 50 mine. The performance of Wad- dington was truly grand, and shows what a sterling road rider he has become. Molyneux and Earns r rather surprised the members as they are compara- tively new at such distances. It is only fair to state that W. 11. Bellian and S. Cook lost what chances they had in the race owing to both their macliines puncturing, and thus causing them gnat delay and annoyance. After the race tea was pro- vided at the Cyclists' Farm, at High Legh, and a goodly spread was promptly despatched. After a nice ride in the cool of the evening to %Farrington, an adjournment was made to the Norton Arms, where a "smoker" was held. Mr. D. Davies occu- pied the chair, and Messrs. W. Mounfield,G.Stnith,J. Dooley, J. Harrison, J. Buxton, F. Molyneux, J. Whitham, and S. Baldry enlivened the evening with songs and selections of music. The party broke up shortly before 11.0 after a grand day, and much credit is due to the officials for the efficient manner in which the arrangements were carried out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>RU. T. ROSS' FAREWELL SERMON.</head>
        <p>RU. T. ROSS' FAREWELL SERMON.</p>
        <p>SOME STRAIGHT SPEAKING.</p>
        <p>On Sunday night, the Rev. Thomas Ross. who is leaving Warrington Wesleyan circuit to go to Hull, preached his farewell sermon at Bold-street Wes- leyan Chapel. He took as his text Romans 15 c. 5 v. "Now the God of patience and consolation grant you to be like-minded one toward another according to Christ Jesus." In the course of his remarks he said that the gospel had been preached in this country for 800 years, yet still there was a large amount of suffering. They had their asylums, their prison houses, their workhouses. To-day there were fathers who were turned into fiends, mothers heart- broken, and homes turned into hovels, all though sin. Surely in a town like Warrington all the followers,of Jesus Christ should be united in aim to get rid of sin. If scripture taught anything it taught that in this world there was a contiaual warfare being waged between light and darkness, truth and falsehood, Christ and Belial. Every man in the town took his stand on one side or the other. It was impossible for anyone to be on the side of both' How often they found men trying to serve Christ aria Bohai, trying to cheat bath, but were terribly duped themselves. He would illustrate this, and he did not care even if he suffered imprisonment for it. There was across Bewsey Bridge a mission called Hoyle-street Mission. Sir Gilbert Greenall some years ago gave £5.000 to the churches, Hoyle-street Mission was built entirely out of that sum. What was done now? The Imperial Hotel, or the Imperial public-house had been erected not far away. If there was any power that was working against the power of Christ in this town, it was the power of the drink traffic, and where- ever God erected a house of prayer, the devil was sure to put a publichouse there, and it would be found upon in- vestigation that the devil would have the nest congre- gation. The other night he was on l'inner's Brow, and he saw sights there which he had never seen in White- chapel or Seven Dials, London. The sights were hor- rible to see on that night. He also knew of some awful cases of wickedness on the Ship Canal. All this showed how necessary it was to be like-minded with Christ. Were these present doing anything ? Some were, some were not. Let those who were not, set to work at once, join the temperance association, the social questions union, for God's sake join something to show that they intended to be united in aim to get rid of sin. There was a diversity of work and something for everyone. With the service that night his (the preacher's) ministry in the circuit came to a close. He could honestly say he had tried to be faithful,and he had done what he believed to be in accordance with the truth of God.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>.*- OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>.*- OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>THE P.S.A. AT WYCLIFFE.</p>
        <p>The usual P.S.A. Meeting was held in Wycliffe Church on Sunday afternoon, and was well attended. The chair was occupied by Mr. A. H. Lee, who delivered an address on temptation. Mr. B. Farrar alio addreg,sed the meeting, his subject being " What is ma-i ?" The soloist was Mrs. Todd, R.A.M., formerly of Warrington, who sang in an excellent manner " A soldier of the cross." The Rev. J. Yonge also took, part in the , meeting. _</p>
        <p>HIS FINAL WORDS. A large congregation assembled at the Wesleyan Chapel Padgate, on Sunday evening, when the Rev. Alfred Free- man, who has been stationed in the Warrington cirrnit for the last throe rears, preached his farewell Germ o prior to his departure to another circuit. The text was "For all the promises in Him are yea, and in Him Amen unto the glory of God by Ira." At the close of an im- pressive sermon, the preactrir besought all to continue their perfect and entire belief in Christ. P,'!":--.f • i f</p>
        <p>tti</p>
        <p>I ii 1/4)</p>
        <p>14 • • tot Nes</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>EX AMI NER, SATURDAY: AUG UST 19. 893</head>
        <p>THE EX AMI NER, SATURDAY: AUG UST 19. 893</p>
        <p>scenery we join the Weaver again. By evening has set in and the scenery we this time has become quite beautiful. Sower' go through past yellow cornfields and quiet se, then will;coure ft clump of trees 44eilsrerelvdefagrimidse; dows In the fading light duke . whose black sha- perhaps vrill folldw steep-hie,' the-,•water ;then we steam between them moo in the banks and through a ravine. Right r wad feel like sailing the sinking sun, dapplin filr in the west there is of trees, house tops, • V,-ith a ruby glow clusters over in the east the • /*A church steeples, whilst is gradually rising f',..theg moon with its pale face or three more la- w-.)he sun sinks. Through two and the darke- .06 *,%174 the sun has disappeared, faint light of 91* ehadows are dissipated by the a bit of pot •OM 161000. I might have scraped out my tate AV ,eneter these stilling influences, but getup', ditidt became otherwise engaged. Two ath w -..wr4s- were ahead of us; one of them was of , ..tettilieliver with her bow on about two yards ..tisgbank ; the other boat was trying to get her Aedt. lik.)w it happened we didn't hear—l thought tnit,ibeltfie boat aground had wanted to walk on the bank. There was an awful hub-bub for a time. There were three engines steaming at it, sometimes 'backing, . sometimes not; there were clinging of bells and cries of "let her go" or "now then— iteady—back her. Now then he; her go." And then Mere would be a clamping and stamping of feet; and tben yours truly would start in and be busy,for every- body would seem to rip out, as a last effort,as strong a force of profanity as could be got for the purpose. My note book in no time was full of some of the finest specimens extant, and- if the editor would only publish them half a column a week there would be such a rush for this paper as you never saw.</p>
        <p>It was dark when we arrived at the city of salt. The wharfs were all lined with barges. I said goodbye to my friends and started to reach the quay wall over one of those spectre like fiats. It wasn't an easy contract. I first commenced to cross the hatches and succeeded in kicking two buckets over. A big bushy head stuck up out of the forecastle cabin and said a few more things that would ruin a Sunday school magazine that published them. In a bit I started to prowl round the hatchway. Well, you know the number,of ropes that are set as traps about these boats, just to trip one over. It was one of them that did it. When I fell, two or three boat hooks, and swabs, and buckets clattered on to the deck. The head came up again. It said it was going to get hold of somebody and cast him first into a frizzling kind of perdition and then into the river. My I how I quailed! But when it bad disappeared again I crawkd over the hatches and commenced to climb the line that moored the flat to the.quay. In about half-an-hour I got to the top. I did not stop to rub off the tar and mud, and dust and things, but I made haste te reach "Hartford station. How I did this is another story, which cannot be related now. The journey was finally completed in a milk cart which I captured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>The first eleven have had better fortune this week, and amongst other things have succeeded in beating a Warrington junior team. More than 2000 spectators resorted to the ground on Saturday, on the occasion of the benefit for the professionals C:ark and Sugg. The game was played to the accompaniment of music, which the hand of the 3rd and 4th South Lancashire rendered whilst tbe spectators were being rendered (to grease spots) by the sun. There was some good cricket at this match Jam happy to have to record. But it was dis- played chiefly by Sefton. The match at first was to have been played as an ordinary fixture, but owing to Sefton hearing of the in:fusion by warrington of a fam- ous player, they cancelled the match as a fixture. Hence it was tha-Warrington were called Mr. Smith's : team, and Sefton Mr. Pnes' team, whilst the prominent county man hid himself cncier tli,.! alias of Hampson.</p>
        <p>The game was a drawl •Warrifigtoh scored 154 for ten wickets. -orgalnst 108 for five wickets. For Warring- toil ME, black hcrse " scored 72, whilst the two ClarkeB I mean Clark and Clarke—and Voisey batted very w."..11. I noticed that, Podmore had some ill-luck at the hands of Oakley. I can remember that the last time Podmore batted against Oakley there were certain pecu- liar balls which went some distance from the crease, but were knocked h iavily by Podmore. Was Oakley taking it out of him on Saturday ? The ball was a very remark- able one that Podmore ran out to,through which he was stumped so quickly.</p>
        <p>The junior Match which the eleven won was played against Walkers Brewery, on Tuesday night. Warring- I ton's team was made up very extensively from recond eleven men who distinguished themselves very much by their bad fielding. The result of the game was 62 runs, all out for Walkers, and 10 runs, five wickets, for War- rington. W. EL rodmore did very well for Warrington, as did also T. Allen for Walkers. The latter took a prize the ottilir week for snapping seven wickets for two runs. " Sullivan" also did well, indeed he was the only one who kept a" straight bat" on the field—some one thought so anyhow. Young Dodd bowled very well,' for Walkers, sad Brown caught very badly for War- rington—he didn't catch at all, indeed, to speak comely..</p>
        <p>OursecMid eleven achieved a victory in their match against Befton second. They scored 124 against 92. I 'noticed that the " innovation I alluded to last week in the person of Mr. Ashton, played and met with success. He has been selected to play against Manchester next week, and there is no doubt if he is careful things will grow rosy in the cricketing world for him.</p>
        <p>The Sankey Wesleyans were badly beaten on their return fixture with the niends of Penketh, only scoring 24 against 51. Their defeat would have been still greater hut for the last Oar's widgets who put together as much as the previous seven. &amp;idlow was the top scorer with six. Linney's score was the highest on the other side, being 19. The Wesleyans had a second innings and easily passed the Friendsscore, making 57 for the loss of six wickets. Smith the Cast bowler was very destructive taking four wickets foe five runs. The career of the Wes.leyans of late has been very erratic, and their sup- porters have to be prepared for anything. It is very strange that one week for the loss of nine wickQts they should score 100, and the following week they should tumble out for 20. Very strange indeed !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>CYCLING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING.</p>
        <p>Phew ! Fancy cycling on Saturday, or any day this week, for the matter of that ! I agree that this is the weather for bathing, but not for balling in one's own perspiration. No less than 43 members of the Warring- tcn Club set out for Siddinston on Saturday, and in accordance with an agreement played a cricket match with the members of the Sale Cricket Club. Warrington won easily owing to the good play of J. P,xlinore, G. Smith, F. Mulyneux, and H. Whittle.</p>
        <p>Knutsford is to be favoured by a visit from the War- rington cyclists on Saturday, when they will meet that noble band of vrheelmen the Cheshire Perishers. (I wonder what they did to earn such an approbrious epithet as that !) A cricket match will be played again and who will perish in the combat will not bo left to the Perishers to decide.</p>
        <p>By the way Fletcher was to have tried to break Mills Land's end to John o Groats record, this week. He was expected in Warrington on Thursday, but he did nrt get here, simply because of some dispute, which took place between the undomitable Lawrence and his machine, and in which the latter was successful, for the clever rider had to throw up the sponge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>AI ISCELLINEOUS.</head>
        <p>AI ISCELLINEOUS.</p>
        <p>T. Green, of %Varringtori, %V. Southern, of Leigh, met at the West End Grounds, Wigan, on Saturday, in a 125 yards match, on level terms, for £25 aside. Both were put into strict training, and Southern went on in charge of his old trainer, Tomnsy Lee, from the Railway Tavern, Leigh. Green, on the other liand, went amiss, and was for days very ill, and his backers did not seem disposed to invest much money. Southern consequently opened favourite at 5 to 4 on, but what money changed hands was mpstly at evens. Green, who had been tak- ing quiet exercise for the past fortnight at home, was accompanied to his mark by W. S. Mills, of Slattocks.</p>
        <p>Everything being made in readiness, the men were despatched at tke first attempt to an even start, and one of the most exciting races seen for some time past in Wigan ensued. For 80 yards they ran stride for stride, and then Green drew out a foot in advance, but he swerved, as did also Southern, but though they were very near, they managed to avoid colliding and about 10 yards from home Green shot out, and won by three- quarters of a yard. A cheque for the stake was handed to the winner by J. Gorton, the representative of the " Sporting Chronicle," who was referee, and J. Flepple- thwaite, of Manchester, fired the pistol—he did it before the cheque was given though.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>GALLANT RESCUE AT SANKEY</head>
        <p>A GALLANT RESCUE AT SANKEY</p>
        <p>BRIDGES.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED SELFISEINESS_ OF SPECTATORS.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday night at 6.30, Josiah 0-7m—so.n, aged five years, son of Richard Ormson, signalman, Littons Mill Crossings, went to Sankey Bridges to a fiat, belonging to William Houghton, near the grav- ing dock at Clare and Ridgway's works. On the way he stopped at the canal side and got on the wood rail on the side, when he saw a piece of wood in the water, and in trying to get it out, he overbalanced him- self and fell into the canal. It is stated that there were nine men at the time the boy fell in, on the opposite side of the canal who were able to swim, but they made no attempt to get him out. The boy's screams, however, drew the attention of Edward Glover, of Sankey Bridges and who works at Houghton's Wire Works. He was on the bridge about two hundred yards away, but he immediately ran to the help of the boy. At the risk of his life, he being unable to swim, he jumped into the canal, and got a foot hold on a hard substance and seized hold of the boy who had already sunk twice. He was, however, unable to keep his foothold, and went over- head, and would have b3OO drowned had not a boat home to his assistance. He was pulled out along with the boy, after a short period of time. He had swallowed several mouthsful of the dirty water, and was for some- time very sick, but he was able to go to his work the next morning. The boy, who really owed his life to the gallant efforts of Glover, was also sick from the areas of the water, but was better the next morning. AN INHUMAN BUSINESS. --- HORRIBLE DISCOVERY.</p>
        <p>VIENNA, THURSDAY.—A horrible discovery has been made at Biskupetz, near Varasnin, in Croatia. A band of men have been detected in making a regular business of the mutilation of children,their crippled victims being, afterwards disposed of to persons who send them out to beg in the streets of large towns, where their deformi- ties excite the pity of the charitable. Three men have been arrested, and in the house occupied by them a num- ber ct implements were found for the forcible produc- tion of deformities. There were five children in the house at the time, four girls and one boy, whose ages ranged from eight to twelve. Two of the girls had their arms and legs broken, another had had her eyes gouged out, and the fourth, who had only been in the hands of the band since Easter, has a number of unhealed wounds. The boy had been forced double between beards, gradually tightened by screws, and had been kept in this position so long that lie is now unable to straighten himself.</p>
        <p>WORKNIEN POISONED IN SCOTLAND.</p>
        <p>Information has been received of a strange case of Poisoning which has occurred at Milreoch Farm, Hollybush, near Ayr, thirty persons being affected. A threshing machine was working at the farm,ar,d a large number of hands (principally people from the district) were Pmployed. The whole company, including Mr. Petrie's family, were provided with dinner, and were soon exhibiting all the signs of poisoning, several being completely prostrated. Iqliiry showed, it i$ alleged, that an old kettle, which had done service for the pre- parafion of a sheep dip containing arsenic, had been used •to boil water fur cooking purposn. Two medical gentlemen from the neitOihourhood were called in, and the sufferers relieved, and all were so far recovered as to he out of danger, with the exception of one of Mr. Petrie's sons, who is still very ill.</p>
        <p>l'lmlGP.ATlON.—lntending emigrants to New York, Hoeg n Philadelphia, San Praneh,eo, Cana,ii, China, Japan, ALS rralla, or New Zea Land, shonld take Li „kets (lowest rates). al, th' " 14Rminer " by Onion. AmericAn, Anstralit.n Oriental. and ether lines.—John Walker, agetst,l•Ex anlnee °Moe. BeWlaY•gt.. r</p>
