      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>DAY'S ADVENTURES. --- INCLUDING A SAIL ON TIIE SHIP</head>
        <p>A DAY'S ADVENTURES. --- INCLUDING A SAIL ON TIIE SHIP</p>
        <p>CA NAL.</p>
        <p>EY LUKE BLUNT.</p>
        <p>I hadn't any evil intentions. When I got on board the steamer at Bank Quay, it was simply to look at the interior, and when I got interested in the coiling of a line and didn't notice that the vessel had put off, it was not, as someone suggested darkly, done purposely that I might secure a clan- destine sail. I wouldn't be guilty of such base meanness ; if I'd intended to stow away I would have told the captain first, in an honourable 'and upright manner, and then stowed away after- wards. That's my way of doing business. I am not going to write a guide bock of the scenery betw;-!en here and Runcorn—the task would be too ponderous—but I will remark that the chief fea- ture of the river scenery was the innumerable bathers. They absolutely lined the bank that hot sultr,y afternoon. I wondered how they could it; I pitted their poor ignorance in mistaking Z"..1 slimy, black, repulsive slush for water i ; __•"'ll,, shuddered when I saw them dive into the n- rise to the surface and thunder abQut Ulu ,trvi:l4 animated mud banks, and innocently bietl,inenf dthwathetnhetyl'leywelieB isnuitt trolled 'about At And scrap iitheyan tith *se ns julmee themselves, aikel roll in the gras " idently they would cor most of the dust then like amnuadvwaiaasneohff.orTmhuedri.ver rolled</p>
        <p>The t o ?-1 for Winsford and *would have tsey, and 'enter the river v els:aev l e Vrta the bAoitent Weaverat Weston Po'Ant. We Ilia do that ; hut not, in the easy way 111141 expected. It is impossible to enter the. toouth, of the Weaver now. That part of it is to farm a portion of the Ship Canal. But they have not, banked it off from the river entirely. Piles hcme been driven right across the mouth of the stream so as to dam it off from the Mersey, but the &amp;an is only as high as the ordinary water line in Ore Ship Canal. Then right above the dam the saint piles give place to upright girders several inches apart, so that at tide time the water will course from the Mersey between these girders and assist the water supply of the canal. Still, at pre- sent it is a nuisance of a dam to Warrington boats navigating the Weaver. It compels these craft to sail along to Eastham,a distance of about five miles, go through the Ship Canal locks, at that little port, and Hid back right along tte Ship Canal down to the mouth of the Weaver, just to get the other side that dam. The crews of the vessels get paid by the freight carried, and so this extra travelling must be a considerable loss to them at present. As we sailed from out of Runcorn gap into the more open ex- panse of water that lay between us and Eastham a pleasant breeze caught us and dispelled the oppres- siveness of the heat; the scenes on all sides grew charming, and the combined effect was intoxicating to the senses; I would have been drunk on it in another half-hour, in fact. When we were nearing the great new locks there was a nice" how-dy-do." The craft had stolen quietly along until three hun- dred yards away, then the engineer let the whistles go like shrieking fiends, and the officials about those locks skipped and capered about, and blew more whistles at us, and banged signal flags about at us in a way that was enough to make one laugh one's head off. Our engines were backed a time, we veered round, and came steaming up to the lock gates. In time we got through into the big lock, and there we were: forty feet below the top:of this locks, with forty feet of damp green wall on either side of us, and below several more yards of celled and bubbling water. A number of sea-faring looking officials had interrogated us, but bv-and-by one tall man in a blue uniform came and stood on the wall above, lie shouted down : " What's yer name." The Captain told him. " Where are you from." The captain told him, quite calmly. " Were are you for."</p>
        <p>The captain said it again' and I was going to observe to him that he should tell that officer not to be so blamed inquisitive, when that individual himself turned his gaze on me a minute and said "Sixpence to pay for the passenger." " Ile isn't one said the captain. " Who is he ?"</p>
        <p>" A member of the family." The officer went away. By this time the lock gates had been closed ; and then the water began to creep quicker and quicker, inch by inch, up that wall till at length our bulwarks were abreast of the top of the lock. The captain gat out and went ashore to do some business, and just as I was quietly smoking a pipe and dangling my legs over the bulwarks I spied that tall officer coming along. I slid off my perch and stood behind the engine house. Ile dropped on to th 3 deck and came towark where I was concealed. When he got near I started quietly for the other side of the house. He followed. We walked round about 20 times—l think it would be 20 —and then I noticed the foot- steps seemed to be before me. So I stopped and just then he turned the corner ahead.</p>
        <p>" Oh 1" he said, "you're a passenger." " Not. I'm one of the family." "Which family ?"</p>
        <p>" The family, of course." " Which family do you mean sir ?" I was going to say I was the captain's fativdr but I changed my mind. I would have said I was a member of one of the crew's family but I didn't like to, because some people are rather particular about their family connections. So I said : " I don't know exactly. But if you'll wait a minute I'll go and—and—and—l'll go and ask th'— t h' captain." You wouldn't believe it perhaps, but he started off and I could hear him talking to himself about different species of imbeciles like a Greek orator all the way along the lock wall.</p>
        <p>On the Ship Canal! It sounded startling: it sent a. I.thrill of eagern.es.s and plea sure through one. To i be at length sailing n reality upon that wondrous j piece of water; the thing we had known once as little more than an extravagant dream ; then as a real speculation, and, finally as an undertaking centred in a mesh of oveyrowleheaglmedinwgerdeiffigociu]eltietos completion till the , vwohui nc gh ht ha dr e agtreonwend tl ho ad re, dl a their rest. To be sailing on it, if only for three miles was so good and savoured so much of the near approach of completion that it became a joy- ful thing! lam not the only susceptible person without interest in the company. Susceptibility i in this direction s very common lately, and tran- sient thing as it, is,com mercial tact has been brought to bear upon it,a corner was formed, and now there exists a company who provide steamboats to carry the susceptible ones over the opened length of the canal for the sum of 15 i is—and t isn't a loss as a philanthropic move. It i looks a magnificent piece of water when you sail upon t. On each side there are the lofty banks, gradually sloping back- wards, and forming on the top a path which has in it such innumerable possibilities for promen- ades, into which there is no doubt they will be converted all along the .canal, bye and bye. One has a chance here of seeing the new life, which the canal will with introduce ithsen:go toiinngboWatanr,rainngdtsonna..faArinngewnileinfe d . They are already invading this opened part of the undertaking. At the several wharfs along the length were moored sea-going vessels of a size that had not often been seen that way before. There were several large harques of heavy tonnage as well as two Gr three full-rigged American ships. The L, and N.-W. Western docks at Ellesmere Pert were full of craft, whilst further down was the rising little place Saltpprt with its docks and wharfs a veri- table pandemonium of excitement--the excitement, of busy rushing labourers and seamen engaged in preparing vessels for sea and unloading others, an excitement borne ot a trade that is overgrowing it- self and requiring as much urgent attention as the children who perform such physical feats. But we do not stay here long enough to court the tlallseA. We skim past two or three sleepy old tugs,. and small craft,and run them so close as t • create quite wave of profanity amongst their various crews. We glide into what I think is the Weaver pool at the mouth of the Weaver, through two more lock, and then on to a canal which runs parallel with the Weaver.</p>
        <p>The air here is not !ling to brag about. There are too many bone works near by. From them eman- ate perfumes which I would rather not smell again for a few hundred years. There is one noticeable thing displayed here. If steamboats do not go slow they cause a backwash that wrenches the flats being unloaded. troin their moorings. Those who are on the fists throw cobs of coal at those on the steamboats, and try to fracture their skulls. The ' steam boatmen are ingenious; they create the hack- was p 1.1,;-,aeolfveahneda ptceonalthfreoy!nketeiple cobs inwi ih la re ayt iana throwngoodu Pal; them. They do this regularly, I believe. After about a couple of miles of uninteresting</p>
        <p>te&amp;:Xoa .111</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND THE SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND THE SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>SOME POINTS OF INTEREST.</p>
        <p>I Very naturally the interest taken hi the progress of the Ship Canal workt,,in the locality of Warring- ton becomes keener as the time of completion draws nearer. Since it was announced a short time ago that, the whole waterway would be opened in January next, there has been much speculation as to the possibility of the length from Runcorn to Thel wall being opened before that time. From in- quiries made in reliable quarters it seems as though there is a prospect of the length alluded to being dpened in the course of two months. The principal work which remains to be dealt with, is in connection with the new river diversion and the locks in Arpley meadows. In the river diversion dredging operations of an extensive character are being carried on as the new waterway will have to be deepened some feet. The means adopted in carrying out these operations are somewhat interesting. At present the dredger is em- ployed at the entrance to the deviation at the Walton Arches end. The w ssel possesses two shoots one pro- truding over each gunwale. The excavated mud rushes from-the buckets down these shoots, and deposits itself Into wagons which are on the lighters. The wagons are not of the ordinary character, but consist of two detached halves. They are lifted off their axles before they are shipped on the barge. Each boat carries six of these half wagons, and when they are filled, the lighter is towed round the bend of the river to a temporary jetty, where there is a strong crane. Behind the crane is a single pair of metals en whicleare the wheels and bottom part of the wagons. The half wagons are lifted by the crane, one at a time, and swung round on to the wheels where the two halves are fixed together firmly by means of a pivot. Some hundreds of %vegans are filled daily in this way. This is practically all the work that is going on in connection with the deviation at present, and there is nothing particular being carried out in connection with carrying the new road across the river. Some of the foundation work has been hid, but ate chief portion of the work I2as yet to be done, and will occupy some weeks when it has been commenced.. The new road itself right along from the Chester road, is in an advanced state. The first bridge over which it is conveyed—the swing bridge over the Ship Canal is en- tirely convicted with the exception of having the hy- draulic pressure apparatus completed. On the side from which the bridge will swing has been erected a house like a signal box for the caretaker of the bridge, who will work the ap- paratus. Continuing a few yards longer to the fixed girder bridge which conveys the road over the river we find that the structure is not in such -a forward state as the other bridge. The flooring has not been commenced yet; it will consist of the liking of a floor of wooden blocks on the present iron bottom. The road from here down to the Iron Bridge end of the meadows is far from being completely built yet. The foundation has been set and the kerb stones laid, but the layiog of the t:etts has not been commenced. To the side of the road in the meadows, and almost opposite Wllderspool brewery, there are being constructed a set of locks. These are being made on ground which forms the promontary as it were, of the bend la the river.. This site has been chosen so that the river, by being diverted a few yards will form a natural basin for the locks. The latter will not be large enough to admit sea-going vessels, hut simply to accommodate local river traffic, for which there will be built jetties, through the leeks for wharf- age purposes. We understand that no preparations are being made for accommodating sea-going vessels, as the Company anticipate that for years yet the small steamers and barges will be sufficient to meet the requirements of Warrington traffic- Considerable difficulty is being experienced between Tom Nines Bridge and Stockton Heath Bridge, owing to the sandy nature of the banks, which have lately com- menced to collapse and sink into the cutting. To pro- vide against recurrence of this a reclining embankment of stone is being built. The new swing bridge at Stock- . ton Heath is completed except for the laying in of the hydraulic apparatus. The road deviation is finished, and when the water and gas pipes have been relaid, un- der the Ship Canal,which will take a few more days, the new road will be opened for traffic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>GREAT HEAT.</head>
        <p>THE GREAT HEAT.</p>
        <p>MANY DEATHS FROM SUNSTROKE.</p>
        <p>Wednesday -wa-s-thei;tet-sid;:y-Cilthe year iti London, the shade maximum being 20deg. The nearest approach to this temperature during the present summer was 6n the previous day. Tuesday, and on July 17th, on each of which occasions the thermometer recorded 87deg. Harry Sparkea, driver of the Derby express, became seriously ill on Wednesday la hen nearing Longton, and WAS taken to the hospital, where he died. His death is attriloted to the excessive heat. Frank Jepson, a youth, who was employed in the offices of Messrs. Brunner, Mond, and Co., at Winning. ton, Northwich, has fa'len a victim to the excessive I heat. While at work on Monday he was suddenly taken ill and was attended at his home in Davenharn by Dr. Marsh, who found him suffering from sunstroke. He died on Wednesday. I Owing to the excessive heat in the Ashford, Kent, district the harvesters have in many instances been com- pelled to suspend work. There are a number of cases of sunstroke, and a harvester named Wood has succumbed. The soldiers at Lydd camp' were again ordered off parade on Thursday, and several more cases of sunstroke are reported this morning. On Wednesday afternoon, while Michael Nolte, a dock labourer, residing at 2, Harlow-st., Liverpool, was worlciog in a shed with a glass roof at the docks, he I suddenly fell down insensible. He was removed to the Southern Hospital, where he expired at eight o'clock without having regained consciousness. His death is believed to be the result of sunstroke.—A shockingly sudden death oecurred on Thursday in Ractelagh-st., Liverpool, owing, it is supposed, to sunstroke. Mr. George Murdoch, tailor and draper, lately residing at 8. Greek-st., while passing down Ranelagh-at. suddenly fell down unconscious. On the arrival of the horse ambu- lance, only a few minutes after the attack, the unfortu- nate man was found to be dead.</p>
        <p>-41 At St. Helens, on Monday, Martin Morrisey, Water lane, Sutton, was fined Ws. and c,sts for assaulting Bal- four F. M'Tear, assisting manager of the Sutton Rolling Mills, and son of Major M'Tear. Morrisey worked at the rolling tnills, and on the sth inst. questioned the mount of his wages. He went to Mr. M`Tear's home, hut the litter's explanation that he would have to see the pay clerk, did not satisfy defendant, and he pulled „the complainant into the street, and struck hiin in the "face. - • -</p>
        <p>TIIE USE OF SAFETY LAMPS IN COAL 6 MINES. PROSECUTION AT ST. nELENS.</p>
        <p>At the Sr. he lens Borough Police Court. on Mon- I day before Aldermau-Rarrieon, Messra.-.1.• Cotton, A.l Sharples, B. A. Dromgoole, and T. Glover, Mr. Francis France, agent, and Mr. William Arnold, certificated manager, for the St. Helen Collieries Company, Limited, were summoned at the instance f Mr., Henry Hall, Government inspector of mines, for having safety lamps in use in their St. Helens collieries which were not constructed so as to be safely carried againSt an air current. Mr. Mulholland appeared to prosecute, Mr. I. Swift defended Mr. France, Mr. .T. 0. Swift defended Mr. Arnold, and Mr.H. L. Riley watched the case on behalf of the Miners' Federation. Mr. Mulholland said the proceedings were brought to induce or compel the owners of the colliery to change their Safety lamps. The Case was taken under rule 9. section 49, of the Coal Mines Regulation Act, 1887, which provided that safety lamps should be so con- structed that they might be safely carried against an air current ordinarily prevailing in that part of the mine. He then explained the different k'nda of safety lamps in use. In the St. Helens collieries the old Davy lamp was used, with the addition of a guard, which could be shifted up and down. He produced a new form of lamp, in which the gauze was protected by a glass tube but added that the inspector was not bound to point out any form of lamp which would serve the purpose. He had merely to see whether the one existing was safe or not.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hall then pve evidence. He said that on the 18th April, he visited the Crop and Deep pits belonging to the St. Helens Collieries Company. The men were using the ordinary Davy lamps, with different sizes of shield attached. He tested the velocity of the air. and I found that at the Monkey-brow it was 2801 t. a minute. A man walking against the air would cause the velocity to be greater. lie estimated that the men wsuld walk about two and a half miles an hour, awl that would cause an additional 2201. t velocity, making 500 ft. at that point. At the tunnel brow it was al. ogether 7401 t. He again visited the colliery on the 16th June,and found I that the men had been provided with larger shields for Itheir lamps. In his district there were 46,000 people ' employed, and he would say that there would not be more than 2,000 of the Davy lamps used, so that it was an almost obsolete lamp. By Mr. T. Swift : "lie considered that the using of the Davy lamp, was illegal under the circumstances. There was scarcely a colliery where it would be legal to use it under the present conditions. Miners appeared to stick to it, but it did not command confidence ainon4st 'mining engineers. There had been several pit expk,s- ions set down to the use of the Davy, notably the one at Altham Colliery, near Manchester, some years ago. Mr. Swift submitted that for thirty-eight years the lamps had been used in the mine without any explosion. The tests were valueless, because they had not been made under the same conditions as in the coal mine. They had made experiments with other lamps, but they did not act as well as those in use. Mr. J. 0. Swift addressed the court on behalf of Mr. Arnold.</p>
        <p>Mr. Riley said the miners were in thorough sympathy with the masters on the question of lamps. Mr. France and Mr. George Clarke, mining engineers, having given evidence, Mr. Swift was about to call Mr. Wm. Ropton, when the magistrates stopped the case, and said the did not wish to hear any more evidence. The case would be dismissed.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens on Tuesday afternoon, the casesagains the officials of the S. Helens Collieries Company weret resumed. William Arnold, certificated manager, was summoned under four informations for neglecting to provide sufficient manholes and places of refuge, and also for not keeping then), clear.—Mr. Mulholland again prosecuted on behalf of Mr. H. Hall, Government Inspector of Mines, and Mr. J. 0. Swift defended.</p>
        <p>For the prosecution Mr. Mulholland stated that the information was laid under rule 14 of the Coal Mines' Regulation Act, which provided that in every roadway or airway in the mine where the boxes ran, manholes were to be constructed at intervals of ten yards if there was no room for a person to stand safely between the boxes and the wall, and at intervals of twenty yards where there was such room. There were no less than four planes, or brows, in the mine in question in which that rule was disregarded. Mr. H. S. Hall, Government inspector of mines for the district, stated that he visited the Crop and Deep Pits of the St. Helens Collieries Company on the 18:k April. Be found there were four planes where there were no manholes. He had never found the manholes so badly neglected in all his experience. There were two shunts worked by an engine with haulage ropes, in which there were no manholes at all for the whcle length of 100 yards, and there was a brow fifty yards long without them, and one, the Jubilee Brow, which was eighty yards long, also had none. He was told that only officials used the Jubilee Brow. He did not know that he had said anything about the manholes on his previous visits to the mine, but lie had complained very much of the 'state of the brew.</p>
        <p>Evidence was given by William Arnold, the defend Ant, to the effect that the manholes- were all there as required, and free from debris, when he examined the places on Wednesday. On the Monday morning he found that a fall had occurred, and as there were not enough empty boxes to clear all the rubbish away soon enough, it was put into the manholes. William Bryant, night fireman, admitted that he had the rubbish put into the manholes. He thought he would get em cleared again before Arnold knew about it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Several other witnesses corroborated this statement.</head>
        <p>Several other witnesses corroborated this statement.</p>
        <p>After a long consultation, the magistrates stated that they found the manholes had been improperly filled up, and imposed a line of .22 and costs for that offence. The charge of not providing manholes in the brew used only by officials was dismissed on payment of costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-08-19_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>NEWTON VOLUNTEERS IN CAMP.</head>
        <p>NEWTON VOLUNTEERS IN CAMP.</p>
        <p>The Newton detachment of the 6th V.B.K.L. Regiment paraded at their Orderly room, South- worth-road, on Saturday, the 6th Mt., to join their headquarters at Liverpool, en route to Aldershot for a weeks' encampment. The men fell in at 10 a.m. in marching order, and presented a very smart and soldierly appearance, their equipments and appoint- ments being very clean and well put on. Captai a McCorquodale and Colour-Sergeant Floughton were pre- sent in uniform at parade, but were, from unavoidable and paramount reasons unable to proceed to camp. Be- fore marching off, the Captain addressed the Company expressing regret at not being able to attend the camp this year, and assured the men of the certainty of their having a hard week. He pointed out many disciplirnary matters which shoula be carefully attended to by both non-commissioned officers and men. The Company were then marched to th 3 station, the marching out strength btine 93 of all ranks, a number which has not been pre- viously exceeded at any military camp attended by this detachment. It will be unnecessary to give a detail of the week's parades and maneeuvres, suffice to say that the weather was extremely hot, many degrees hotter than in the North,, and that on the week's average 8 hours per day were spent on parade. Reveille sounding at 4.30 and 5 a.m.. and lights out at 10.15 p.m. The .catering for the men was carried out by Lieut. Dixon, Quatermaster of the Regiment, and was of .an excellent and plentiful character, affording nat a sMgle nstance of complaint during the week. The Newton detachment performed their work in first class style and were many times complimented upon their hardihoad and stamina. On the field day on Wednesday at Fox Hills, where they were under arms from 6 a.m. until 5 p.m.. and covered ground to the extent of from 25 to 27 miles (a day admitted by the oldest veterans to be the most severe that ever took place since the establish- ment of a camp at Aldershot) only two men of the detachment fell out, whilst many or all of the enrps which took part in the manceuvres had a very high percentage of exhausted men. The company was under the command of Lieut. Perry with Lieut. Dixon as supernumerary, Corporal Appleton acted as Colour Ser- geant, the other sergeants being Sergt. McCormick, Acting Sergeants Rimmer, Rose, and Parks, L. Sergt. Hurst being in charge of the bugle band. Before part- ing with the men at Liverpool on the return journey, Lieut. Perry addressed them in thecarriages, expressing himself as zratified to have had the command fora week of such a fine and well disciplined company, and that it would Rive him the greatest pleaatireto report to Cap- tain McCorquodale the steadiness and excellence of conduct of his men. The company cheered from the carriage windows, this popularofficer to the echo. Upon arrival at Newton, and previous to their dismissal. Col. Wood addressed a few words to the men thanking them for their excellent muster, and expressine his apprecia- tion of the conduct and steadiness ot the Newton detachment, which was not only reported to him by his officers, but came under his own observation. Refreshments were provided at the orderly room, pies, beer, and aerated waters being much esteemed after so long and tedious a journey in hot weather. The entrain- ing took place at North Camp Station, at 10 a.m. and the detraining at Newton at 8 p.m.</p>
        <p>4-Cat</p>
        <p>The St. Helens Town Council have memorialised the Local Government Board with regard to the new bylaws which the St. Helens Corporation seek to enforce. The 28th1 of these new enactments forbid the liOlding of public meetings in the public parks of the boreugh; and the Trades Council considers that this is an encroach- ment on the rights of the people. At the meeting of the Executive Committee of the Trades Council on Wednes- day night Mr. James Hart, the president, produced a letter he had received from the Local Government Board stating that the subject should have their consid- eration. The Town Clerk of St. Helens (Mr. W. J. Jeeves), act- ing as hon. secretary to the Royal Marriage Celebration ! Committie, St. Helena, on Thursday issued the list of subscribers to, and halance-sheet of the Royal marri ire treat te the schoolchildren. It shows that £267 10s. 7d. was subscribed. The amount paid out to the 28 schools in the borough was £264 Is. 41., and urldries 15s. ed., leaving an adverse balance of £7 6s. 3d.</p>
